\begin{abstract}

This article addresses the output regulation problem for a class of nonlinear systems using a data-driven approach.
%
An output feedback controller is proposed that integrates a traditional control component with a data-driven learning algorithm based on Gaussian Process ($\mathcal{GP}$) regression to learn the nonlinear internal model. 
%
Specifically, a data-driven technique is employed to directly approximate the unknown internal model steady-state map from observed input-output data online. 
%
Our method does not rely on model-based observers utilized in previous studies, making it robust and suitable for systems with modelling errors and model uncertainties. 
%
Finally, we demonstrate through numerical examples and detailed stability analysis that, under suitable conditions, the closed-loop system remains bounded and converges to a compact set, with the size of this set decreasing as the accuracy of the data-driven model improves over time.

%In this paper, we solve the adaptive output regulation problem of a class of nonlinear systems using a data-driven approach to reconstruct the internal model of an unknown nonlinear continuous map online from some input and output data. The data-driven model is then used to estimate the ideal feed-forward steady-state control inputs obtained by solving the regulator equation instead of implementing it with an extended observer as in previous studies. Secondly, we implement an output feedback stabilizer that does not rely on the complete knowledge of the system but on output measurement of the regulated output, making the proposed approach suitable for systems with modelling errors. Finally, we showed through detailed Lyapunov analysis that under certain conditions the closed-loop system is practically asymptotically stable. 


%In this paper, we address an output regulation problem for a class of nonlinear systems using a data-driven approach. Specifically, we reconstruct the unknown steady-state internal model map online from input and output data. The data-driven model is then employed to approximate the ideal feedforward steady-state control inputs, derived by solving the regulator equation, rather than implementing it through an extended observer as done in previous studies. Additionally, we implement an output feedback stabilizer that relies solely on output measurements of the regulated variable, rather that requiring a complete knowledge of the system dynamics. This makes the proposed approach robust and suitable for systems with modelling errors or uncertainties. Finally, we demonstrate through numerical examples and detailed stability analysis that, under suitable conditions, the closed-loop system remains bounded and converges to a compact set, with the size of this set decreasing as the prediction error of the data-driven model reduces over time.



\end{abstract}

