Here, we report the emergence of superconductivity at approximately 6 K in nominally Eu-capped p-BBO thin films grown via molecular beam epitaxy. X-ray diffraction measurements reveal that oxygen scavenging by the in-vacuo deposited metallic Eu overlayer leads to the reduction of the monoclinic p-BBO layers %and the decomposition of the reduced BBO films 
into tetragonal superconducting BaBi$_3$ and rock-salt BaO. Several measurements, including angle-dependent magnetotransport and current-voltage characteristics, confirm the quasi-two-dimensional nature of the superconductivity. An in-plane upper critical field B$_{c2}$ far exceeding the Pauli paramagnetic limit, raising intriguing possibilities of spin-protected superconducting states, such as Ising superconductivity\cite{zhang2022tailored}.
These findings highlight the potential of utilizing \textit{in situ} reduction of bismuthate heterostructures as a platform for exploring novel quantum states. The strong spin-orbit coupling at Bi sites, %combined with broken time-reversal symmetry, 
may pave the way for the realization of high-temperature topological superconductivity, a long-sought phenomenon in perovskite-based systems\cite{li2015topological, yan2013large}.

