\subsection{Polarization}
\label{sec: polarization} 

In our previous work~\cite{biondi2023dynamics}, we reviewed and classified the most popular polarization metrics used in the related literature. These metrics, given an opinion vector, measure polarization according to different semantics. They can be summarized in the following definition.
%
\begin{definition}\label{def:polarization indices} For an opinion $\bm{x}=(x_i)\in [-1,1]^v$ the following polarization indices are defined:
%
\begin{align}
NDI(\bm{x}) &= \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{E}} w_{ij} (x_i - x_j)^2 \label{eq:ndi}\\
GDI(\bm{x}) &= \sum_{i,j \in \mathcal{V} : i<j}  (x_i - x_j)^2 \label{eq:gdi}\\
P_{1}(\bm{x}) &= \sum_{i \in \mathcal{V}} (x_i - \bar{\bm{x}})^2 = \| \bm{x} - \bar{\bm{x}}\|_2^2 \label{eq:p1}\\
P_{2}(\bm{x}) &= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{V}} x_i ^2 = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \| \bm{x} \|_2^2 \label{eq:p2}\\
P_{3}(\bm{x}) &=  \sum_{i \in \mathcal{V}} x_i ^2 = \| \bm{x} \|_2^2 \label{eq:p3}\\
P_4(\bm{x}) &=  \sum_{i \in \mathcal{V}} |x_i| = \| \bm{x} \|_1 \label{eq:p4}
%\label{eq:polarization_formulas}
\end{align}
\end{definition}
In the context of measuring polarization in social networks, the metrics $NDI$, $GDI$, and $P_1$ quantify polarization based on the degree of disagreement among individuals' opinions. On the other hand, metrics $P_2$, $P_3$, and $P_4$ consider polarization to occur when opinions are significantly distant from a neutral opinion, which is assigned a value of 0. 

A dynamic opinion model is considered \emph{polarizing} with respect to a given metric $\Phi$ if there exists at least one initial opinion vector $\bm{u}$ such that the corresponding final opinion  $\bm{z}$ exhibits greater polarization, i.e., $\Phi(\bm{z}) > \Phi(\bm{u})$. The quantity
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_\Phi(\bm{u}) = \Phi(\bm{z})-\Phi(\bm{u}) \label{eq:polarization}
\end{equation}
is referred to as the \emph{polarization value} and, by definition, is positive if and only if the model is polarizing with respect to the metric $\Phi$.
Our previous research has demonstrated that the FJ model is in fact polarizing, and a formula for obtaining polarizing initial opinion vectors for the metrics $P_2$, $P_3$, and $P_4$ have been identified. For what concerns vectors that maximise $P_2,P_3$ polarization, they are correlated to the spectral properties of matrix $H=(I-\Lambda W)^{-1} (I-\Lambda)$ which transforms the initial opinions $\bm{u}$ into the final opinions $\bm{z}$ as described in Equation~\eqref{eq:fj_solution}. In particular, we identified $\bm{u}_{B_2(1)}$ maximises polarization on the $L_2$-ball of radius 1 and  $\bm{u}_{B_2(t)}= t\cdot \bm{u}_{B_2(1)}$ is its scalar multiple that maximises polarization on  the $L_2$-ball of radius $t$. Furthermore, $\bm{u}_{V_{>1}}$ maximises polarization on the subspace of the specific $H$'s eigenvectors and $\bm{u}_{\max_{P_{2,3}}}$ on the entire domain.  For what concerns vectors that maximise $P_4$ polarization, we identified $\bm{u}_{B_1(1)}$ that maximises polarization on the $L_1$-ball of radius 1 and $\bm{u}_{\max_{P_{4}}}$ on the entire domain. 
Unfortunately, while vectors $\bm{u}_{B_2(1)}$, $\bm{u}_{B_2(t)}$ and $\bm{u}_{B_1(1)}$ can be always found, $\bm{u}_{V_{>1}}$, $\bm{u}_{\max_{P_{2,3}}}$ and $\bm{u}_{\max_{P_{4}}}$ are often impossible to be computed since they are the solutions of NP-hard problems. However,  $\bm{u}_{V_{>1}}$ can often be estimated with a heuristic. Please refer to~\cite{biondi2023dynamics} for further details. 
%These vectors maximize polarization on specific subsets of the opinion domain, as listed in Table~\ref{tab:polarizing_vectors}. 
In Section~\ref{sec:performance_evaluation} we will examine the impact of information cascades when the opinion dynamic process starts with users featuring these polarizing opinions. Notably, we will observe that FJC model induces significantly greater polarization than the FJ for most of the scenarios studied.   


