\section{The Friedkin-Johnsen on Cascades model}
\label{sec:fjc}

In this section, we will discuss the FJC (Friedkin-Johnsen on Cascades) model. The FJ model is an opinion dynamic model that explains how people form their opinions in the offline world, where face-to-face interactions significantly influence the depth of relationships and consequently the shared understanding of opinions. This is the unique mechanism that triggers the \emph{tension resolving process}, which is the brain's reaction to cognitive dissonance. Information cascades, on the other hand, describe how information is spread through online social networks, where interactions are encoded as engagement with each other's posts. They characterise how a single post by a source can go viral and inundate the entire network. 
Despite belonging to two different and separate research areas, opinion dynamics and information cascades are intricately linked. In online social networks, opinions are expressed in a cascade manner, by subsequent exposure to other contents/opinions. It is reasonable to expect that this unique exposure to others' posts influences how we update our opinions. The amplification of virality in social networks can disrupt the balance of real-life discussions in offline networks, thus strengthening the role of central nodes.
%It is worth noting that this simple change in opinion formation unbalances the fairness of a real-life discussion in offline social networks and empowers central nodes. 
This is a crucial insight that has far-reaching implications for understanding opinion formation and information dissemination in online social networks.



\subsection{Revisiting the independent cascade model}\label{sec:model_setting} %Our model conceptualizes the act of posting social content following the independent cascade model~\cite{kempe2003maximizing}. In the independent cascade model, each node can be \emph{active} or \emph{inactive}. An initial set of active nodes $v$ have a single chance to activate their neighbours $w$, and each activation is successful with probability $p_{vw}$. The nodes $w$ that are successfully activated, become active at the next step and try to activate their neighbours as well.  The process continues until no other nodes can be activated. 

The standard independent cascade model needs to be slightly adjusted to integrate it into the FJC framework. To this aim, we identify three new node states, which replace the previous ones: \emph{unaware}, \emph{aware} and \emph{spreader}. A node is unaware when it has not been exposed to the post yet. As soon as it ``sees'' the post from a neighbour, the node becomes \emph{aware} and then it updates its opinion as described in the following sections. With a probability $\theta_{s,i}$ it also reshares the post, and, when it does, the node moves into the \emph{spreader} state. %In the original independent cascade model, the probability $\theta$ is different for each node pair $(s,i)$, where $s$ is the spreader of the post and $i$ is the node that receives the information from~$s$. However, here we make the simplifying assumption (A1) that this probability $\theta$ does not depend on the relation $(s,i)$ and is equal for all node pairs. We leave it to future work to relax this hypothesis. %However, it is worth noting that making the parameter $\theta$ to be different among nodes, would strengthen the difference with the classical FJ model because it would unbalance the edges of the network. 
An illustration of the possible transition of states for a single node is provided in Figure~\ref{fig:actions_states}. We assume (A1) that the content being shared does not express an opinion on its own, but rather the opinion is conveyed only by the person sharing it along with a comment. This assumption allows us to avoid considering the post as an external factor that can modify opinions and instead to focus on studying how the simple cascade dynamic affects opinion formation in comparison to the FJ scenario.


