A post may become obsolete and lose its relevance over time. When this occurs, nodes cease to propagate it, even if some nodes have not been reached. We capture this effect by defining a threshold parameter $L$, known as the \emph{interest threshold}. Specifically, $L$ represents the maximum hop distance that a post can travel from its source to its destinations. Thus, once a post has reached $L$ hops, its propagation ceases. It is important to note that if this threshold is greater than or equal to the diameter of the network (defined as the longest shortest path between any two nodes in the graph $\mathcal{G}$), all posts will always propagate throughout the entire network, rendering the parameter $L$ ineffective in influencing the resulting cascade.

In our model, an individual identified as the source disseminates a piece of news to the online social network. In the rest of this paper, we streamline our discussion by initially constructing the model under the assumption that each post originates from a single seed node. However, it is worth noting that the model's flexibility allows for straightforward extension to accommodate multiple sources, akin to the original information cascade model. In fact, including multiple sources simply affects the first level of the information cascade tree, while the rest remains unchanged. 
We assume (A2) that once the post is shared the first time by the seed node $r$, it spreads outwards like a tree, from the original source to the first, second, third, and subsequent in-neighbours, which we will refer to as the nodes in the first, second, third, ... \emph{layer}. This assumption means that the opinions of parent nodes represent the first exposure a node has in its news feed, thereby solidifying their influence. Following this initial encounter, we maintain that nodes typically become disinterested in posts shared by others. Thus, we will refer to the $l$-th in-neighbourhood as the $l$-th \emph{layer} or \emph{layer} $l$ and we will indicate it with $\mathcal{O}_l^{(r)}$. Keeping in mind that $L$ is the interest threshold, the entire tree that comprehends all nodes in the \emph{aware} state of the cascade is denoted as the sequence $\mathcal{O}^{(r)}=\left(\mathcal{O}_l^{(r)}\right)_{l=0}^{L}$ of all layers. %As the content spreads outwards, nodes that receive the content will then reshare it with their neighbours in the following layer, according to a certain probability. In this work we make the assumption that this probability $\theta$ is equal for all nodes in the social network, leaving as a future work to relax this hypothesis. However, it is worth noting that making the parameter $\theta$ to be different among nodes, would strengthen the difference with the classical FJ model because it would unbalance even more the edges of the network. Once a node has seen the post, it becomes immune to further re-sharing of the same post. 

%The only difference lies in the order in which they collect these opinions, which depends on their position in the graph. In fact, from $r$ the post arrives at its followers, who get acquainted with its opinion and update its opinion according to it (please refer to Section~\ref{sec:fj} for the details). Then, nodes that have received the content can decide to re-share the post with their comment that represents their opinion or not; the parameter $\theta\in [0,1]$, determines this probability and we assume that it is equal for all the nodes in the social network, leaving as future work to relax this assumption and make it dependent on the opinions of nodes. Please note that making the parameter $\theta$ to be different among nodes, would strengthen the difference with the classical FJ model because it would unbalance even more the edges of the network. Once the nodes have seen the post, we assume that they become immune to the further re-sharing of the same post. Thus, the original post then propagates over the network in a tree-like manner, where nodes that receive the content reshare (according to $\theta$) it to the followers that have not yet received it (see Figure~\ref{fig:example}).


A  post $p$ generated at node $r$ spreads into the network over the tree $\mathcal{O}^{(r)}$. However, not all nodes in this tree receive the content, because there is a probability $\theta$ that each node will not reshare the post. The subtree of  $\mathcal{O}^{(r)}$ that includes only nodes that have received the post is denoted as $\mathcal{T}^{(r,\theta)} = (\mathcal{T}^{(r,\theta)}_{l} )_{l=0,\dots,L}$, and it holds that $\mathcal{T}^{(r,\theta)}_{l}\subseteq \mathcal{O}^{(r)}_{l}$ for each $l$. $\mathcal{T}^{(r,\theta)}$ is  a stochastic realization of the tree $\mathcal{O}^{(r)}$, as indicated by the superscript~$\theta$. In Figure~\ref{fig:example}, there is a simple example of a network and a corresponding propagation tree. 
