\subsection{The cascade-driven Friedkin-Johnsen}
\label{sec:fjc} 

We will now discuss how to integrate the independent cascade model described in Section~\ref{sec:model_setting} with the FJ model from Section~\ref{sec:fj}, resulting in a FJ variant (FJC) where opinions are influenced by the sharing of posts. In this model, opinions are influenced by the sharing of posts among nodes. The FJC model operates iteratively, following specific states and transitions for each node (see Figure~\ref{fig:actions_states}). At the start of each cascade, every node, except for the designated seed node $r$, begins in the unaware state. During each iteration, nodes $i$ that are exposed to a post from another node $s$ undergo two consecutive actions. %For every post at each time stamp, two operations occur. At first, the nodes that have just seen the content (because it has been reshared by some of their predecessors at the step before) update their opinions. 
First, these exposed nodes transition to the "aware" state and update their opinions regarding the post. Following this, with a probability $\theta_{s,i}$, these nodes enter the spreader state, sharing the post again if they belong to a layer $l$ that is smaller than the maximum layer $L$. If a node does not meet this condition, the propagation of the post stops, and a new cascade is initiated.
We will examine the spreading process of $N$ posts, denoted with $p_1,\dots,p_N$. We make an assumption (A3) that posts propagate one at a time, meaning that simultaneous spread of two posts is not possible. While this assumption is stringent, it aids in designing the model .%deriving closed forms. 
In future work, we will explore ways to relax this assumption and understand its impact on the model. However, in Section~\ref{sec:eval_validation}, we demonstrate that the model is robust in real-world settings.
%In this section, we will present an overview of the FJC model, integrating the modeling approaches outlined in Sections and~\ref{sec:fj}. 

Now, we want to discuss the details of the opinion updating operation. Please observe that, since we want to update opinion layer by layer, we need an asynchronous version of the FJ, similar to that of Equation~\eqref{eq:fj_asyn_update_i}.  

We focus on the spreading process of the $n$-th post $p_n$ generated at node $r_n$ which involves nodes in the cascade $\mathcal{C}^{(r_n)}$. As the cascade starts, the opinions of nodes are equal to $\bm{u}=\bm{x}(0)$ if $n=1$, i.e. if we are considering the spreading of the first post. Otherwise, they are equal to the final opinion of nodes as the previous post $p_{n-1}$ completed spreading. 
Every time a node $j$ in the cascade reshares the post $p_n$ %(and this happens with probability $\theta$) 
at a time $t$, it conveys its current opinion $x_j(t)$ together with the reposting.  A node $i$ in layer $l$ can receive the post (which, as explained above, is a proxy for a node's opinion) from one or more predecessors $j$. This triggers the update of node $i$'s opinion, which is carried out according to the following update rule:
% (receiving then the opinion $x_j(t)$ together with the repost), thus the opinion update should factor in their total influence on $i$ by averaging across all the single influences. 
% In particular, if the update occurs at time $t_{i}$, the opinion update is equal to:
\begin{align}
    x_{i}&(t)= \nonumber\\
    &=\frac{1}{|\varphi(i)|} \Bigg\{\sum_{j\in\varphi(i)} h_{i} \left[(1-\gamma_{ij})x_{i}(t-1)+ \right.\nonumber\\
    &\qquad\left.+\gamma_{ij}x_{j}(t-1)\right]+(1-h_{i})u_{i}\Bigg\},%\\
    %&=\frac{h_{i}}{|\varphi(i)|} \Bigg\{\sum_{j\in\varphi(i)} \left[(1-\gamma_{i j})x_{i}(t_{i}-1)\right.\nonumber\\
    %&\qquad\left.+\gamma_{ij}x_{j}(t_{i}-1)\right]\Bigg\} +(1-h_{i})u_{i},
    \label{eq:fjc_update}
\end{align}
%
where $h_i$ and $\gamma_i$ are defined as in Equations 4 and 5, and $\varphi(i)$ is defined in the Equation~\eqref{eq:predecessors}.
The above update rule factors in the average total influence on node~$i$ by all its in-neighbours that have reshared the post, hence shared their opinion with $i$. Note that, differently from the asynchrounous FJ update in Equation~\eqref{eq:fj_asyn_update_i}, node $i$ assesses the opinions of its predecessors all at once, not in a pairwise fashion. %\hl{This is consistent with how users process information online, i.e., by scrolling their timelines at once.} 



