\subsection{FJC with real cascades}
\label{sec:eval_validation}

\subsubsection{Dataset description and experimental setting}

We validate the model using a real-world dataset from Bhowmick~\etaltwo~\cite{bhowmick2019temporal}, which contains Twitter (now X) data related to the 2015 Nepal earthquake. This dataset is well-suited for our analysis as it includes both the follower network and tweet/retweet histories. Unlike the synthetic networks in Sec.~\ref{sec:eval_synth_casc}, this is a directed graph, with multiple connected components. Since FJ-based models operate within a connected structure, we extract the largest strongly connected component (LSCC). Some cascades span disconnected components, suggesting hidden links, so we restrict our analysis to cascades occurring entirely within the LSCC. The resulting graph contains 220,974 nodes, 16,772, 693 edges, and 32,313 cascades.
%
%As in the synthetic experiments, we derive the influence matrix by row-normalizing the adjacency matrix and test three susceptibility settings: directly proportional, inversely proportional to PageRank centrality, and uniform ($\lambda = 0.6$). 
Initial opinions are set using the polarizing vectors described in Section~\ref{sec: polarization}. However, due to the large size of the network, even optimized methods could not compute these vectors over the full LSCC. We therefore define two subgraphs:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Nepal cut degree}: includes only nodes with degree in the range $[150, 400]$ (where the node degree spans from 2 to 53718 in the original LSCC), balancing network activity and computational feasibility. We extract the LSCC and retain only the cascades involving its nodes.
%
\item \textbf{Nepal top cascades}: constructed from nodes involved in the 500 longest cascades using a heuristic for the directed Steiner Tree problem~\cite{watel2016practical} to ensure that all the nodes involved in these cascades are in the subgraph.
%
\end{itemize}
%
Table~\ref{tab:Nepal_datasets} summarizes the two subgraphs. The \textit{Nepal cut degree} graph has more nodes but fewer edges, while \textit{Nepal top cascades} is denser and includes more cascades (hence making it more likely that assumption (A3) is violated).

