\subsubsection{Opinion evolution of real opinion dynamics vs FJC and FJ} Due to the large size of the networks, we do not visualize the polarizing vectors as in Figure~\ref{fig:results}, since the plots would be unreadable. Instead, our main objective with these datasets is to evaluate the robustness of the FJC model against the \emph{real} opinion dynamics. We do so by comparing the distributions of opinion shifts—i.e., the differences between final and initial opinions—for the real evolution (labeled \textit{Real}) and the predictions of FJC and FJ. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:opinion_shift}, FJC closely matches the real dynamics in the \emph{Nepal cut degree} dataset (the two curves are nearly indistinguishable) and shows reasonable agreement in the \emph{Nepal top cascades} dataset. This is expected, as longer cascades are more likely to violate assumption (A3), reducing FJC’s accuracy. Nevertheless, FJC remains surprisingly robust even in this more challenging scenario.

