\subsubsection{Polarization} 
Figure~\ref{fig:nepal_pol} shows polarization outcomes across different settings. Both cascade-based dynamics—FJC and \emph{Real}—exhibit very low or even negative polarization, in sharp contrast to the FJ model. This suggests that, in this case, cascades tend to reduce polarization, seemingly contradicting the findings from synthetic networks in Section~\ref{sec:eval_synth_casc}. However, this can be explained by the fact that, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:theta}, most nodes have $\theta$ values close to zero, indicating minimal reposting activity. These results are therefore consistent with our earlier observation: when $\theta$ is small, opinion dynamics are largely inactive, preventing polarization from developing.
