As part of the argument, we will move from one scale to another, partitioning parallelepipeds into smaller ones. The following definition will be useful for this purpose:
\begin{Definition}
Let $B,C$ be parallelepipeds in $\R^3$.  We define $\mathcal{A}(B,C)$ as a collection of disjoint translates of $C$ covering $B$, that is, a subset $\{C+a:\; a\in\R^3\}$, satisfying:
$$\bigcup_{C'\in \mathcal{A}(B,C)} C'\cap B=B,$$
 $C_1\cap C_2=\emptyset$ for all distinct $C_1,C_2\in \mathcal{A}(B,C)$, and there exists $C'\in \mathcal{A}(B,C)$ whose center coincides with the center of $B$.
\end{Definition}


