\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Chord diagrams, triangular diagrams, equilevel and trinitary algebras}

Chord diagrams are collections of pairs of distinct points of $S^1$. They naturally appear in  knot theory because they participate in the enumeration of the strata of the {\em discriminant set} of maps $S^1 \to \R^3$, i.e., the maps which are not the smooth embeddings. Each singular knot with $k$ simple self-intersections is characterized by $k$ chords, namely, by the preimages of all self-intersection points. Each $k$-chord diagram defines a subalgebra of codimension $k$ in $C^\infty(S^1, \R)$ that consists of all maps $f$ such that $f(x_i) = f(\tilde x_i)$ for the points $(x_i, \tilde x_i)$ of each chord. More complicated points of the discriminant (i.e., maps with multiple self-intersection points, self-tangencies, zeros of the derivative, etc) are related to subalgebras that can be approximated by algebras defined by chord diagrams. These subalgebras are studied in \cite{EA}.

Knot invariants {\em of finite type} (see \cite{bn}, \cite{bl}, \cite{CDM}, \cite{ks}) can be described in the terms of formal linear combinations of chord diagrams that satisfy certain conditions. The first condition requires that no chord diagram participating in such a combination can have chords whose points $(x_i, \tilde x_i)$ are not separated in $S^1 $ by the points of other chords of the same chord diagram. The simplest chord diagram satisfying this condition is the cross \ {\unitlength=1.60mm \special{em:linewidth 0.4pt}
\linethickness{1pt}
\begin{picture}(3.33,5.2)
\put(2.6,-1){\line(0,1){5.2}}
\put(0,1.67){\line(1,0){5.2}} 
\thicklines
\put(2.6,1.67){\circle{5.2}} 
\end{picture}
} \ . This chord diagram defines a knot invariant, called the Casson invariant: it is equal to the coefficient of the Alexander polynomial at the monomial $t^2$. 

The theory of {\em doodles} is parallel to knot theory: it classifies and investigates 
 the maps $f: S^1 \to \R^2$ having no triple (or more complicated)
points. Unlike the generic immersions studied in \cite{A}, a doodle $f$ can have a standard self-tangency or simple cusp point because the maps with these singularities are not limits of maps with triple points. However, a doodle cannot have a point $x \in S^1$ at which $f'(x)=f''(x)=0$, nor can it have two points $x$ and $y$ such that $f(x)=f(y)$ and $f'(x)=0$. 
(Notice that a slightly different class of plane curves, also called doodles, was considered in \cite{FT}.) 

The simplest invariant of doodles, which is fairly analogous to the Casson invariant, was discovered by A.~Merkov, see \cite{Merx} and \cite{MD}. 

The natural analog of chord diagrams in this theory is {\em triangular diagrams}, i.e. collections of triples of distinct points in $S^1$, that are the preimages of the triple points in $f(S^1)$, see \cite{V-11}. The interpretation of the Merkov's invariant in the terms of triangular diagrams was found in \cite{MD}. Analogously to the case of knots, it is expressed by the simplest triangular diagram \ \
