having no triangles some two points of which are not separated by the points of other triangles. Continuing the analogy with knot theory, we begin studying of {\em trinitary subalgebras} in $C^\infty(S^1, \R)$, i.e., the subalgebras that are the limit positions of the algebras of codimension $2k$ defined by $k$ independent triangles. For example, such an algebra can be defined by two points $a, b \in S^1$ and one real parameter $\alpha$, and consist of all functions $f: S^1 \to \R$ such that 
$$f(a)=f(b), \ \ f'(a)=f''(a), \ \ f'''(a) = \alpha f'(b). $$
We define the structure of a CW-complex on the space of all trinitary subalgebras of codimension four (which has the natural structure of a six-dimensional semialgebraic variety), and calculate the mod 2 incidence coefficients of its cells. In the next version of this preprint, I plan to compute the cohomology rings of this variety and the Stiefel--Whitney classes of the canonical vector bundle on it.




\section{Formal definition and basic properties of trinitary algebras}
\subsection{Main definition}
\label{maindef}

Let $M$ be a smooth algebraic manifold. 

A {\em $k$-triangular diagram} in the manifold $M$ is any collection of triples 
of pairwise distinct points $x_i, \tilde x_i, \hat x_i$, $i=1, \dots, k$, such that the subalgebra in $C^\infty(M, \R)$ defined by $2k$ conditions $f(x_i)=f(\tilde x_i) = f(\hat x_i)$ has codimension exactly $2k$. We denote by $TD_k(M)$ both the space of all $k$-triangular diagrams and the space of corresponding subalgebras of codimension $2k$.

Consider the {\em ordered configuration space} $F(M, 3k) \subset M^{2k},$ i.e., the set of all sequences $(x_1, \tilde x_1, \hat x_1; x_2, \tilde x_2, \hat x_2; $ $\dots; x_k, \tilde x_k, \hat x_3)$ of $3k$ distinct points of $M$. Each point of this space defines a subalgebra of codimension $2k$ in $C^\infty(M, {\mathbb R})$ consisting of functions that satisfy all $2k$ equations $f(x_i)=f(\tilde x_i)=f(\hat x_i)$, $i=1, \dots, k$.

Consider an arbitrary germ of an algebraic parametric curve $\nu: [0,\varepsilon) \to M^{3k}$ such that all points $\nu(t),$ $t \in (0, \varepsilon),$ belong to the subspace $F(M, 3k)$. For any $t \in (0,\varepsilon)$, denote by $\F_t$ the subalgebra defined by the configuration $\nu(t)$. 

Define $\F_0$ as the space of all functions $f \in C^\infty(M, \R)$ such that there exists a continuous (in $C^{3k}$ topology) family of functions $f_t$, $t \in [0,\varepsilon)$, such that $f=f_0$ and $f_t \in \F_t$ for $t \in (0, \varepsilon)$.

 Clearly, the space $\F_0$ is also a subalgebra in $C^\infty(M, {\mathbb R}).$ 

By definition, {\em trinitary algebras} of class $\overline{TD}_k(M)$ are arbitrary subalgebras $\F_0 \subset C^\infty(M, {\mathbb R})$ that can be defined by algebraic curves $\nu$ in this way. 

\begin{remark} \rm
In general, the limit subalgebra $\F_0$ does not uniquely define the point $\nu(0) \in M^{3k}$, even up to the permutations of the elements $x_i$, $ \tilde x_j,$ and $\hat x_m$. For example, the subalgebra of class $\overline{TD}_2(S^1)$ consisting of all functions that satisfy the conditions $f(A)=f(B)=f(C)=f(D)=f(E)$ for five distinct points $A, B, C, D, E \in S^1$ can be defined by this construction with the point $\nu(0) \in (S^1)^4$ equal to  the point $(A, B, C; A, D, E)$ or $(A, B, C; B, D, E)$, or $(A, D, E; B, C, E)$, etc. 
\end{remark}

 However, the set of all points of $M$ that are involved in the sequence $\nu(0)$ for any such construction of a trinitary subalgebra is the same for all its constructions; this set is called the {\em support} of the subalgebra. Clearly, the support of a trinitary algebra of codimension $2k$ consists of at most $3k$ points. 

\begin{proposition}
\label{trui}
Suppose that a subalgebra ${\mathcal F} \subset C^\infty(M, {\mathbb R})$ of class $\overline{TD}_k(M)$ has the above construction, and its support consists of the points $A_1, \dots, A_r$, where each point $A_i$ participates in the sequence $\nu(0)$ with some multiplicity $m_i$, \ $m_1 + \dots + m_r =3k$. Then, the subalgebra ${\mathcal F}$ contains 

a$)$ all constant functions,

b$)$ the ideal 
\begin{equation} \M_{A_1}^{m_1} \cap \dots \cap \M_{A_r}^{m_r}
\label{ide}
\end{equation} in $C^\infty(M, {\mathbb R})$ consisting of all functions vanishing at each point $A_i$ with multiplicity $m_i$ $($i.e., together with all partial derivatives up to degree $m_i-1)$.
\end{proposition}

\noindent
{\it Proof} repeats the proof of Proposition 21 in \cite{EA}.



\begin{example}
\rm $TD_1(M)$ is the configuration space $B(M, 3)$. The space $\overline{TD}_1(S^1)$ is naturally homeomorphic to the space $Sym^3(S^1) \equiv (S^1)^3/S(3)$.
Both spaces $TD_1(S^1)$ and $\overline{TD}_1(S^1)$ are thus homotopy equivalent to $S^1$.
\end{example} 

\subsection{Fighting the infinite-dimensional issues}
\label{findim}

\begin{definition} \rm
A vector subspace $ \F \subset C^\infty(M, \R)$ is {\em of codimension $q$} if, for any vector subspace ${\mathcal L}^n$ of finite dimension $n \geq q$, the intersection $\F \cap {\mathcal L}^n$ is at least $(n-q)$-dimensional, and there exist $q$-dimensional vector subspaces ${\mathcal L}^q$ intersecting $\F$ at the origin only. The subspace ${\mathcal L}^n,$ $ n \geq q$, is {\it transversal} to a subspace of codimension $q$ if the dimension of their intersection is exactly $n-q$. 
\end{definition}

It is easy to see that if the finite-dimensional subspace ${\mathcal L}$ is transversal to a subspace ${\mathcal F}$, then ${\mathcal L}$ is also transversal to any larger subspace ${\mathcal F}' \supset {\mathcal F}$. 

Let ${\mathcal L}$ be an arbitrary finite-dimensional vector subspace of $C^\infty(M, {\mathbb R})$ such that 
%\begin{center}
\begin{equation} 
\label{condition}
\mbox{
\fbox{
\parbox{13cm}{
for any $3k$ different points of $M$ and any collection of $(3k-1)$-jets of functions $M \to {\mathbb R}$ at these points, there exists a function $f \in {\mathcal L}$ whose $(3k-1)$-jets at these $3k$ points coincide with the given ones.}
}
}
\end{equation}
%\end{center}

\begin{lemma}
\label{le24}
1. Every trinitary algebra defined as previously by an algebraic curve $\nu: [0, \varepsilon) \to M^{3k}$ is a subspace of codimension $2k$ in $C^\infty(M, \R)$.

2. Each such trinitary algebra intersects the subspace ${\mathcal L}$ along a subspace of codimension $2k$ in ${\mathcal L}$.

3. The intersections of different trinitary algebras with the subspace ${\mathcal L}$ are different subspaces of codimension $2k$ in ${\mathcal L}$.
\end{lemma}

\noindent
{\it Proof}   repeats the proof of Lemma 22 in \cite{EA}.
\medskip

Thus, the entire space $\overline{TD}_k(M)$ can be identified with a semialgebraic subvariety of the finite-dimensional Grassmann manifold $G_{\dim {\mathcal L}-2k}({\mathcal L})$: each subalgebra is associated with its intersection with the space ${\mathcal L}$. If $M$ is compact then this subvariety is also compact. 
The topological structure of the space $\overline{TD}_k(M)$ induced by this identification from the topology of the Grassmann manifold does not depend on the choice of the space ${\mathcal L}$ satisfying the condition (\ref{condition}). Indeed, each larger space ${\mathcal L}' \supset {\mathcal L}$ defines the same topology of $\overline{TD}_k(M)$, and any two finite-dimensional subspaces are subspaces of a common larger finite-dimensional subspace.



\section{Cell structure of variety $\overline{TD}_2(S^1)$}

\subsection{} We divide the trinitary subalgebras of $C^\infty(S^1, {\mathbb R})$ into two types: those algebras
whose supports do not contain or do contain the distinguished point $\bullet = \{0, 2\pi\}\in S^1 \equiv [0,2\pi]/(0 \cup 2\pi)$. We will describe 122 families of such algebras of codimension four of either of these two types.

 These families are all diffeomorphic to open balls of certain dimensions; they define the structures of $CW$-complexes on the spaces $\overline{TD}_2(S^1)$. Algebras that can be obtained from each other by an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism of $S^1$  taking the distinguished point to itself belong to the same cell. Thus, the points of their supports are among the parameters of these cells. Moreover, uniform (in a sense) algebras with the same support also lie in the same cell, and their moduli make the remaining part of the parameters of the cells.

Each cell of the first type (consisting of algebras, whose supports do not contain the point $\bullet$) is related to a cell of the second type: the elements of the second cell are the algebras obtained from the former algebras by shifting the argument space $S^1$ along its orientation until the support points with the largest value of the coordinate come to the distinguished point $\bullet$. The dimension of the resulting cell is obviously one less than that of the initial cell. The notation of this cell is obtained from the notation of the former one by 

\subsection{About the notation}
\label{onnot}

In the figures illustrating the algebras of codimension 4, the tripods with feet at the points $a,$ $b$, and $c$, of the source circle $S^1$ denote the condition $f(a)=f(b)=f(c)$.  An asterisk $\ast$ at point $a$ denotes the condition $f'(a)=0$, and a double asterisk denotes the condition $f'(a)=f''(a)=0$. A circled asterisk $\circledast$ at point $a$ denotes the family of pairs of conditions 
$$ f'(a)=0, \quad f'''(a) = \alpha f''(a)$$
depending on a parameter $\alpha \in {\mathbb R}$ . 

An ark connecting points $a$ and $b$, which are also the legs of the same tripod
(and therefore satisfy the condition  $f(a)=f(b)$)
denotes a family of additional conditions $f'(a)=\alpha f'(b)$, depending on a real parameter $\alpha \neq 0$. This notation is always accompanied by a plus or minus sign that indicates the sign of $\alpha$ for all algebras forming the cell.  The notations of more complicated conditions is explained later.

Each picture in Theorem~\ref{mainprop} represents a family of algebras parameterized by non-general points of the circle participating in the picture that are not the distinguished point $\bullet$, and also by the moduli of the uniform algebras with the same support.

\begin{theorem} 
\label{mainprop}
The variety $\overline{TD}_2(S^1)$ has the structure of a CW-complex with 122 cells of the first type $($i.e., those with support not containing the distinguished point $\bullet)$ and 122 cells of the second type. 
Namely, it has 
\begin{itemize}
\item 10 cells $A_{i j}$ of dimension 6, see Fig.~\ref{A};
\item 
37 cells of dimension 5, including one  cell  $B$ $($see Fig.~\ref{BE}$)$, 20 cells $C_{i j}$ $($see Fig.~\ref{C}$)$, 12 cells $D_{i j}^\pm$ $($see Fig.~\ref{D}$)$, and four cells $E_+$, $E_1,$ $E_2$, and $E_3$, see Fig.~\ref{BE};
\item 
45 cells of dimension 4, including four cells $F_i$ $($see Fig.~\ref{F}$)$, 12 cells $G_{i j k l}$ $($see Fig.~\ref{G}$)$, four cells $H_i$ $($see Fig.~\ref{H}$)$, three cells $I_i$ $($see Fig.~\ref{I}$)$, 12 cells $J^{\pm}_{i j l}$ $($see Fig.~\ref{J}$)$, six cells $K_i^{\pm}$ $($see Fig.~\ref{K}$)$, and four cells $W_i^{\pm}$ $($see Fig.~\ref{W}$)$;
\item
23 cells of dimension 3, including six cells $L_{i j l}$ $($see Fig.~\ref{L}$)$, three cells $M_i$ $($see Fig.~\ref{MN}$)$, three cells $N_i$ $($see Fig.~\ref{MN}$)$, two cells $P_i$, two cells $S_i$, two cells $X^{\pm}$ $($see Fig.~\ref{PSX}$)$, four cells $V_i^{\pm}$, and the cell $\Omega$ $($see Fig.~\ref{VOmega}$)$;
\item
six cells of dimension 2, including two cells $Y_i$, two cells $U_i$, one cell $Z$, and one cell $\Theta$ $($see Fig.~\ref{Theta}$)$;
\item
and one cell $\nabla$ of dimension 1, see Fig.~\ref{nabla}.
\end{itemize}
Also it contains the same number of corresponding cells of dimensions 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, and 0, respectively.
\end{theorem}

The definition of algebras of classes $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $F$, $G$, $H$, $I$, $J$, $K$, $L$,  $M$, $N$, $P$, $Y$, $U$, $Z$, $\nabla$ is clear from the pictures. 

Algebras of class $E$ depend on the parameter $(\alpha: \beta: \gamma) \in \RP^2$,
$\alpha \beta \gamma \neq 0$. Namely, the algebra $E((\alpha:\beta:\gamma),  a, b, c)$
consists of functions $f$ such that $f(a)=f(b)=f(c)$ and 
$(f'(a): f'(b): f'(c)) = (\alpha:\beta:\gamma)$. The cell
$E_+$ is formed by all such algebras with arbitrary $a<b<c \in (0,2\pi)$ and the points $(\alpha: \beta:\gamma)$ such that the signs of $\alpha, \beta,$ and $ \gamma$ are the same. The cell $E_1$ (respectively,  $E_2$, $E_3$) consists of such algebras with the sign of $\alpha$ (respectively, $\beta, \gamma$) different from the signs of two other coefficients.

Algebras of class $W$ depend on the parameter $(\alpha: \beta: \gamma )  \in \R P^2$, $\beta \gamma \neq 0$. Such an algebra $W((\alpha: \beta: \gamma); a, b)$ consists of all functions $f$ such that 
$$f(a)=f(b), \quad f'(a)=0, \quad ( f'''(a) :   f''(a) : f'(b)) = (\alpha : \beta : \gamma). $$ 
These algebras with $a<b$ comprise the cells 
$W_1^{\pm}$ where the sign $\pm$ is the sign of $\beta/\gamma$;
analogously the algebras with $a>b$ form the cells  $W_2^{\pm}. $ 

The algebras of class $S$ depend on two points $a$, $b$, and the real parameter $\alpha$. The algebra $S(\alpha; a, b)$ consists of functions $f$ such that
$$f(a)=f(b) , \quad f'(a)=f''(a)=0, \quad f^{\tiny IV}(a)=\alpha f'''(a).$$
These algebras belong to the cell $S_1$ if $a<b$ and to the cell $S_2$ if $a>b$.

The algebras of class $X$ depend  on the parameter $\alpha \neq 0$: the algebra $X(\alpha; a, b)$ consists of functions $f$ satisfying the conditions 
$$f(a)=f(b), \quad f'(a)=0, \quad f'(b)=0, \quad f''(a) = \alpha f''(b).$$ 
These algebras fill in two cells $X^{\pm}$ depending on the sign of $\alpha$.

The algebras of class $V$ also depend on two points of the circle and one real parameter $\alpha \neq 0$. The algebra $V(\alpha; a, b)$ consists of functions $f$ such that $$f(a)=f(b), \quad f'(a)=f''(a)=0, \quad f'''(a) = \alpha f'(b).$$ These algebras fill in four cells characterized by the sign of $\alpha$ and by the order of point $a, b$ in the regular part $(0, 2\pi)$ of the source circle.

The algebras of class $\Omega$ depend on two parameters $\alpha$ and $\beta$. 
The algebra $\Omega (\alpha, \beta; a)$ consists of functions satisfying the conditions 
$$f'(a)=f''(a)=0 \quad f^{\tiny IV}(a) = \alpha f'''(a) \quad f^{\tiny V}(a) = \beta f'''(a).$$

The algebras of class $\Theta$ depends on one parameter $\alpha$. The algebra $\Theta(\alpha; a)$ consists of functions $f$ satisfying the conditions $$f'(a)=f''(a)=f'''(a)= 0, \quad f^{\tiny V}(a) = \alpha f^{\tiny IV}(a).$$ These algebras constitute one 2-dimensional cell.

