Indeed, anti-correlation effects are expected to modify it to a stretched exponential of the form $\ln \langle n_\mathrm{A}(t) \rangle \sim - (D t)^{d/2}$ in $d < 2$ (with additional logarithmic corrections at $d=d_c = 2$) reflecting the emergence of depletion zones.\cite{Tauber2014}
We also expect pronounced effects due to spatial segregation of the reactive agents into separate inert clusters.

We note that $C = 0$ in the mean-field solution (\ref{eq:two_species_annihilation_mf_solution}) is a singular point in parameter space. 
Hence, we must carefully take the limit $C \to 0$ to obtain
\begin{equation}
    \langle n_\mathrm{A}(t) \rangle = \frac{\langle n_\mathrm{A}(0) \rangle}{1 +\langle n_\mathrm{A}(0) \rangle \lambda_0 t} \ ,
\label{eq:mfpairan}    
\end{equation}
which is just the mean-field rate  solution for the single-species binary annihilation reaction.
The limits $t \to \infty$ and $C \to 0$ do not commute.

