    \includegraphics{Figures/Figure7.pdf}
    \caption{(a) A capillary disk of radius $R=0.3$ cm moves erratically on the surface of a fluid bath vibrating at $f=140$ Hz, forced by unsteady Faraday waves and the concomitant surface flows. (b) The root-mean-square-displacement of the disk as a function of lag time shows a transition from ballistic to diffusive motion, as in prior work \cite{welch2014ballistic,xia2019tunable}, with a crossover time occurring at $\Delta t\approx 0.5$ s. Inset: sample trajectory of the erratically driven capillary disk. (c-d) Capillary disks with embedded magnets exhibit pattern formation due to competing attractive (capillary) and repulsive (magnetic) interactions \cite{hooshanginejad2024interactions}. (c) Repulsive lattice, (d) cluster, and (e) stripe patterns are observed at different packing fractions in square confinement.}
