\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

\smallskip

Optimization problems with stochastic components (stochastic programs) occur frequently in the finance and risk management literature. The original form of the problem, introduced in \citet{dantzig}, takes the form
\begin{equation} \label{eq:stochastic_program}
\inf_{\boldsymbol{a}\in\mathcal{A}}\E[\ell(\boldsymbol{a},\X)],
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{A}$ is a set of feasible actions, $\ell$ is a loss function, $\X$ is a random vector, and the expectation is taken with respect to (wrt) $\X$. One major restriction of stochastic programs is the assumption that the distribution of $\X$ is known. However, in many practical applications, the distribution is only partially known or needs to be estimated. Difficulties from estimating or partial knowledge of the underlying distribution are well studied in finance (\citet{michaud}), decision theory (\citet{smith}), and risk management (\citet{cont, embrechts, pesenti4}). To address this restriction, \citet{soyster} proposed a distributionally robust version of \Cref{eq:stochastic_program}, distributionally robust optimization (DRO). DRO problems take the form 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:DRO}
\inf_{\boldsymbol{a}\in\mathcal{A}}\; \sup_{\X\in\mathcal{U}} \; \E[\ell(\boldsymbol{a},\X)]\,,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{U}$ is a set of plausible alternative distributions --- so--called uncertainty sets. Over the past two decades, DRO problems have become extremely popular in operations research and economics; indicatively see \citet{rahimian} and \citet{hansen}. 

Early research in DRO focuses on linear programs with ellipsoidal uncertainty sets (\citet{ben-tal, ben-tal2}). We work, among others, with uncertainty sets defined by a Wasserstein distance constraint. The Wasserstein distance is a well studied metric that originated from the field of optimal transport (\citet{villani}). The metric is now widely used outside of optimal transport, with important applications in mathematics, probability, and statistical theory (\citet{panaretos, dobrushin, munk}). More recent works discussing applications of the Wasserstein distance to DRO problems include \citet{gao}, who focus exclusively on the Wasserstein distance, \citet{blanchet}, who consider optimal transport distances associated with lower semi-continuous cost functions, and \citet{Pesenti2023SIM} who study portfolio allocation. In our work, we consider both the Wasserstein distance and an asymmetric generalization of the Wasserstein distance known as the Bregman-Wasserstein (BW) divergence. The BW divergence was first introduced by \citet{carlier}, its geometry is discussed in \citet{rankin}, and BW uncertainty sets are studied in \citet{guo,pesenti}, and \citet{pesenti3}. 

In this work, we focus on the inner optimization problem in \eqref{eq:DRO} and assume that the risk factor $\X$ is multivariate. Specifically, we consider uncertainty sets defined using multivariate Wasserstein distance and multivariate BW divergence constraints. DRO problems involving the multivariate Wasserstein distance are challenging because explicit formulas of the Wasserstein distance between distributions are only known for few distributions such as the Gaussian (\citet{dowson, gelbrich}). Numerical algorithms, e.g., the Sinkhorn algorithm, can be used in multivariate settings, but it is well-known that the computational cost significantly increases with the dimension of the problem (\citet{cuturi}). We contribute to the literature by proposing an upper bound to the inner optimization problem of \eqref{eq:DRO} that depends only on the univariate Wasserstein distance, regardless of the dimension of the original problem. Moreover, for specific cases we show that the multivariate problem is equal to a univariate one. Additionally, we consider uncertainty sets characterized via the BW divergence and again show that the multivariate problem can be bounded with a univariate one. Furthermore, some choices of BW uncertainty sets allows us to intimately tie the uncertainty sets to the aggregation function. Finally, we explore uncertainty sets induced by the max-sliced Wasserstein distance --- and introduce the new max-sliced BW divergence. We show that uncertainty sets characterized by the max-sliced Wasserstein distance account for both uncertainty in the risk factors and the function mapping risk factors to univariate decision variables.

Furthermore, instead of the classical DRO, we consider a generalized DRO problem, sometimes called risk-aware DRO,  by replacing the expectation in \eqref{eq:DRO} with an arbitrary law-invariant risk functional $\rho$ (also called a risk measure in the risk management literature). Many different classes of risk functionals are well studied in the literature. One of the earliest and most significant classes of risk functionals is the class of coherent risk measures, which was first introduced in \citet{artzner}. This class includes the expected value and the popular Expected Shortfall (ES). Over the past two decades, various other classes of risk functionals have been proposed, including convex risk measures (\citet{follmer}), generalized deviation measures (\citet{rockafellar}) and the characterization of law-invariant coherent risk measures (\citet{kusuoka}). More recently, \citet{wang} provided an extensive study of signed Choquet integrals, which are a large class of risk functionals containing the well-known class of distortion risk measures. Worst-case risk problems for distortion risk measures with different uncertainty sets are for example studied in \citet{bernard}, \citet{cai}, \citet{Coache2024WP}, and \citet{moresco2024uncertainty}. \citet{bernard} focus on Wasserstein distance constraints for univariate risks, \citet{cai} consider moment based constraints for multivariate risks, and \citet{Coache2024WP} and \citet{moresco2024uncertainty} work with robust dynamic risk measures. In our work, we consider multivariate risk factors and any law-invariant risk functional, and provide an application of our results to the class of signed Choquet integrals.

The paper is structured as follows. \Cref{sec:notation} introduces the notation, while \Cref{sec:static} is devoted to the inner optimization problem in \eqref{eq:DRO} for law-invariant risk functionals when $\mathcal{U}$ is a multivariate Wasserstein uncertainty set and $\ell$ is a Lipschitz continuous function. In \Cref{sec:bw}, we generalize our results to the case when $\mathcal{U}$ is a BW uncertainty set. In \Cref{sec:max_sliced}, we consider DRO problems where the uncertainty is in both $\ell$ and $\X$ and introduce the generalized max-sliced Wasserstein uncertainty. \Cref{sec:application} contains explicit bounds when the risk functional is a signed Choquet integral, and \Cref{sec:examples} illustrates numerical examples. 

\section{Multivariate Wasserstein Uncertainty}\label{sec:notation}

We work throughout with a non-atomic probability space $(\Omega, \B, \P)$ and for $n\in\N^+$, we denote by $\B:=\B(\R^n)$ the Borel sigma algebra on $\R^n$. We further denote vector valued random variables (rvs) on $(\Omega, \B, \P)$ with capital boldface letters and real valued vectors with lowercase boldface letters. For any random vector $\X=(X_1, \ldots, X_n)$ we write $F_{\X}(\x):=\P(\X\leq\x)$, $\x=(x_1, \ldots, x_n)\in\R^n$, for the cumulative distribution function (cdf) of $\X$, where vector inequalities are understood component-wise. For $a\geq 1$, we let $\norm{\x}_a:=(\sum_{i\in\mN}\abs{x_i}^a)^{\frac{1}{a}}$ denote the $\L^a$ norm on $\R^n$, where $\mN:=\{1,\ldots, n\}$. Furthermore, we denote by $\L^p_n$ the space of all $n$-dimensional random vectors $\X$ on $(\Omega, \B, \P)$ such that $\E[\norm{\X}_a^p]<\infty$, and by $\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$ the corresponding set of cdfs. As $a$ is fixed throughout, we write $\L^p_n$ and omit its dependence on the norm $a$. Moreover, if $n = 1$, we simply write $\L^p$. 
For any pair of random vectors $\X,\Y$, we denote by $\X\eqd \Y$ equality in distribution and by $\X=\Y$ $\P$-almost sure equality. 

The ($p$)-Wasserstein metric with norm $a$ on $\R^n$ (which we just call the Wasserstein distance) between $F\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$ and $G\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$ is defined as
\begin{equation} \label{d-dim_Wasserstein}
W^{n}(F,G):=\inf_{F_{\X}=F, F_{\Y}=G}(\E[\norm{\X-\Y}_{a}^{p}])^{\frac{1}{p}},
\end{equation}
where the infimum is taken over all cdfs on $\R^{n}\times\R^{n}$ with $n$-dimensional marginal distributions $F$ and $G$. As we fix $a,\ p\geq 1$ throughout the exposition, we simply write $W^n$, where $n$ indicates the dimension of the marginals, and omit the dependence on the power $p$ and the norm $a$.
For $n=1$, we again omit the dimension superscript, i.e., write $W(\cdot, \cdot):= W^1(\cdot, \cdot)$,  and note that the norm $a$ becomes irrelevant. For $n = 1$, by the well-known fact that the infimum is attained by the comonotonic coupling (\citet{dall}), the 1-dimensional Wasserstein distance has representation
\begin{equation} \label{eq:1d_Wasserstein}
W(F,G):=\Big(\int_{0}^{1}\abs{F^{-1}(s)-G^{-1}(s)}^p ds\Big)^{\frac{1}{p}}\,,
\end{equation}
where $F^{-1}(\alpha):=\inf\{x\in\R| F(x)\geq\alpha\}$, $\alpha\in (0,1)$, is the (left-continuous) quantile function of $F$.

Next, we introduce two different uncertainty sets characterized by the Wasserstein distance. 

\begin{definition} [Wasserstein Uncertainty Sets for cdfs] \label{definition:uncertainty} For $\epsilon\geq 0$, we define the following uncertainty sets:
\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]
\item The univariate Wasserstein uncertainty set (also called ball) around the cdf $F\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R)$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:1d_uncertainty}
\M_{\epsilon}(F):=\{G \in \mathcal{M}_p(\R)~|~W(G, F)\leq\epsilon\}.
\end{equation}
\item The multivariate Wasserstein uncertainty set (ball) around the cdf $H\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:d-dim_uncertainty}
\bM_{\epsilon}^{n}(H):=\{G\in \mathcal{M}_p(\R^{n})~|~W^{n}(G, H)\leq\epsilon\}.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}
In both uncertainty sets, the parameter $\epsilon$ (also called tolerance distance) represents the magnitude of uncertainty. That is, the larger $\ep$ is, the larger the uncertainty set becomes, and $\lim_{\ep \to \infty}\bM_{\epsilon}^{n}(H) = \mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$.

The set given by \Cref{eq:1d_uncertainty} corresponds to the set of univariate cdfs that are close (in the univariate Wasserstein distance) to the univariate reference cdf $F$. In contrast, the set given by \Cref{eq:d-dim_uncertainty} corresponds to the set of multivariate cdfs that are close (in multivariate Wasserstein distance) to the multivariate reference cdf $H$. Generally, it is more convenient to work with sets of rvs rather than cdfs, and DRO problems for law-invariant risk functionals can be equivalently stated in terms of rvs or cdfs, thus we introduce the following notation. 

\begin{definition} [Wasserstein Uncertainty Sets for RvS] \label{definition:uncertainty_rv} For $\epsilon\geq 0$, we define the following uncertainty sets:
\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]
\item The univariate Wasserstein uncertainty set (for rvs) around the rv $X\in\L^p$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:1d_uncertainty_rv}
\U_{\epsilon}(X):=\{Z\in\L^p~|~F_Z\in\M_{\epsilon}(F_X)\}.
\end{equation}
\item The multivariate Wasserstein uncertainty set (for random vectors) around the random vector $\X\in\L^p_n$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:d-dim_uncertainty_rv}
\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X):=\{\Z\in\L^p_n~|~F_{\Z}\in\bM_{\epsilon}^{n}(F_{\X})\}.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

Throughout the paper, we use $g \colon \R^n\to\R$ to denote an aggregation (or prediction) function, that maps input risk factors $\X$ to a univariate output decision rv $g(\X)$. Here we assume that the aggregation function is given, e.g., estimated via statistical methods, and that the uncertainty stems from the risk factors $\X$ alone. In \Cref{sec:max_sliced}, we explore, via the max-sliced Wasserstein distance, uncertainty in both $\X$ and $g$. 
The distribution of the aggregate, i.e. $g(\X)$, is of interest in many practical applications where decisions are based on a univariate output $g(\X)$, particularly any DRO setting. In an investment setting, for example, $g(\X)$ could be the payoff of a portfolio with $n$ risky assets, where each component of $\X$ is a risky asset price and $g$ depends on the investments made. 

We consider two ways to quantify uncertainty around the distribution of $g(\X)$, $(i)$ uncertainty in the risk factors $\X$ which then propagates to uncertainty in $g(\X)$, and $(ii)$ uncertainty directly on the aggregate $g(\X)$. The former approach uses the multivariate Wasserstein uncertainty set given in \eqref{eq:d-dim_uncertainty_rv} to account for uncertainty in $\X$. In this case, the resulting uncertainty set for the aggregate risk is given by $g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big):=\{g(\Z)~|~\Z\in\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\}$, that is, each random vector in the uncertainty set $Z \in \bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)$ is mapped to a potential aggregate output $g(\Z)$. \Cref{fig:set} provides an illustration of the set $g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)$. The second approach considers uncertainty in the aggregate $g(\X)$ via the univariate Wasserstein ball around $g(\X)$. In general, these two approaches for introducing uncertainty are not the same, i.e., $g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)\neq \U_{\epsilon}(g(\X))$. 

For the set $g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)$, the source of the uncertainty stems from the multivariate risk factor $\X$, whereas in $\U_{\epsilon}(g(\X))$ the uncertainty could stem from the risk factors $\X$ and/or the aggregation function $g$. In other words, the source of uncertainty is more clearly identifiable in sets of the form \eqref{eq:d-dim_uncertainty_rv}, making this choice more attractive in practical applications. However, the multivariate Wasserstein distance is difficult to compute for most distributions and numerical algorithms such as the Sinkhorn algorithm are typically the only tools available. Thus, we aim to find relationships between the sets \eqref{eq:1d_uncertainty} and \eqref{eq:d-dim_uncertainty} for different classes of functions $g$ so that we can rewrite or bound the inner optimization problem in \eqref{eq:DRO} with an optimization problem over rvs. 

