\section{DRO with Wasserstein Uncertainty}\label{sec:static}

In this section, we provide conditions on the aggregation function $g$ such that the univariate uncertainty around $g(\X)$ is equal to the uncertainty set that maps the multivariate uncertainty around $\X$ to uncertainty around $g(\X)$, i.e. $g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)= \U_{\epsilon'}(g(\X))$. We note that the tolerance distances for the two uncertainty sets are different.

Throughout the manuscript, we make the assumption that the aggregation function is non-constant, since otherwise, the inner problem of \eqref{eq:DRO} becomes meaningless.
\begin{assumption}
    The aggregation function $g\colon \R^n \to \R$ is non-constant. 
\end{assumption}

\smallskip

\subsection{Lipschitz Aggregation Functions}\label{sec:Lipschitz}

We first consider the class of $L$-Lipschitz functions $f:\R^n\to\R$. Recall that a function $f$ is $L$-Lipschitz (wrt the $\L^a$ norm) if $\abs{f(\x)-f(\y)}\leq L\norm{\x-\y}_a$ for all $\x,\y\in\R^n$. 

The first result states that for Lipschitz functions, the uncertainty set stemming from the risk factors is contained in the univariate uncertainty set around the aggregate $g(\X)$ with a tolerance distance of $\ep L$, the product of the Lipschitz constant and the tolerance distance $\ep$ of the multivariate uncertainty set. Lipschitz continuous functions are prevalent in portfolio optimization, important for fairness assessment (\citet{dwork2012fairness}), as well as ubiquitous in machine learning. Indeed, any fully connected network (FCN) and any convolutional neural network (CNN) with Lipschitz activation functions (e.g., ReLU, SoftPlus, sigmoid) is itself Lipschitz continuous. We refer to \citet{virmaux2018NIPS,Jordan2020NIPS}, and \citet{Kim2021proce} for details and discussions on Lipschitz constants of neural networks.

\begin{theorem} [Lipschitz aggregation]\label{theorem:Lipschitz} Let $g:\R^n\to\R$ be $L$-Lipschitz wrt the $\L^a$ norm and $\X\in\L^p_n$ such that $g(\X)\in\L^p$. Then, for any $\epsilon\geq 0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Lipschitz}
g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)\subseteq\U_{L\epsilon}(g(\X)).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} Let $Z\in g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)$. By definition of $g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)$, there exists a random vector $\Z$ such that $W^{n}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})\leq\epsilon$ and $Z=g(\Z)$. Therefore, 
\begin{align*}
W(F_{g(\Z)}, F_{g(\X)}) &=\inf_{X'\eqd g(\X), \ Z'\eqd g(\Z)}\E[\abs{X'-Z'}^p]^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&= \inf_{g(\X')\eqd g(\X), \ g(\Z')\eqd g(\Z)}\E\big[\abs{g(\X')-g(\Z')}^p\big]^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&\leq \inf_{g(\X')\eqd g(\X), \ g(\Z')\eqd g(\Z)}  \E\big[L\norm{\X'-\Z'}_{a}^{p}\big]^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&\leq \inf_{\X'\eqd \X, \ \Z'\eqd \Z} \E\big[L\norm{\X'-\Z'}_{a}^{p}\big]^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&=L\, W^{n}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X}) \\
&\leq L\, \epsilon,
\end{align*}
and we conclude that $Z \in \U_{L\epsilon}(g(\X))$.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

\begin{remark} It follows from the definition of the uncertainty sets for rvs and cdfs, that the set inclusion of \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz} also holds for the uncertainty sets described by cdfs. That is, under the assumptions of \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz}, it holds that $g\big(\bM_{\epsilon}^{n}(F_{\X})\big)\subseteq\M_{L\epsilon}(F_{g(\X)})$, where $g\big(\bM_{\epsilon}^{n}(F_{\X})\big):=\{F_{g(\Z)}\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R)~|~F_{\Z}\in\bM_{\epsilon}^{n}(F_{\X})\} = \{F_Z\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R)~|~Z \in g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)\}$.
\end{remark}

Next, we are interested in when the set inclusion in \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz} becomes a set equality. We first show that Lipschitz continuity is in general not sufficient, i.e., the set inclusion is strict. To see this, let $g(\x)=\sin(x_1)$ (which is Lipschitz with $L=1$), $\epsilon=3$, $\X\in\L^p_n$, and define $Z:=g(\X)+\epsilon$. Since $\E[\abs{Z-g(\X)}^p])^{\frac{1}{p}}=\epsilon$, it follows that $Z\in\U_{L\epsilon}(g(\X))$. Furthermore, for any random vector $\Y$, the support of $g(\Y)=\sin(Y_1)$ is a subset of $[-1,1]$. Since $Z=\sin(X_1)+3$ is supported on a subset of $[2,4]$, it follows that there does not exist a random vector $\Z$ such that $g(\Z)=Z$. Therefore, $Z\notin g\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n}(\X)\big)$. 

To obtain set equality, we consider a subclass of Lipschitz functions $\mfg:\R^{n+1}\to\R$ defined by $\mfg(\x,x'):=g(\x)+\beta x'$, where $g:\R^n\to\R$ is $L$-Lipschitz wrt the $\L^a$ norm and $\abs{\beta}\geq L$. That is, at least one of the components (which we assume w.l.o.g. to be the last one) is linear. We use the notation $\mfg$ whenever the aggregation function has at least one linear component. The next lemma shows that this class of functions is $\abs{\beta}$-Lipschitz. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:Lipschitz}
Let $g:\R^n\to\R$ be $L$-Lipschitz wrt the $\L^a$ norm. If $\abs{\beta}\geq L$, then the function $\mfg:\R^{n+1}\to\R$ given by $\mfg(\x,x')=g(\x)+\beta x'$ is $\abs{\beta}$-Lipschitz wrt the $\L^a$ norm. 
\end{lemma}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} Let $\x,\y\in\R^n$, $\mfg(\x,x')=g(\x)+\beta x'$ for some $L$-Lipschitz function $g$, and $\abs{\beta}\geq L$. Then,
\begin{align*}
\abs{\mfg(\x,x')-\mfg(\y,y')} &= \abs{\mfg(\x,x')-\mfg(\y,x')+\mfg(\y,x')-\mfg(\y,y')} \\
&\leq \abs{\mfg(\x,x')-\mfg(\y,x')} + \abs{\mfg(\y,x')-\mfg(\y,y')} \\
&\leq L\norm{\x-\y}_a+\abs{\beta}\abs{x'-y'} \\
&\leq \abs{\beta}\norm{(\x,x')-(\y,y')}_a\,,
\end{align*}
where the last inequality follows from the fact that $\norm{\x-\y}_a\leq\norm{(\x,x')-(\y,y')}_a$.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

For this subclass of Lipschitz continuous functions, we show next that $\U_{\abs{\beta}\epsilon}(\mfg(\X))\subseteq \mfg\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n+1}(\X)\big)$. Then, by \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz}, we obtain that the two sets (and the analogous sets for cdfs) are equal. This result generalizes Theorem 5 of \citet{mao}, who consider linear aggregation functions. There are different streams of literature that aim at simplifying robust DRO problems. \citet{Pesenti2024MP} for examples studies when non-convex risk-aware DRO problems can be recast as convex risk-aware DRO problems. While these authors show necessary and sufficient assumptions on the uncertainty set to obtain equality  and mostly work with univariate uncertainty sets, here we focus on upper bounds or restrictions on the aggregation function. 

\begin{proposition} [Lipschitz aggregation with linear component]\label{prop:lin_comp} 
Let $\mfg:\R^{n+1}\to\R$ such that $\mfg(\x,x')=g(\x)+\beta x'$, where $g:\R^n\to\R$ is $L$-Lipschitz wrt the $\L^a$ norm and $\abs{\beta}\geq L$. Let $\X\in\L^p_{n+1}$ with $\mfg(\X)\in\L^p$. Then, for any $\epsilon \geq 0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:lin_comp}
\mfg\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n+1}(\X)\big)
=
\U_{\abs{\beta}\epsilon}(\mfg(\X))
.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} For simplicity of notation, we write $A:=\mfg\big(\bU_{\epsilon}^{n+1}(\X)\big)$, $B:=\U_{\abs{\beta}\epsilon}(\mfg(\X))$, and $\X:=(X_1, \ldots, X_{n+1})\in\R^{n+1}$. We want to show that $A\subseteq B$ and $B\subseteq A$. The fact that $A\subseteq B$ follows from a similar argument to \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz} since $g$ is $\abs{\beta}$-Lipschitz by \Cref{lemma:Lipschitz}. 

\smallskip

Next, we show that $B\subseteq A$. Let $Y\in B$. Since $\{V~|~\big(\E[\abs{V-\mfg(\X)}^p]\big)^{\frac{1}{p}}\leq \abs{\beta}\epsilon\}$ is a closed set, there exists a rv $Z$ such that $\big(\E[\abs{Z-\mfg(\X)}^p]\big)^{\frac{1}{p}}\leq \abs{\beta}\epsilon$ and $Y=Z$. Define $\Z=\X+\frac{1}{\beta}\e_{n+1}(Z-\mfg(\X))$, where $\e_{n+1}:=(0, 0, \ldots, 0, 1)\in\R^{n+1}$. It suffices to show that $\mfg(\Z)=Z$ and $W^{n+1}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})\leq\epsilon$.

\smallskip

Since $\Z=\big(X_1, \ldots, X_n, X_{n+1}+\frac{1}{\beta}\big(Z-\mfg(\X)\big)\big)$, it holds that
\begin{align*}
\mfg(\Z) &= g(X_1, \ldots, X_n)+\beta \left(X_{n+1}+\tfrac{1}{\beta}\big(Z-\mfg(\X)\big)\right) \\
&= g(X_1, \ldots, X_n)+\beta X_{n+1}+Z-g(X_1, \ldots, X_n)-\beta X_{n+1} 
= Z.
\end{align*}
Moreover,
\begin{align*}
W^{n+1}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X}) 
&=
\inf_{\X'\eqd\X, \Z'\eqd\Z}\big(\E\big[\norm{\Z'-\X'}_{a}^{p}\big]\big)^{\frac{1}{p}} 
\\
&\leq \big(\E[\norm{\Z-\X}_{a}^{p}]\big)^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&= \Big(\E\Big[\norm{\e_{n+1}\tfrac{1}{\beta}\big(Z-\mfg(\X)\big)}_{a}^{p}\Big]\Big)^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&= \Big(\E\Big[\abs{\tfrac{1}{\beta}\big(Z-\mfg(\X)\big)}^{p}\Big]\Big)^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&= \tfrac{1}{\abs{\beta}}\big(\E[\abs{Z-\mfg(\X)}^{p}]\big)^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&\leq \tfrac{\abs{\beta}}{\abs{\beta}}\epsilon\\
&= \epsilon,
\end{align*}
which concludes the proof.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

Next, we illustrate how \Cref{prop:lin_comp} and \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz} can be used to solve (risk-aware) DRO problems for law-invariant risk functionals, which of course includes the expected value. For this we denote by $\rho:\L^p\to\R$ a risk functional and say that $\rho$ is law-invariant if $\rho(X)=\rho(Y)$ whenever $X\eqd Y$. 

\begin{proposition} [Worst-case risks under Lipschitz aggregation]\label{prop:Lipschitz}
Let $g:\R^n\to\R$ be $L$-Lipschitz wrt the $\L^a$ norm. Further, let $\rho$ be a law-invariant risk functional and $\epsilon\geq 0$. Then the following holds:

\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]

    \item If $\X\in\L^p_n$ with $g(\X)\in\L^p$, then
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Lipschitz_bound} 
\sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X)}\rho\big(g(\Y)\big)
\leq
\sup_{Y\in \U_{L\epsilon}(g(\X))}\rho(Y)\,.
\end{equation}

    \item Define $\mfg:\R^{n+1}\to\R$ such that $\mfg(\x,x')=g(\x)+\beta x'$, where $\abs{\beta}\geq L$. If $\X\in\L^p_{n+1}$ with $\mfg(\X)\in\L^p$, then
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Lipschitz eq} 
\sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n+1}_{\epsilon}(\X)}\rho\big(\mfg(\Y)\big) =
\sup_{Y\in \U_{\abs{\beta}\epsilon}(\mfg(\X))}\rho(Y).
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}   
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} Case $i)$ follows from \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz} and $ii)$ follows from \Cref{prop:lin_comp}. 
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

\subsection{Locally Lipschitz Aggregation Functions}\label{sec:loc_Lipschitz}

We extend the results of the preceding section by relaxing the assumptions on the aggregation function in that we allow the Lipschitz condition to hold locally instead of globally. We say a function $f\colon \R^n \to \R$ is locally $L$-Lipschitz on a compact set $C \subseteq \R^n$, if $\abs{f(\x)-f(\y)}\leq L\norm{\x-\y}_a$ for all $\x,\y\in C$.

In the locally Lipschitz case, we require some additional boundedness assumptions on the risk factors $\X$. For this we denote by $\text{supp}(\Y)$ the support of a random vector $\Y$. In this section, when considering locally Lipschitz aggregation functions $g:\R^{n}\to\R$, we require the existence of a compact subset $C\subseteq\R^{n}$, such that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:boundedness}
\text{supp}\big((X_1, \ldots, X_n)\big)\subseteq C.
\end{equation} 

Moreover, similar to \Cref{prop:Lipschitz}, when one of the components of the locally Lipschitz aggregation function is linear, we obtain an equality of the two uncertainty sets. In this case, if $\mfg:\R^{n+1}\to\R$ is locally Lipschitz and linear in the last component, then we require $(X_1, \ldots, X_n)$ to have compact support. That is, the last component $X_{n+1}$ can be unbounded as long as $\X\in\L^p_{n+1}$. The subsequent results rely on a property of locally Lipschitz functions from \citet{scanlon}, recalled next.

\begin{lemma} \label{lemma:loc_lip} (Theorem 2.1 from \citet{scanlon}) Let $(M,d)$ be a metric space. A function $f:M\to\R$ is locally Lipschitz if and only if it is Lipschitz on each compact subset of $M$. 
\end{lemma}

Due to the boundedness assumption given by \Cref{eq:boundedness}, we slightly modify the notation of the uncertainty sets used in \Cref{sec:Lipschitz}. 

\begin{definition} [Wasserstein Uncertainty --- Compact Support] \label{definition:uncertainty_c} Let $\epsilon\geq 0$. Then, the multivariate Wasserstein uncertainty set for random vectors with compact support $C\subseteq\R^n$ around the cdf $F_{\X}\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$, is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:d-dim_uncertainty_c}
\bM_{\epsilon,C}^{n}(F_{\X}):=\{F_{\Z}\in \mathcal{M}_p(\R^{n})~|~W^{n}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})\leq\epsilon, \ \text{supp}\big((Z_1, \ldots, Z_{n})\big)\subseteq C\}, 
\end{equation}
where $\X=(X_1, \ldots, X_{n})$ satisfies the boundedness condition given by \Cref{eq:boundedness}.
\end{definition}

When working with aggregation functions that are linear in the last component, we work with the larger set, for which we use the superscript $l$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:d-dim_uncertainty_cl}
\bM_{\epsilon,C}^{l,n}(F_{\X}):=\{F_{\Z}\in \mathcal{M}_p(\R^{n})~|~W^{n}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})\leq\epsilon, \ \text{supp}\big((Z_1, \ldots, Z_{n-1})\big)\subseteq C\},
\end{equation}
where $C\subseteq\R^{n-1}$ is compact, $\text{supp}\big((X_1, \ldots, X_{n-1})\big)\subseteq C$ and $\X\in\L^p_n$. The corresponding uncertainty sets for random vectors are $\bU^{n}_{\epsilon,C}(\X):=\{\Z\in\L^p_n~|~F_{\Z}\in\bM_{\epsilon,C}^{n}(F_{\X})\}$ and $\bU^{l,n}_{\epsilon,C}(\X):=\{\Z\in\L^p_n~|~F_{\Z}\in\bM_{\epsilon,C}^{l,n}(F_{\X})\}$. Now, we are ready to state results analogous to \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz} and \Cref{prop:Lipschitz} for locally Lipschitz aggregation functions.   

\begin{theorem} [Locally Lipschitz aggregation]\label{theorem:loc_lip} Let $\epsilon\geq 0$. Then the following holds:
\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]

    \item Let $\X\in\L^p_n$ such that \Cref{eq:boundedness} is satisfied for some compact subset $C\subseteq\R^{n}$ and let $g:\R^n\to\R$ be locally $L$-Lipschitz on $C$ wrt the $\L^a$ norm. Then, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:loc_lip_1}
g\big(\bU_{\epsilon,C}^{n}(\X)\big)
\subseteq
\U_{L\epsilon}\big(g(\X)\big)\, .
\end{equation}

    \item Let $\X\in\L^p_{n+1}$ such that $(X_1, \ldots, X_n)$ satisfies \Cref{eq:boundedness} for some compact subset $C\subseteq\R^{n}$. Further let $\mfg:\R^{n+1}\to\R$ be given by $\mfg(\x,x')=g(\x)+\beta x'$, where $g:\R^n\to\R$ is locally $L$-Lipschitz on $C$ wrt the $\L^a$ norm and $\abs{\beta}\geq L$. Then, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:loc_lip_2}
\mfg\big(\bU_{\epsilon,C}^{l,n+1}(\X)\big)
=
\U_{\abs{\beta}\epsilon}\big(\mfg(\X)\big)\, .
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} 
\end{theorem}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} As $i)$ follows using similar arguments as in the proof of $ii)$, we only prove $ii)$. For simplicity of notation, we write $A:=\mfg\big(\bU_{\epsilon,C}^{l,n+1}(\X)\big)$, $B:=\U_{\abs{\beta}\epsilon}(\mfg(\X))$, and $\X:=(X_1, \ldots, X_{n+1})\in\R^{n+1}$. We first show that $A\subseteq B$.

\smallskip

Let $Z\in A$. By definition of $A$, there exists a random vector $\Z\in \L^p_{n+1}$ such that $W^{n+1}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})\leq\epsilon$, $Z=\mfg(\Z)$, and $\text{supp}((Z_1, \ldots, Z_n))\subseteq C$. Moreover, 
\begin{align*}
W(F_{\mfg(\Z)}, F_{\mfg(\X)}) \ &=\inf_{X'\eqd \mfg(\X), \ Z'\eqd \mfg(\Z)}\E[\abs{X'-Z'}^p]^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&= \inf_{\mfg(\X')\eqd \mfg(\X), \ \mfg(\Z')\eqd \mfg(\Z)}\E[\abs{\mfg(\X')-\mfg(\Z')}^p]^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&\leq \inf_{\X'\eqd \X, \ \Z'\eqd \Z} \E[\abs{\mfg(\X')-\mfg(\Z')}^p]^{\frac{1}{p}}\\
&\leq \inf_{\X'\eqd \X, \ \Z'\eqd \Z} \abs{\beta}\E[\norm{\X'-\Z'}_{a}^{p}]^{\frac{1}{p}} \\
&= \abs{\beta}W^{n+1}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X}) \\
&\leq \abs{\beta}\epsilon,
\end{align*}
where the second last inequality follows from \Cref{lemma:Lipschitz,lemma:loc_lip}.

\smallskip

Next, we show that $B\subseteq A$. For this, let $Y\in B$. Since $\{W~|~\big(\E[\abs{W-\mfg(\X)}^p]\big)^{\frac{1}{p}}\leq \abs{\beta}\epsilon\}$ is a closed set, there exists a rv $Z$ such that $\big(\E[\abs{Z-\mfg(\X)}^p]\big)^{\frac{1}{p}}\leq \abs{\beta}\epsilon$ and $Y=Z$. Define $\Z:=\X+\frac{1}{\beta}\e_{n+1}(Z-\mfg(\X))$, where $\e_{n+1}:=(0, 0, \ldots, 0, 1)\in\R^{n+1}$. 
It suffices to show that $\mfg(\Z)=Z$, $W^{n+1}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})\leq\epsilon$, and that $\text{supp}((Z_1, \ldots, Z_n))\subseteq C$. The first two facts follow from similar arguments as in the proof of \Cref{prop:lin_comp}. Finally, by definition of $\Z$, we have that $\Z=\big(X_1, \ldots, X_n, X_{n+1}+\frac{1}{\abs{\beta}}(Z-\mfg(\X))\big)$. Thus, $\text{supp}((Z_1, \ldots, Z_n))\subseteq C$ holds since $\X$ satisfies \Cref{eq:boundedness}.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

Applying the above results to risk-aware DRO problems, we obtain the following statement.
\begin{proposition} [Worst-case risks under locally Lipschitz aggregation]\label{prop:loc_lip} Let $\rho$ be a law-invariant risk functional and $\epsilon\geq 0$. Then the following holds:

\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]
    \item Let $\X\in\L^p_n$ such that \Cref{eq:boundedness} is satisfied for some compact subset $C\subseteq\R^{n}$ and let $g:\R^n\to\R$ be a locally $L$-Lipschitz on $C$ wrt the $\L^a$ norm. Then, 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:loc_lip_bound} \sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n}_{\epsilon,C}(\X)}\rho\big(g(\Y)\big)\leq\sup_{Y\in \U_{L\epsilon}(g(\X))}\rho(Y)\,.
\end{equation}

    \item Let $\X\in\L^p_{n+1}$ such that \Cref{eq:boundedness} is satisfied for some compact subset $C\subseteq\R^{n}$. Further, let $\mfg:\R^{n+1}\to\R$ such that $\mfg(\x,x')=g(\x)+\beta x'$, where $g:\R^n\to\R$ is a locally $L$-Lipschitz on $C$ wrt the $\L^a$ norm and $\abs{\beta}\geq L$. Then, 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:loc_lip_eq} \sup_{\Y\in \bU^{l,n+1}_{\epsilon,C}(\X)}\rho\big(\mfg(\Y)\big)=\sup_{Y\in \U_{\abs{\beta}\epsilon}(\mfg(\X))}\rho(Y)\,.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} 
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} This follows from \Cref{theorem:loc_lip}.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

\section{DRO with Bregman-Wasserstein Uncertainty} \label{sec:bw}

\smallskip

Here we consider uncertainty sets defined via a BW divergence, which is a generalization of the Wasserstein distance. Specifically, we consider uncertainty sets defined as all random vectors that have a BW divergence of at most $\ep$ around a reference random vector.

Key results are in \Cref{sec:m_distance}, where we generalize \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz} and the first half of \Cref{prop:Lipschitz} to a special case of the BW divergence, the Mahalanobis distance. In \Cref{sec:separable}, we study multivariate Bregman generators that can be written as sums of univariate (so-called separable) Bregman generators, and in \Cref{sec:composable}, we consider Bregman generators that are compositions of a univariate generator and a scalar valued aggregation function.


\subsection{Multivariate Bregman-Wasserstein Uncertainty} \label{sec:notation_bregman}

Before defining the BW divergence, we recall the definition of the Bregman divergence. Unlike the Wasserstein distance, the Bregman divergence is not necessarily symmetric. This allows for different penalizations of positive and negative deviations, which is of importance in financial applications. 

\begin{definition}[Bregman Divergence]  \label{definition:bregman}
A Bregman generator is a function $\bphi: \R^n\to\R, \ n\geq 1$, that is convex and differentiable. The Bregman divergence associated with Bregman generator $\bphi$ is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bregman}
    B_{\bphi}\big(\z_1, \z_2\big)
    := \bphi(\z_1) - \bphi(\z_2) - \nabla\bphi(\z_2)\cdot(\z_1-\z_2)
    \,,\quad \z_1,\z_2\in\R^{n}\,,
\end{equation}
where $\nabla\bphi(\z)$ denotes the gradient of $\bphi$ and $\cdot$ denotes the dot product.
\end{definition}

Note that the convexity of $\bphi$ guarantees that the Bregman divergence is non-negative. When the Bregman generator is the squared 2-norm, i.e. $\phi(\x) =\norm{\x}_2^2$, then the BW divergence reduces to the squared $2$-Wasserstein distance with norm $\L^2$. If $\phi$ is strictly convex, then the Bregman divergence $B_{\bphi}\big(\z_1, \z_2\big)$ is zero if and only if $\z_1=\z_2$ (and therefore is a mathematical divergence). Here, we only require $\bphi$ to be convex, and refer to \citet{pesenti3} for examples of non-strictly convex generators to model distributional uncertainty. 

\begin{definition} [BW Divergence] \label{definition:bw} Let $\bphi : \R^{n} \to \R$ be a Bregman generator. Then the BW divergence associated with $\bphi$, from cdf $F$ to cdf $G$, is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bw}
    \DB^{n}_{\bphi} [F, G]
    := \inf_{F_{\X}=F, F_{\Y}=G}\E\big[B_{\bphi}\big(\X, \Y\big)\big].
\end{equation}
\end{definition}
For random vectors $\X, \Y$, with cdfs $F, G$, respectively, we use the notation $\DB^{n}_{\bphi} (\X, \Y) = \DB^{n}_{\bphi} [F, G]$, that is round brackets for random vectors and square brackets for cdfs. 

For $n=1$, the infimum is achieved by the comonotonic coupling (\citet{pesenti}). In other words, we can rewrite the 1-dimensional BW divergence as 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:1d_bregman}
\DB_{\phi}[F, G]=\int_{0}^{1}B_{\phi}\big(F^{-1}(t), G^{-1}(t)\big)dt.
\end{equation}
For $n= 1$ and using \eqref{eq:1d_bregman}, it is straightforward to prove that the BW divergence is convex in its first component on the space of quantile functions (\citet{pesenti3}). This is however, not necessarily the case for arbitrary dimensions. Indeed, even though the Bregman divergence is convex in its first argument, the BW divergence is generally not convex in its first argument on the space of random vectors. We refer to \citet{pesenti}, \citet{rankin}, and the references therein, for detailed discussions of the BW divergence.

With the definition of the BW divergence at hand, we now introduce the univariate, and two versions of multivariate BW uncertainty sets.

\begin{definition}[BW Uncertainty Sets]
For $\epsilon\geq 0$, we define the following uncertainty sets:
\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]

    \item The univariate BW uncertainty set, associated with Bregman generator $\phi:\R\to\R$, around the rv $X$ is given by 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:univ_bw_set_1}
\mfB_{\phi, \epsilon}(X):=\{Z~|~\DB_{\phi}[F_Z,F_X]\leq\epsilon\}.
\end{equation} 

    \item The multivariate BW uncertainty set, associated with $\bphi:\R^{n}\to\R$, around the random vector $\X$ is given by 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mv_bw_set_1}
\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X):=\{\Z~|~\DB^{n}_{\bphi}[F_{\Z},F_{\X}]\leq\epsilon\}.
\end{equation}

    \item Let $\bphi:\R^{n}\to\R$ be given by $\bphi(\x)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\phi_k(x_k)$ for Bregman generators $\phi_k:\R\to\R$, $k\in\mN$. We define an alternative multivariate BW uncertainty set, associated with $\bphi$, around the random vector $\X$ by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mv_bw_set_2}
\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X):=\mfB_{\phi_1, \epsilon}(X_1)\times \ldots\times\mfB_{\phi_n, \epsilon}(X_n),
\end{equation}
where $\times$ denotes the Cartesian product.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}
Whenever we write $\mfB_{\phi, \epsilon}(X), \bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$ or $\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$, we tacitly assume that the uncertainty sets contain at least two, and thus infinitely many elements.

The uncertainty set $\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$ contains all random vectors that have a BW divergence of at most $\ep$ from the random vector $\X$. The alternative uncertainty set $\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$ makes use of the additive structure of its Bregman generator. Indeed, $\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$ contains all random vectors $\Z$, such that each component of $\Z$ is close in a univariate BW divergence to the corresponding component of $\X$, i.e., 
\begin{equation*}
    \bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)
    =
    \big\{\Z~|~ \DB_{\phi_k}\big(Z_k, X_k \big) \le \ep\,, \ \text{for all} \ k \in \mN\big\}\,.
\end{equation*}
The two multivariate uncertainty sets are in general not equal. However, in the next proposition, we show that if $\bphi(\x) = \sum_{k \in \mN}\phi_k(x_k)$, then $\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)\subseteq\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$. Thus, allowing for deviation, measured via the BW divergence $\DB^{n}_{\bphi}$, between all components simultaneously, yields a smaller uncertainty set than if we separately account for uncertainty in each component.

\begin{proposition} [BW uncertainty sets] \label{prop:bregman} 
Let $\phi_k:\R\to\R$, $k \in \mN$, be Bregman generators and define the Bregman generator $\bphi(\x)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\phi_k(x_k)$. Then, for any $\epsilon\geq 0$, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bregman_sets}
\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)\subseteq\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X).
\end{equation}
Furthermore, if $\ep>0$ and there exists $\Z\in\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$, such that $\DB_{\phi_l}(F_{Z_l},F_{X_l})=\epsilon$ for some $l\in\mN$ and $Z_m\neq X_m$ for some $ m \neq l \in \mN$, then the inclusion in \Cref{eq:bregman_sets} is strict. 
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} Let $\Z=(Z_1, \ldots, Z_n)\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$. It suffices to show that $Z_k\in\mfB_{\phi_k, \epsilon}(X_k)$ for all $k\in\mN$. For any $k\in\mN$, 
\begin{align*}
\DB_{\phi_k}[F_{Z_k},F_{X_k}] &= \quad \inf_{Z'_k\eqd Z_k, X'_k\eqd X_k}\E[B_{\phi_k}(Z'_k,X'_k)] \\
&\leq \ \sum_{i\in\mN} \inf_{Z'_i\eqd Z_i, X'_i\eqd X_i}\E[B_{\phi_i}(Z'_i,X'_i)] \\
&= \inf_{\Z'\eqd \Z, \X'\eqd \X}\E\Big[\sum_{i\in\mN}\phi_i(Z'_i)-\phi_i(X'_i)-\phi'_i(X'_i)(Z'_i-X'_i)\Big] \\
&= \inf_{\Z'\eqd \Z, \X'\eqd \X}\E[B_{\bphi}(\Z',\X')] \\
&\leq\epsilon, 
\end{align*}
where the first inequality follows since the Bregman divergence is non-negative and the second inequality follows from as $\Z\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$. The second equality follows as each term in the sum only depends on a single component of $\X$ and $\Z$.
Since $k$ was arbitrary, it follows that $\Z\in\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$.

\smallskip

For the second statement, the assumptions on $\Z$ imply that $\DB_{\phi_l}[F_{Z_l},F_{X_l}]<\DB^{n}_{\bphi}[F_Z,F_X]$. Since $\DB_{\phi_l}[F_{Z_l},F_{X_l}]=\epsilon$, it follows that $\epsilon=\DB_{\phi_l}[F_{Z_l},F_{X_l}]<\DB^{n}_{\bphi}[F_{\Z},F_{\X}]$ and $\Z\notin\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$. 
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

\subsection{The Mahalanobis Distance} \label{sec:m_distance}

For the first example of a BW divergence, we consider the Mahalanobis distance as the Bregman generator. The Mahalanobis distance is a popular distance used in classification problems (\citet{mclachlan}) as well as in financial applications.

\begin{definition} [Mahalanobis Distance] \label{definition:m_distance} Let $Q$ be a symmetric, positive semi-definite  $n\times n$ matrix. Then the squared Mahalanobis distance between $\x\in\R^n$ and $\y\in\R^n$ is given by $(\x-\y)^TQ(\x-\y)$. 
\end{definition}

The main result of this subsection is for a special case of the Mahalanobis distance when $Q$ is a diagonal matrix. Since the Wasserstein distance is a special case of the Mahalanobis distance with $Q$ equal to the identity matrix, this result generalizes \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz}.

\begin{theorem} [Mahalanobis distance]\label{theorem:m_distance} Let $g:\R^{n}\to\R$ be L-Lipschitz wrt $\L^2$ norm and $\X\in\L^2_n$ such that $g(\X)\in\L^2$. Let $Q$ be a positive semi-definite diagonal $n\times n$ matrix and denote by $\lambda\geq 0$ its smallest eigenvalue. Furthermore define the Bregman generators $\bphi(\x)=\x^TQ\x$ and $\phi(x)=\lambda x^2$. Then, for any $\epsilon \geq 0$, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:m_distance}
g\big(\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)\big)
\subseteq
\mfB_{\phi, L\epsilon}\big(g(X)\big).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} 
Let $\Z\in \bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$. Then by definition we have that $\DB^{n}_{\bphi}[F_{\Z}, F_{\X}]\leq\epsilon$. Moreover, the BW divergence from $g(\Z)$ to $g(\X)$ satisfies
\begin{align*}
\DB_{\phi}[F_{g(\Z)}, F_{g(\X)}] &=\inf_{X\eqd g(\X), \ Z\eqd g(\Z)}\E[\lambda(Z-X)^2] \\
&= \inf_{g(\X')\eqd g(\X), \ g(\Z')\eqd g(\Z)}\lambda\, \E\big[\abs{g(\Z')-g(\X')}^2\big] \\
&\leq \inf_{g(\X')\eqd g(\X), \ g(\Z')\eqd g(\Z)}  \E\big[L\lambda\norm{\Z'-\X'}_{2}^{2}\big]\\
&\leq \inf_{\X'\eqd \X, \ \Z'\eqd \Z} \E\big[L\lambda\norm{\Z'-\X'}_{2}^{2}\big] \\
&= \inf_{\X'\eqd \X, \ \Z'\eqd \Z} \E\big[L\lambda\sum_{i\in\mN}(Z'_i-X'_i)^2\big] \\
&\leq \inf_{\X'\eqd \X, \ \Z'\eqd \Z} \E\big[L(\Z'-\X')^TQ(\Z'-\X')\big] \\
&= L\,\DB^{n}_{\bphi}[F_{\Z}, F_{\X}] \\
&\leq L\, \epsilon\,,
\end{align*}
where the first inequality follows by Lipschitz continuity of $\mfg$. Thus, $g(\Z) \in \mfB_{\phi, L\epsilon}\big(g(\X)\big)$.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

The following corollary follows from \Cref{theorem:m_distance}.

\begin{corollary} \label{cor:m_distance} Let $g:\R^{n}\to\R$ be an L-Lipschitz function wrt the $\L^2$ norm and $\X\in\L^2_n$ with $g(\X)\in\L^2$. Let $Q$ be a positive semi-definite diagonal $n\times n$ matrix and denote by $\lambda\geq 0$ its smallest eigenvalue. Furthermore define the Bregman generators $\bphi(\x)=\x^TQ\x$ and $\phi(x)=\lambda x^2$. Then, for any $\epsilon \geq 0$ and law-invariant risk functional $\rho$,
\begin{equation} \label{eq:m_distance_bound} \sup_{\Y\in \bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}\rho\big(g(\Y)\big)\leq\sup_{Y\in \mfB_{\phi, L\epsilon}(g(\X))}\rho(Y).
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Separable Bregman Generators}\label{sec:separable}

In this section, we consider Bregman generators $\bphi:\R^{n}\to\R$ of the form $\bphi(\x)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\phi_k(x_k)$, where $\phi_k:\R\to\R$, $k\in\mN$, are themselves Bregman generators. This class of generators includes the popular Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence and we refer to \citet{rankin} for a detailed discussion of the KL divergence and its connection to BW divergences. 

\begin{example}[KL Divergence]
{\parindent0pt Define 
\[S:=\Big\{\y\in\R^n~|~\y=\Big(\frac{e^{x_{1}}}{1+\sum_{i\in\mN}e^{x_i}}, \ldots, \frac{e^{x_{n}}}{1+\sum_{i\in\mN}e^{x_i}}\Big), \  \x\in\R^n\Big\}.\] Then, $\Delta^{n}:=\{\z=(z_0,\y)\in\R^{n+1}~|~\y\in S, \ z_0=1-\sum_{i\in\mN}y_i\}$ is the open unit simplex in $\R^{n+1}$.

If $\bphi(\z)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}z_k\log(z_k)$, then $\nabla\bphi(\z)=(\log(z_0)+1, \ldots, \log(z_n)+1)$ and thus
\[\DB^{n}_{\bphi}[\z_1,\z_2]=\sum_{i=0}^{n}z_{1i}\log\big(\frac{z_{1i}}{z_{2i}}\big),\]
which is the KL divergence on the simplex. Moreover, if we remove the restriction onto the simplex, then we recover the generalized KL divergence (\citet{miller}).  
}
\end{example}

For separable Bregman generators, using \Cref{prop:bregman}, we obtain an upper bound for subadditive risk functionals. Recall that a risk functional $\rho$ is subadditive if $\rho(X+Y)\leq\rho(X)+\rho(Y)$ for all rvs $X,Y$ for which the risk functional is well-defined. We note that the Mahalanobis distance is also defined by a separable Bregman generator, however, for the special case of Mahalanobis distance, we obtain a stronger result that is a generalization of \Cref{theorem:Lipschitz}.

\begin{theorem} [Separable Bregman generators]\label{theorem:bregman_sum}
Let $\phi_k:\R\to\R$, $k \in \mN$, be Bregman generators and define the Bregman generator $\bphi(\x)=\sum_{k\in\mN}\phi_k(x_k)$. Let $g:\R^{n}\to\R$ and $\rho$ be a law-invariant risk functional. Then, for any $\epsilon\geq 0$, 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bregman_upper_bound_1}
\sup_{\Z\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}\rho(g(\Z))\leq\sup_{\Z\in\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}\rho(g(\Z)).
\end{equation}
If moreover $\rho$ is subadditive, then 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bregman_upper_bound_2}
\sup_{\Z\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}\rho\Big(\sum_{i\in\mN}Z_i\Big)\leq\sum_{i\in\mN}\sup_{Z_i\in\mfB_{\phi_i, \epsilon}(X_i)}\rho(Z_i).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} \Cref{eq:bregman_upper_bound_1} follows from \Cref{prop:bregman}. For \Cref{eq:bregman_upper_bound_2}, using first \Cref{prop:bregman} and then subadditivity, we obtain
\[\sup_{\Z\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}\rho\Big(\sum_{i\in\mN}Z_i\Big) \leq \sup_{\Z\in\bmfB^\perp_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}\rho\Big(\sum_{i\in\mN}Z_i\Big)
\le\sum_{i\in\mN}\sup_{Z_i\in\mfB_{\phi_i, \epsilon}(X_i)}\rho(Z_i)\,.\]
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

Suppose the assumptions of \Cref{theorem:bregman_sum} are satisfied and consider a portfolio optimization setting where the portfolio wealth $\sum_{i\in\mN}\theta_iZ_i$, $\theta_i \ge 0$, is assessed by a subadditive and positive homogeneous risk functional $\rho$. A risk functional $\rho$ is positive homogeneous if $\rho(\theta X)=\theta\rho(X)$ for any $\theta\geq 0$. Then it holds for all $\theta_i \ge 0$, $i \in \mN$,
\begin{equation} \label{eq:pos_hom}
\sup_{\Z\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}\rho\Big(\sum_{i\in\mN}\theta_iZ_i\Big)\leq\sum_{i\in\mN}\theta_i\sup_{Z_i\in\mfB_{\phi_i, \epsilon}(X_i)}\rho(Z_i)\,.
\end{equation}
In other words, the worst-case aggregate risk of the portfolio is bounded by a weighted average of the worst-case risks for each asset in the portfolio. Moreover, the weights in the bound coincide with the portfolio weights. 

We note that the upper bound in \Cref{eq:bregman_upper_bound_1} is the supremum over random vectors $\Z$ such that each component of $\Z$ is ``close'' to the corresponding component of the reference random vector $\X$. Thus, there is no constraint on the dependence (copula) between different components of $\Z$, and the bound corresponds to the worst-case risk under marginal (component-wise) and complete dependence uncertainty. 

Next we compare the bound in \eqref{eq:Lipschitz_bound}, which corresponds to the Wasserstein uncertainty case, with the bound in \eqref{eq:bregman_upper_bound_2} corresponding to the BW uncertainty case for subadditive risk measures. The bound \eqref{eq:Lipschitz_bound} is the worst-case univariate risk $Y$ that is close to the reference aggregate risk $\mfg(\X)$. In contrast, the bound in \eqref{eq:bregman_upper_bound_2} is the sum of the component-wise worst-case risks. 
If we choose the Bregman generator $\bphi(\x)=\sum_{i\in\mN}x_i^2$, then the BW divergence coincides with the Wasserstein distance (of order 2) squared and we obtain equality of the following two uncertainty sets: $\bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X) =\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon^2}(\X) $. Thus, the two multivariate DRO problems are the same: 
\begin{equation*}
    \sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X)}\rho\big(g(\Y)\big)
    =
    \sup_{\Y\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon^2}(\X)}\rho\big(g(\Y)\big)\,.
\end{equation*}
We note that the uncertainty set $\bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X)$ is defined using the Wasserstein distance whereas the uncertainty set $\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon^2}(\X)$ is defined using the squared Wasserstein distance. Hence, the tolerance distances differ by a factor of $\epsilon$. 

If the aggregation function is linear, i.e. $\mfg(\x) = \sum_{k \in \mN}x_k$, and the risk measure is subadditive, then the bounds in  \eqref{eq:Lipschitz_bound} and \eqref{eq:bregman_upper_bound_2} imply that
\begin{equation*}
     \sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X)}\rho\big(\mfg(\Y)\big)
     =
     \sup_{Y\in \U_{\epsilon}(\mfg(\X))}\rho(Y)
     \le
     \sum_{i\in\mN}\; \sup_{Y_i\in\U_{\epsilon}(X_i)}\rho(Y_i)
    , 
\end{equation*}
since $\U_{\epsilon}(X) = \mfB_{x^2, \epsilon^2}(X_i)$. From this inequality, we see that the bound in \eqref{eq:Lipschitz_bound} is tighter than the bound in \eqref{eq:bregman_upper_bound_2}. In other words, the extra subadditivity assumption in \Cref{theorem:bregman_sum} does not improve the bound for linear aggregation functions in the Wasserstein case. However, \eqref{eq:bregman_upper_bound_2} applies more generally to all separable generators, whereas \eqref{eq:Lipschitz_bound} only holds for the special cases of the Wasserstein and the Mahalanobis distance.

\subsection{Composable Bregman Generators} \label{sec:composable}

Here, we consider Bregman generators $\bphi:\R^{n}\to\R$ of the form $\bphi(\x)=\phi(g(\x))$, where $g:\R^{n}\to\R$ and $\phi:\R\to\R$ are a Bregman generators and $\phi$ is non-decreasing. Non-decreasingness of $\phi$ (combined with convexity of $g$), ensures that $\bphi$ is convex.

Recall that for a DRO problem $\sup_{\X\in \U}\rho\big(g(\X)\big) $ with some uncertainty set $\U$ to be well-defined, the aggregation function $g\colon \R^n \to \R$, mapping input to output, is often convex and $\U$ is a convex set. Thus, composable Bregman generators allow us to tailor the uncertainty set to the aggregation function at hand. Indeed, for a univariate and non-decreasing Bregman generator $\phi$, the uncertainty set induced by the composable BW divergence, i.e., $\bmfB^{n}_{\phi\circ g, \epsilon}(\X)$, is quantified via the Bregman divergence as a composition of the aggregation function and $\phi$. Thus the aggregation function plays a pivotal role in defining the uncertainty set. 

For composable Bregman generators, we again bound the risk-aware DRO problem with multivariate BW uncertainty with a risk-aware DRO problem with univariate BW uncertainty. Depending on the choice law-invariant risk functional, the univariate DRO problem can be solved analytically. We refer to \Cref{sec:univ_bound} where we solve the univariate BW DRO problem for signed Choquet integrals.
\begin{theorem} [Composable Bregman generators]\label{theorem:bregman_upper_bound}
Let $\bphi:\R^{n}\to\R$ be a Bregman generator of the form $\bphi(\x)=\phi(g(\x))$, where $g:\R^{n}\to\R$, $\phi:\R\to\R$ are Bregman generators and $\phi$ is non-decreasing. Then, for any $\epsilon\geq 0$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:bregman_upper_bound_3}
\sup_{\Z\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}\rho(g(\Z))\leq\sup_{Z\in\mfB_{\phi, \epsilon}(g(\X))}\rho(Z).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} It suffices to show that $\Z\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$ implies that $ g(\Z)\in\mfB_{\phi, \epsilon}(g(\X))$. For any random vector $\Z$,
\begin{align*}
\DB^{n}_{\bphi}[F_{\Z},F_{\X}] &= \inf_{\Z'\eqd\Z, \X'\eqd\X}\E[\bphi(\Z')-\bphi(\X')-\nabla\bphi(\X')\cdot(\Z'-\X')] \\
&= \inf_{\Z'\eqd\Z, \X'\eqd\X}\E[\phi(g(\Z'))-\phi(g(\X'))-\phi'(g(\X'))\nabla g(\X')\cdot(\Z'-\X')] \\
&= \inf_{\Z'\eqd\Z, \X'\eqd\X}\E\big[\phi(g(\Z'))-\phi(g(\X'))-\phi'(g(\X'))\nabla g(\X')\cdot(\Z'-\X') \\
&\qquad\qquad\qquad\quad -\phi'(g(\X'))(g(\Z')-g(\X'))+\phi'(g(\X'))(g(\Z')-g(\X'))\big] \\
&= \inf_{\Z'\eqd\Z, \X'\eqd\X}\E[B_{\phi}(g(\Z'), g(\X'))+\phi'(g(\X'))B_g(\Z',\X')] \\
&\geq \DB_{\phi}[F_{g(\Z)}, F_{g(\X)}]+\inf_{\Z'\eqd\Z, \X'\eqd\X}\E[\phi'(g(\X'))B_g(\Z',\X')] \\
&\geq \DB_{\phi}[F_{g(\Z)}, F_{g(\X)}], 
\end{align*}
where the last inequality follows from the fact that $\phi'(g(\X'))$ is non-negative (since $\phi$ is non-decreasing) and the Bregman divergence is non negative. 

\smallskip

Thus, for any $\Z\in\bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)$, we have that $\DB_{\phi}[F_{g(\Z)}, F_{g(\X)}]\leq\DB^{n}_{\bphi}[F_{\Z},F_{\X}]\leq\epsilon$. 
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

\section{Uncertainty in the Aggregation Function} \label{sec:max_sliced}

While in some settings the aggregation function $g$ is exogenously given, e.g., in portfolio optimization or contracts such as in (re)insurance, and is thus not subject to uncertainty, in other cases, e.g., when the aggregation function is estimated via statistical or machine learning methods, it is part of the model ambiguity. Here, we propose a novel way to account for both uncertainty in the aggregation function $g$ and uncertainty in the risk factor $\X$ via the max-sliced Wasserstein distance and its generalization proposed here. 

To introduce the concept, we first focus on linear aggregation functions of the form $\mfg(\x)=\bgamma^T\x$ for some $\bgamma\in\R^n$. This is for example of interest when the aggregation function is estimated using linear regression. To account for uncertainty in $\mfg$, we define uncertainty sets using a max-sliced Wasserstein distance constraint instead of the usual Wasserstein constraint. The max-sliced Wasserstein distance is the largest univariate Wasserstein distance between (linear) projections of the multivariate cdfs onto the space of univariate cdfs. We next present a formal definition of the max-sliced Wasserstein distance. 

\begin{definition} [Max-Sliced Wasserstein Distance] \label{definition:max_sliced} Let $F_{\X},G_{\Y}\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$. Then, the ($p$)-max-sliced Wasserstein distance between $F_{\X}$ and $G_{\Y}$ is given by
\begin{equation} \label{eq:max_sliced}
\mW_{p}(F_{\X},G_{\Y}):=\sup_{\bgamma\in \R^n}W_{p}(F_{\bgamma^T\X}, G_{\bgamma^T\Y}).
\end{equation}
Similarly to earlier sections, we omit the power $p$ whenever it is clear from the context. 
\end{definition}

Wasserstein distances between projections of high-dimensional random vectors onto lower dimensional spaces are a popular topic of interest in the recent statistical literature. \citet{deshpande, paty, kolouri, lin} work with projections in the space of cdfs using Radon transforms. \citet{olea} gives a definition similar to ours, but their work focuses on minimizing prediction errors in regression problems. We first verify that \Cref{eq:max_sliced} defines a distance.

\begin{lemma} \label{lemma:distance} The max-sliced Wasserstein distance is a distance on $\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$. 
\end{lemma}
{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} See Appendix \ref{appendix:a}.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

\begin{definition} [Max-Sliced Wasserstein Uncertainty Set] \label{definition:max_sliced_uncertainty} Let $F_{\X}\in\mathcal{M}_p(R^n)$ be a reference cdf. For $\epsilon\geq 0$, the multivariate max-sliced Wasserstein uncertainty set is given by 
\begin{align} \label{eq:max_sliced_uncertainty}
\hM_{\epsilon}(F_{\X})
:&=\{F_{\Z} \in \mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)~|~\mW(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})\leq\epsilon\}
\\
\nonumber
&=\big\{F_{\Z} \in \mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)~|~\sup_{\bgamma\in \R^n}W(F_{\bgamma^T\Z}, F_{\bgamma^T\X})\leq\epsilon\big\}\,.
\end{align}
The corresponding set for random vectors is given by
\begin{equation} \label{eq:max_sliced_uncertainty_rv}
\hU_{\epsilon}(\X):=\{\Z \in \L^p_n~|~F_{\Z}\in\hM_{\epsilon}(F_{\X})\}.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}
Thus the set $\hU_{\epsilon}(\X)$ contains all random vectors $\Z$ such that Wasserstein distance between any linear projection of $\Z$ and $\X$ is not larger than $\ep$. 

For a fixed reference or baseline aggregation vector $\bgamma_0\in\R^n$ (e.g., estimated from data), we work with the set $\bgamma_{0}\big(\hM_{\epsilon}(F_{\X})\big):=\{F_{\bgamma_0^T\Z}~|~F_{\Z}\in\hM_{\epsilon}(F_{\X})\}$. This corresponds to the set of all univariate cdfs $F_{\bgamma_0^T\Z}$ such that the aggregate risks $\bgamma^T\Z$ and $\bgamma^T\X$ are close in the Wasserstein distance for any linear aggregation vector $\bgamma$. Clearly, we have that $\bgamma_{0}\big(\hM_{\epsilon}(F_{\X})\big)\subseteq\M_{\epsilon}(F_{\bgamma_0^T\X})$ since the latter set consists of all univariate cdfs that have a Wasserstein distance of at most $\ep$ to $\bgamma_0^T\X$, with $\bgamma_0$ fixed. The following proposition follows from this observation. 

\begin{proposition} [Max-sliced Wasserstein distance]\label{prop:Lipschitz_max}
Let $\bgamma_0\in\R^n$, $\rho$ be a law-invariant risk functional and $\X\in\L^p_n$. Then, for any $\epsilon\geq 0$, 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Lipschitz_bound_max} 
\sup_{Y\in \bgamma_0(\hU_{\epsilon}(\X))}\rho(Y)\leq
\sup_{Y\in\U_{\epsilon}(\bgamma_0^T\X)}\rho(Y), 
\end{equation}
where $\bgamma_0\big(\hU_{\epsilon}(\X)\big)=\{Y\in\L^p~|~F_{Y}\in \bgamma_{0}\big(\hM_{\epsilon}(F_{\X})\big)\}$.
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} This follows from law-invariance and the fact that $\bgamma_{0}\big(\hM_{\epsilon}(F_{\X})\big)\subseteq\M_{\epsilon}(F_{\bgamma_0^T\X})$.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

On the right-hand side of \Cref{eq:Lipschitz_bound_max}, all of the uncertainty is around the reference aggregate rv $\bgamma_0^T\X$, and the uncertainty set contains any rv that is close in the Wasserstein distance to $\bgamma_0^T\X$. On the left-hand side of the inequality, the uncertainty is "shared'" between the risk factors $\X$ and the aggregation function, in that only random vectors $\Z$ whose linear projections are all close to the reference aggregate rv lie in the uncertainty set. As the latter is more restrictive, the upper bound in \Cref{eq:Lipschitz_bound_max} thus accounts not only for uncertainty in the aggregate but also jointly uncertainty in the risk factor and the aggregation function. 

To obtain a more conservative bound, one could take the supremum over a set of alternative linear aggregation functions $\Gamma$. It follows immediately from \Cref{prop:Lipschitz_max} that 
\begin{equation*}
    \sup_{\bgamma_0\in\R^n}\left(\sup_{Y\in \bgamma_0(\hU_{\epsilon}(\X))}\rho(Y)\right)
    \leq
    \sup_{\bgamma_0\in\R^n}\left(\sup_{Y\in\U_{\epsilon}(\bgamma_0^T\X)}\rho(Y)\right)\,.
\end{equation*} 
However, there are two limitations with this upper bound. First, it is difficult to compute, and second, the aggregation functions are restricted to the set of linear projections. We address both of these limitations by introducing a generalization of the max-sliced Wasserstein distance that allows for non-linear projections. For the rest of this section, we let $\mathcal{G} \subseteq \{h \colon \R^n\to\R\}$ denote a subset of functions mapping $\R^n $ to the reals. Furthermore, we write $\L^p_{\mathcal{G}}$ to denote the space of random vectors satisfying $g(\X),\ g(\Y)\in\L^p$ for all $g\in\mathcal{G}$ and $\mathcal{M}^p_{\mathcal{G}}$ the corresponding set of cdfs. 

\begin{definition} [$\mathcal{G}$-Max-Sliced Wasserstein Distance] \label{definition:gen_max_sliced} Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a set of functions. The $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distance between $F_{\X}, G_{\Y}\in\mathcal{M}^p_{\mathcal{G}}$ is given by
\begin{equation} \label{eq:gen_max_sliced}
\mW_{p}^{\mathcal{G}}(F_{\X},G_{\Y}):=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\;W_{p}\big(F_{g(\X)}, G_{g(\Y)}\big)\,.
\end{equation}
Again, we omit the power $p$, whenever it is clear from the context. 
\end{definition}

The $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distance is always a pseudo-distance on $\mathcal{M}^p_{\mathcal{G}}$, but it is not always a distance. For example, when $\mathcal{G}$ is the trivial subspace consisting only of the zero function, then $\mW_{p}^{\mathcal{G}}(F_{\X},G_{\Y})=0$ for all distributions $F_{\X}$ and $F_{\Y}$. Thus, more conditions on $\mathcal{G}$ are necessary to guarantee that the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distance is a distance. We state this formally in the next lemma. 

\begin{lemma} \label{lemma:gen_distance} Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a set of functions. Then, the following holds:
\begin{enumerate} [label = $\roman*)$]
\item $\mW_{p}^{\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,\cdot)$ is a pseudo-distance on $\mathcal{M}^p_{\mathcal{G}}$.
\item The following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[label = $(\alph*)$]
\item $\mW_{p}^{\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,\cdot)$ is a distance on $\mathcal{M}^p_{\mathcal{G}}$.
\item $F_{g(\X)}=F_{g(\Y)}$ for all $g\in\mathcal{G}$ if and only if $F_{\X}=F_{\Y}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} See Appendix \ref{appendix:a}.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

Using the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distance, we next define uncertainty sets characterized by the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distance and obtain bounds for non-linear aggregation functions, akin to \Cref{prop:Lipschitz_max}.

\begin{definition} [$\mathcal{G}$-Max-Sliced Wasserstein Uncertainty Set] \label{definition:gen_max_sliced_uncertainty} Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a set of functions and $\X\in\L^p_{\mathcal{G}}$. For $\epsilon\geq 0$, the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein uncertainty set associated with $\mathcal{G}$ is given by 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:gen_max_sliced_uncertainty}
\hM_{\mathcal{G}, \epsilon}(F_{\X}):=\big\{F_{\Z}~|~\mW_{p}^{\mathcal{G}}(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})\leq\epsilon, \ \Z\in\L^p_{\mathcal{G}}\big\}\,.
\end{equation}
The corresponding set for random vectors is 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:gen_max_sliced_uncertainty_rv}
\hU_{\mathcal{G}, \epsilon}(\X):=\big\{\Z\in \L^p_{\mathcal{G}}~|~F_{\Z}\in\hM_{\epsilon}(F_{\X})\big\}\, .
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

With these uncertainty sets, we next derive bounds when the uncertainty is characterized by the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distance.
\begin{proposition} [$\mathcal{G}$-Max-Sliced Wasserstein distance]\label{prop:Lipschitz_max_gen}
Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a set of functions, $g_0\in\mathcal{G}$ a reference aggregation function, and $\X\in\L^p_{\mathcal{G}}$. Then, for a law-invariant risk functional $\rho$ and any $\epsilon\geq 0$, the following holds:

\begin{enumerate} [label = $\roman*)$]
\item \begin{equation} \label{eq:Lipschitz_bound_gen_max} 
\sup_{Y\in g_0(\hU_{\mathcal{G}, \epsilon}(\X))}\rho(Y)\leq
\sup_{Y\in\U_{\epsilon}(g_0(\X))}\rho(Y), 
\end{equation}
\item \begin{equation} \label{eq:Lipschitz_bound_gen_max_2} 
\sup_{g_0\in\mathcal{G}}\left(\sup_{Y\in g_0(\hU_{\mathcal{G}, \epsilon}(\X))}\rho(Y)\right)\leq
\sup_{g_0\in\mathcal{G}}\left(\sup_{Y\in\U_{\epsilon}(g_0(\X))}\rho(Y)\right), 
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} The proof of $i)$ uses similar arguments as the proof of \Cref{prop:Lipschitz_max}. The second statement $ii)$ follows directly from part $i)$. 
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

Using the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distance allows for greater flexibility in the uncertainty of the aggregation function than the max-sliced Wasserstein distance. Furthermore, if we only consider a finite number of alternative aggregation functions, then the outer suprema in \Cref{eq:Lipschitz_bound_gen_max_2} become maxima and are computable whenever the inner suprema are. 

\begin{remark}
We can also define the max-sliced BW divergence in an analogous manner. The $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced BW divergence is given by
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\DB}_{\phi}^{\mathcal{G}}[F_{\X}, F_{\Y}]:=\sup_{g\in\mathcal{G}}\DB_{\phi}[F_{g(\X)}, F_{g(\Y)}]
\end{equation} and the corresponding uncertainty set is $\widehat{\mfB}_{\mathcal{G}, \phi, \epsilon}(\X):=\{\Z~|~\widehat{\DB}_{\phi}^{\mathcal{G}}[F_{\Z}, F_{\X}]\leq\epsilon\}$. Note that the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced BW divergence is always a pseudo-divergence on $\L^p_{\mathcal{G}}$, but not necessarily a divergence for all $\mathcal{G}$. This can be shown with a similar argument to the one used in the proof of \Cref{lemma:gen_distance}. Furthermore, \Cref{prop:Lipschitz_max_gen} holds for the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced BW divergence by replacing $g_0(\hU_{\mathcal{G}, \epsilon}(\X))$ with $g_0(\widehat{\mfB}_{\mathcal{G}, \phi, \epsilon}(\X))$ and $\U_{\epsilon}(g_0(\X))$ with $\mfB_{\phi, \epsilon}(g_0(\X))$. 
\end{remark}

\section{Explicit Bounds for Signed Choquet Integrals} \label{sec:application}

\smallskip

Here, we derive explicit upper bounds for the worst-case risk when the risk functional belongs to the class of signed Choquet integrals and the uncertainty is quantified using (a) univariate BW uncertainty sets and (b) BW uncertainty around multivariate risk factors. We recall the definition of a signed Choquet integral, which was first introduced by \citet{choquet}. 

\begin{definition} [Signed Choquet Integrals] \label{definition:choquet} A signed Choquet integral, denoted $I_h$, is a mapping from $L^{p}$ to $\mathbb{R}$ given by 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:choquet}
I_h(X)=\int_{-\infty}^{0}\big[h(\mathbb{P}(X\geq x))-h(1)\big]\, dx+\int_{0}^{\infty}h(\mathbb{P}(X\geq x))\, dx,
\end{equation}
where $h: [0,1]\to\R$ has bounded variation and satisfies $h(0)=0$. The function $h$ is called the distortion function of $I_h$ and we denote the set of distortion functions by $\mathcal{H}$. 
\end{definition}

Note that the integrals in \Cref{eq:choquet} may be infinite, in which case $I_h(X)$ may not be well-defined. In this paper, we assume that $X$ is such that $I_h(X)$ is finite and thus well-defined. We refer to \citet{wang} for an extensive discussion on signed Choquet integrals. When the distortion function $h$ is non-decreasing and satisfies $h(1)=1$, then $I_h(X)$ is a distortion risk measure. The class of distortion risk measures includes many commonly used risk measures, including the Value-at-Risk (VaR) and the ES. However, several important risk measures such as the Interquartile Range (IQR), Mean-Median Difference and Gini Deviation belong to the class of signed Choquet integrals but are not distortion risk measures. Furthermore, inverse S-shaped distortions, which are popular in economics (\citet{tversky}), belong to the class of signed Choquet integrals. 

If the distortion function $h$
is absolutely continuous, then by Lemma 3 in \citet{wang}, the signed Choquet integral has representation
\begin{equation} \label{eq:quantile_rep}
I_h(X)=\int_{0}^{1}\gamma (u)F_X^{-1}(u)du\,,
\end{equation}
where $\gamma : [0,1]\to \R$ is called a distortion weight function and defined by $\gamma(u) := \frac{d^-}{dx} h(x)|_{x = 1-u}$, where $\frac{d^-}{dx}$ denotes the left derivative. We assume throughout the exposition that a signed Choquet integral satisfies representation \eqref{eq:quantile_rep}. Moreover, as signed Choquet integrals are law-invariant, we use the notation $\tilde{I}_h(G):=I_h(X)$ whenever $X$ has cdf $G$. 

For distortion risk measures, the  distortion weight function $\gamma$ is non-negative (since $h$ is non-decreasing) and $\int_{0}^{1}\gamma (u)du=h(1)-h(0)=1$ (since $h(1) = 1)$, thus $\gamma$ is a density on $[0,1]$.

\subsection{Worst-case Quantile Function for Univariate Risks} \label{sec:univ_bound}

We first consider the largest signed Choquet integral under univariate BW uncertainty 
\begin{equation} \label{opt:WC-RMtoto}
\max_{G\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R)}\tilde{I}_h(G), \quad \text{subject to} \quad \DB_\phi (G^{-1}, F^{-1})\leq\epsilon\,,
\end{equation} 
where for the univariate BW divergence, we write $\DB_{\phi}(F^{-1}_1, F^{-1}_2)=\DB_{\phi} [F_1, F_2]$. That is, we use round brackets for quantile functions and square brackets for cdfs. We choose to state the constraint in terms of quantile functions instead of cdfs to emphasize that the optimization problem is convex when considered over the space of quantile functions. The power $p$ of $\mathcal{M}_p(\R)$ must be chosen such that the divergence constraint is well-defined. For example, if $\DB_\phi (G^{-1}, F^{-1})$ is the squared $2$-Wasserstein distance, then $p=2$. We call the quantile function that attains the maximum the worst-case quantile function. 
For $\phi(x)=x^2$, optimization problem \eqref{opt:WC-RMtoto} coincides with the bound given by \Cref{prop:Lipschitz}. For a separable Bregman generator $\bphi(\x)=\sum_{k\in\mN}\phi_k(x_k)$, we can compute the bound given in \Cref{theorem:bregman_sum} by solving the optimization problem \eqref{opt:WC-RMtoto} for each $\phi_k$ separately. 

\citet{pesenti} study in Section 4.1 the optimization problem \eqref{opt:WC-RMtoto} for non-decreasing and strictly concave distortion functions $h$. In the next theorem, we generalize this in two directions. First, we consider concave $h$ that are not necessarily non-decreasing --- corresponding to the class of subadditive signed Choquet integrals. Second, we consider signed Choquet integrals with non-concave $h$ and non-negative distortion weight function $\gamma$ --- corresponding to monotone signed Choquet integrals. In both cases, we assume that $h$ is not the zero function. 

In order to solve optimization problem \eqref{opt:WC-RMtoto} for non-concave $h$, we introduce the isotonic projection, which is the continuous analogue to the isotonic regression; see, e.g., \citet{barlow, barlow2, brunk}. The use of isotonic projections to solve problems related to distortion risk measures can be found in \citet{pesenti2} and \citet{bernard}. For this we write $\L^2((0,1)):=\{l \colon (0,1) \to \R ~|~ \int_0^1 l^2(u) \, du < \infty\}$ to denote the set of all square integrable functions defined on $(0,1)$. Moreover, for any $l\in\L^2((0,1))$, we write $\norm{l}_2:=\sqrt{\int_{0}^{1}l^2(u)du}$ for the $\mathcal{L}^2$ norm of the function $l$.

\begin{definition} [Isotonic Projection] \label{definition:isotonic_proj} The isotonic projection of a function $l\in\L^2((0,1))$, denoted $\iso{l}$, is given by
\begin{equation} \label{eq:iso}
\iso{l}:=\argmin_{j\in\K}\int_{0}^{1}\big(j(u)-l(u)\big)^2du,
\end{equation}
where $\K\subseteq \mathcal{L}^2((0,1))$ is the set of all left-continuous and non-decreasing square integrable functions defined on $(0,1)$. 
\end{definition}

Note that $\K$ is the set of quantile functions. Furthermore, isotonic projections preserve the ordering of functions in $\L^2((0,1))$, discussed next. 

\begin{proposition} [Ordering property]\label{prop:iso_ordering}
Let $l_1, l_2\in\L^2((0,1))$. If $l_2(s)\leq l_1(s)$ for all $s\in (0,1)$, then $\iso{l_2}(s)\leq\iso{l_1}(s)$. This result also holds when the inequalities are replaced with strict inequalities. 
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} See Appendix \ref{appendix:b}.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

\begin{theorem} [Worst-case quantile function]\label{theorem:wc_signed_choquet_int} 
Let $I_h$ be a signed Choquet integral with absolutely continuous distortion function $h$ and distortion weight function $\gamma$. Assume $\phi$ is strictly convex and differentiable with $\lim_{x\to\infty}\phi'(x)=\infty$ and $\lim_{x\to -\infty}\phi'(x)=-\infty$. Assume at least one of the following holds:

\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]
    \item $\gamma$ is non-decreasing
    \item $\gamma$ is non-negative and $\int_0^1\phi'\big(F^{-1}(u)\big)^2   + \gamma(u)^2 d u< \infty$.
\end{enumerate}
Then, the worst-case quantile function of \eqref{opt:WC-RMtoto} is uniquely given by
        \begin{equation}\label{eq:solution1}
        G^{-1}_{\lambda^*}(u)
        := 
        \left(\phi'\right)^{-1}
        \left(\left(
        \phi'\big(F^{-1}(u)\big) + \frac{1}{\lambda^*}\gamma(u)
        \right)^{\uparrow}\right)
        \,,
        \end{equation}
    where $\lambda^{*}>0$ is the smallest solution to $\DB_\phi\big(G_{\lambda}^{-1}, \, F^{-1}\big) = \epsilon$. 
\end{theorem}
We note that in case $i)$, the function $\phi'\big(F^{-1}(u)\big) + \frac{1}{\lambda^*}\gamma(u)$ is non-decreasing and equal to its isotonic projection.


{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:}
To see $i)$, we first show that the solution (if it exists) is of the form \eqref{eq:solution1}. Let the worst-case quantile function have a BW constraint of $\ep_0 \in [0, \ep]$. Then the Lagrangian for the constrained optimization problem \eqref{opt:WC-RMtoto} is 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:Lagrangian}
\mathcal{L}(G^{-1}, \lambda):=\int_{0}^{1}-G^{-1}(u)\gamma(u)du+\lambda [\DB_\phi\big(G^{-1}, \, F^{-1}\big)-\epsilon_0]\,,
\end{equation}
where $\lambda>0$ is the Lagrange parameter such that the BW divergence from the worst-case to the reference quantile is $\epsilon_0$. Thus, 
\[\mathcal{L}(G^{-1}, \lambda)=\int_{0}^{1}-G^{-1}(u)\gamma(u)+\lambda\, \Big[\phi(G^{-1}(u))-\phi'(F^{-1}(u))G^{-1}(u)\Big]du+k(\lambda),
\]
where $k(\lambda)$ is a function that does not depend on $G^{-1}$.

\smallskip

It suffices to find a point-wise minimum (in $u$) of $S(x):=-x\gamma(u)+\lambda[\phi(x)-\phi'(F^{-1}(u))x]$ and verify that it is a quantile function. By direct computation, we have $S'(x)=0$ if and only if
\[ x=(\phi')^{-1}\left(\phi'(F^{-1}(u))+\frac{1}{\lambda}\gamma(u)\right).\]
Since $\gamma$ is non-decreasing, $F^{-1}(u)$ is non-decreasing, and $\phi'$ is strictly increasing, the function $\tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda}(u):=(\phi')^{-1}(\phi'(F^{-1}(u))+\frac{1}{\lambda}\gamma(u))$ is a non-decreasing function of $u$. Further note that $\tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda}(u)$ is of the form \eqref{eq:solution1}, since the isotonic projection of a non-decreasing function is the function itself. 

\smallskip

Next, we prove existence of the Lagrange parameter. For this we first show that the signed Choquet integral is strictly decreasing with respect to the Lagrange parameter, i.e., $0<\lambda_1<\lambda_2 $ implies that $I_h(\tilde{G}_{\lambda_1})>I_h(\tilde{G}_{\lambda_2})$. Note that $0<\lambda_1<\lambda_2$ implies that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:G}
\begin{cases} \tilde{G}_{\lambda_1}^{-1}(u)> \tilde{G}_{\lambda_2}^{-1}(u) & \text{if} \quad \gamma(u)> 0 \\[0.5em] \tilde{G}_{\lambda_1}^{-1}(u)< \tilde{G}_{\lambda_2}^{-1}(u) & \text{if} \quad \gamma(u)<0 \,.
\end{cases} 
\end{equation}
Since $\gamma$ is non-decreasing, there exists $y\in [0,1]$ such that $\gamma(u)<0$ on $[0,y)$ and $\gamma(u)\geq 0$ on $(y,1]$. Therefore, the signed Choquet integral becomes
\[I_h(\tilde{G}_{\lambda})=\int_{0}^{y}\gamma(u)\tilde{G}_{\lambda}^{-1}(u)du+\int_{y}^{1}\gamma(u)\tilde{G}_{\lambda}^{-1}(u)du.\]
It follows from \Cref{eq:G} that $I_h(\tilde{G}_{\lambda})$ is a strictly decreasing function of $\lambda$. Thus, the optimal Lagrange parameter is the smallest $\lambda>0$ such that the constraint is satisfied. Finally, we show existence of the Lagrange parameter. As $\phi$ is differentiable and strictly convex, it is continuously differentiable and therefore $\DB_\phi\big(\tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda}, \, F^{-1}\big)$ is continuous in $\lambda$. Furthermore, $\lim_{\lambda\to 0}\DB_\phi\big(\tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda}, \, F^{-1}\big)=\infty$ (since $\phi'$ is unbounded) and $\lim_{\lambda\to \infty}\DB_\phi\big(\tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda}, \, F^{-1}\big)=0$. Thus, a unique smallest solution to $\DB_\phi\big(\tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda}, \, F^{-1}\big) = \epsilon$ exists for any $\epsilon>0$.

\smallskip

For $ii)$ the Lagrangian is the same as in $i)$, that is
\[\mathcal{L}(G^{-1}, \lambda)=\lambda\int_{0}^{1}\phi\left(G^{-1}(u)\right)-\Big(\frac{1}{\lambda}\gamma(u)+\phi'\left(F^{-1}(u)\right)\Big)G^{-1}(u)du+k(\lambda),\]
where $k(\lambda)$ is a function that does not depend on $G^{-1}$.
Since $\phi$ is strictly convex, by \citet{barlow} Theorem 3.1, the argmin of the Lagrangian over the set of quantile functions $\K$ is uniquely attained at $\tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda}(u)=\left(\phi'\right)^{-1}\left(\left(\phi'\big(F^{-1}(u)\big)+\frac{1}{\lambda}\gamma(u)\right)^{\uparrow}\right)$. 

\smallskip

Next, we show that the signed Choquet integral is decreasing in $\lambda$. Let $0<\lambda_1<\lambda_2$. Since $\gamma$ is non-negative, by \Cref{prop:iso_ordering}, 
\begin{equation*}
    \tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda_1}(u)=\left(\phi'\right)^{-1}\Big(\Big(\phi'\big(F^{-1}(u)\big)+\frac{1}{\lambda_1}\gamma(u)\Big)^{\uparrow}\;\Big)
    < \left(\phi'\right)^{-1}\Big(\Big(\phi'\big(F^{-1}(u)\big)+\frac{1}{\lambda_2}\gamma(u)\Big)^{\uparrow}\;\Big)=\tilde{G}^{-1}_{\lambda_2}(u),
\end{equation*}
where the strict inequality holds for any $u\in(0,1)$ such that $\gamma(u)\neq 0$. 

Thus, $\tilde{I}_h(\tilde{G}_{\lambda})$ is a strictly decreasing function of $\lambda$ and the optimal Lagrange parameter is the smallest $\lambda>0$ such that the constraint is satisfied. Existence of a unique Lagrange parameter follows by the same arguments as in $i)$. 
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

\subsection{Explicit Upper Bounds for Multivariate Risks} \label{sec:mult_bound}

In this subsection, we derive explicit upper bounds for multivariate risks under three different settings. First for the multivariate Wasserstein setup (\Cref{prop:Lipschitz}), second for the Mahalanobis distance case (\Cref{cor:m_distance}), and third, for the max-sliced Wasserstein cases discussed in \Cref{sec:max_sliced}.

The first result generalizes Theorem 5 from \citet{kuhn}, who consider the expected value as the risk measure. 


\begin{proposition}[Multivariate Wasserstein distance]\label{prop:wc_signed_choquet_int}
Let $I_h$ be a signed Choquet integral with absolutely continuous distortion function $h$ and distortion weight function $\gamma$ satisfying $0< \norm{\gamma}_2< \infty$. Let $g:\R^n\to\R$ be $L$-Lipschitz wrt the $\L^a$ norm and $\X\in\L^2_n$ with $g(\X)\in\L^2$. Fix $\epsilon>0$. 
\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]
\item If $\gamma$ non-decreasing, then 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:wc_bound} \sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X)}I_h\big(g(\Y)\big)\leq I_h(g(\X))+L\epsilon\norm{\gamma}_2. 
\end{equation}
\item If $\gamma$ is non-negative, then 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:wc_bound_2} \sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X)}I_h\big(g(\Y)\big)\leq\int_{0}^{1}\gamma(u)\iso{\Big(F_{g(\X)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda^{*}}\gamma(u)\Big)}du, 
\end{equation}
where $\lambda^{*}$ is the smallest positive solution to 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:constraint}
 \int_0^1 \left(F_{g(\X)}^{-1}(u) - \iso{\Big(F_{g(\X)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda^{*}}\gamma(u)\Big)}\right)^2 du = L^2 \, \ep^2  \,.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} For $i)$ we apply \Cref{prop:Lipschitz}, $i)$ to obtain
\begin{equation}\label{eq:proof-bound-multi-wasser}
    \sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X)}I_h\big(g(\Y)\big)\leq
  \sup_{Y\in \U_{L\epsilon}(g(\X))}\rho(Y)\,.  
\end{equation}
Next, by part one of \Cref{theorem:wc_signed_choquet_int} (with $\phi(x)=x^2$), the quantile function attaining the bound in \eqref{eq:proof-bound-multi-wasser} is of the form \eqref{eq:solution1}. 

Plugging in $\phi(x)=x^2$ and $F^{-1}(u)=F^{-1}_{g(\X)}(u)$ into \eqref{eq:solution1} yields 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:quantile1}
G_{\lambda}^{-1}(u)=F_{g(\X)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda}\gamma(u).
\end{equation}

By \Cref{theorem:wc_signed_choquet_int}, the optimal $\lambda\ge 0$ is the smallest positive solution to 
\begin{equation*}
   W(F_{g(\X)}, G_{\lambda})^2
    =
    \frac{1}{4\lambda^2}\int_0^1 \gamma(u)^2 \, du = L^2  \, \ep^2.
\end{equation*}

Solving for the positive root of $\lambda$, we obtain that $\lambda^{*}=\frac{\norm{\gamma}_2}{2L\epsilon}>0$.

Plugging $\lambda^{*}$ into \Cref{eq:quantile1} we obtain
\begin{align*}
    \sup_{Y\in \U_{L\epsilon}(g(\X))}\rho(Y)
    =
    \tilde{I}_h\big(G_{\lambda^*}\big)
    =
    I_h\big(g(\X)\big) + L\, \ep\norm{\gamma}_2\,.
\end{align*}

\smallskip

For $ii)$, similarly to part $i)$, we apply \Cref{prop:Lipschitz}, $i)$ to obtain
\begin{equation}\label{eq:proof-bound-multi-wasser-2}
    \sup_{\Y\in \bU^{n}_{\epsilon}(\X)}I_h\big(g(\Y)\big)\leq
  \sup_{Y\in \U_{L\epsilon}(g(\X))}\rho(Y)\,.  
\end{equation}
As $g(\X)\in\L^2$ and $\gamma $ is square integrable, the integrability assumption of  \Cref{theorem:wc_signed_choquet_int} part $ii)$ holds. Thus, by \Cref{theorem:wc_signed_choquet_int} part $ii)$, the worst-case quantile function of the upper bound in \eqref{eq:proof-bound-multi-wasser-2} is 
\begin{align*} 
G_{\lambda}^{-1}(u) &=\frac{1}{2}\iso{\big(2F_{g(\X)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{1}{\lambda}\gamma(u)\big)} =\iso{\big(F_{g(\X)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda}\gamma(u)\big)},
\end{align*}
where the second equality follows from  \Cref{prop:iso_properties}, part $i)$, see Appendix \ref{appendix:b}. Again by \Cref{theorem:wc_signed_choquet_int} we obtain the equation for the Lagrange multiplier $\lambda^*$. Calculating the signed Choquet integral with the worst-case quantile function $G_{\lambda^*}^{-1}$ yields \Cref{eq:wc_bound_2}.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

From the above proposition we see that if $\gamma$ is non-decreasing, then the supremum is bounded by the signed Choquet integral of the reference distribution plus a positive penalty term. The penalty term depends on the Lipschitz constant of the aggregation function, the size of the uncertainty, and the risk measure. Thus, enlarging the uncertainty set increases the bound. Indeed, the bound interpolates between the signed Choquet integral of the reference distribution ($\epsilon=0$) and infinity ($\ep = \infty)$. The bounds in \Cref{prop:wc_signed_choquet_int} also hold for $g$ locally Lipschitz as long as $\X$ satisfies the conditions of \Cref{prop:loc_lip}. Moreover, if $g$ is linear in at least one component, then the inequalities in \Cref{prop:wc_signed_choquet_int} become equalities. 

Next, we consider bounds for the Mahalanobis distance, which is an example of a BW divergence.

\begin{proposition}[Mahalanobis distance] \label{prop:wc_signed_choquet_int_m_dist}
Let $I_h$ be a signed Choquet integral with absolutely continuous distortion function $h$ and distortion weight function $\gamma$ satisfying $0< \norm{\gamma}_2< \infty$. Consider $\bphi(\x)=\x^TQ\x$, where $Q$ is a positive definite diagonal $n\times n$ matrix with smallest eigenvalue $q>0$. Further let $g:\R^n\to\R$ be $L$-Lipschitz wrt the $\L^a$ norm and $\X\in\L^2_n$ with $g(\X)\in\L^2$. Fix $\epsilon>0$. 
\begin{enumerate}[label = $\roman*)$]
\item If $\gamma$ is non-decreasing, then 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:wc_bound_3} \sup_{\Y\in \bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}I_h\big(g(\Y)\big)\leq I_h(g(\X))+\sqrt{\tfrac{L\ep}{q}}\norm{\gamma}_2. 
\end{equation}
\item If $\gamma$ is non-negative, then 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:wc_bound_4} \sup_{\Y\in \bmfB^{n}_{\bphi, \epsilon}(\X)}I_h\big(g(\Y)\big)\leq\int_{0}^{1}\gamma(u)\iso{\Big(F_{g(x)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{1}{2q\lambda^{*}}\gamma(u)\Big)}du, 
\end{equation}
where $\lambda^{*}$ is the smallest positive solution to the constraint equation 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:constraint3}
\int_{0}^{1}\Big(F_{g(\X)}^{-1}(u)-\iso{\big(F_{g(x)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{1}{2q\lambda^{*}}\gamma(u)\big)}\Big)^2\,du=\tfrac{L\epsilon}{q}\,.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} The proof is omitted as it follows similar steps as the proof of \Cref{prop:wc_signed_choquet_int}, with the difference that we apply \Cref{theorem:m_distance} instead of \Cref{prop:Lipschitz} in the first step.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

The bounds for signed Choquet integrals with uncertainty sets characterized by the squared Mahalanobis distance take a similar form than those with the Wasserstein distance. In the case when $\gamma$ is non-decreasing, the penalty term is proportional to $\sqrt{\frac{L\epsilon}{q}}$ (Mahalanobis distance) rather than $L\epsilon$ (Wasserstein distance). This is because the Mahalanobis uncertainty set is defined using the squared Mahalanobis distance. 

Next, we consider bounds for signed Choquet integrals when the uncertainty sets are characterized by the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distances introduced in \Cref{sec:max_sliced}. For simplicity of exposition, we only state the case for when $\gamma$ is non-decreasing. 

\begin{proposition}[Max-sliced Wasserstein distance] \label{prop:wc_signed_choquet_int_max}
Let $I_h$ be a signed Choquet integral with absolutely continuous distortion function $h$ and distortion weight function $\gamma$ satisfying $0< \norm{\gamma}_2< \infty$. Let $\mathcal{G}$ be a set of functions mapping $\R^n$ to $\R$ and $\X\in\L^{2}_{\mathcal{G}}$. For non-decreasing $\gamma$ and $\epsilon>0$, it holds that 
\begin{equation} \label{eq:wc_bound_5} \sup_{g_0\in\mathcal{G}}\left(\sup_{Y\in g_0(\hU_{\mathcal{G}, \epsilon}(\X))}I_h(Y)\right)\leq \sup_{g_0\in\mathcal{G}}\Big(I_h(g_0(\X))\Big)+\sqrt{\epsilon}\norm{\gamma}_2. 
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof:} Fix $g_0\in\mathcal{G}$. First we apply \Cref{prop:Lipschitz_max_gen} to the inner supremum of the left hand side (lhs) of \eqref{eq:wc_bound_5} and obtain 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:proof-wc-max-sliced}
    \sup_{Y\in g_0(\hU_{\mathcal{G}, \epsilon}(\X))}\rho(Y)
    \leq
\sup_{Y\in\U_{\epsilon}(g_0(\X))}\rho(Y)\,.
\end{equation}
Next we apply \Cref{theorem:wc_signed_choquet_int} with $\phi(x) = x^2$ to the right hand side (rhs) and find that its worst-case quantile function is $F_{g_0(\X)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda^*}\gamma(u)$ with optimal Lagrange multiplier $\lambda^{*}=\frac{\norm{\gamma}_2}{2\sqrt{\epsilon}}$. Hence, the worst-case quantile function is $F_{g_0(\X)}^{-1}(u)+\frac{\sqrt{\epsilon}}{\norm{\gamma}_2}\gamma(u)$. Thus, for any $g_0\in\mathcal{G}$, the rhs of \eqref{eq:proof-wc-max-sliced} is bounded by $I_h(g_0(\X))+\sqrt{\epsilon}\norm{\gamma}_2$. Taking suprema over all $g_0\in\mathcal{G}$ concludes the proof. 
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

From the above result we observe the following. First, the lhs of \eqref{eq:wc_bound_5} is the largest signed Choquet integral over (a) all distributions whose aggregate risk is close to the aggregate risk of the reference distribution and (b) all aggregation functions in $\mathcal{G}$. This quantity is bounded by the largest signed Choquet integral of the reference distribution over all aggregation functions in $\mathcal{G}$, plus a penalty term that only depends on the risk measure and the magnitude of uncertainty.

\section{Numerical Examples}\label{sec:examples}

We discuss three numerical examples of the bounds in \Cref{sec:application}. For the first two examples, we work with the ES and the Inter-Expected Shortfall Range (IER), which are signed Choquet integrals with non-decreasing distortion weight functions. In the last example, we consider a distortion weight function that is non-negative and non-monotonic. For all three examples, we work with the same reference distribution $\X=(X_1, \ldots, X_4)$ with marginal distributions given in \Cref{tab:parameters}. The dependence structure of $\X$ is given by a $t$ copula with $3$ degrees of freedom and correlation coefficient $0.7$. 

