The aggregation function in all three examples is 
\begin{equation*}
    \mfg(\x)=-x_1-2\max\{x_2-5,0\}-3\max\{35-x_3,0\}-4x_4\,.
\end{equation*} This aggregation function corresponds to the negative payoff of a portfolio consisting of $1$ unit of a risky asset $X_1$, $2$ units of a call option on $X_2$ with strike price $5$, $3$ units of a put option on $X_3$ with strike price $35$, and $4$ units of a risky asset $X_4$. Clearly, $\mfg$ is Lipschitz (wrt the $\L^2$ norm) with Lipschitz constant $L=4$. As the aggregate distribution of $\mfg(\X)$ does not admit a closed form expression, we approximate its density using kernel density estimation on a Monte Carlo sample of size $100,000$. 

\begin{example} [Expected Shortfall] 

The Expected Shortfall (ES) at level $p\in(0,1)$ is given by $\text{ES}_p(X)=\frac{1}{1-p}\int_{p}^{1}F_X^{-1}(t)dt$. Thus, the ES is a distortion risk measure with distortion weight function $\gamma(u)=\frac{1}{1-p}\Id_{u>p}$. Clearly, $\gamma$ is non-decreasing, thus by \Cref{prop:wc_signed_choquet_int}, $i)$, 
\begin{align*}
\sup_{\Y\in \bU^{4}_{\epsilon}(\X)}\text{ES}_p\big(\mfg(\Y)\big) &= \text{ES}_p(\mfg(\X))+L\epsilon\norm{\gamma}_2\,.
\end{align*}
Note that we have equality as $\mfg$ is linear in $x_1$ and $x_4$. 

\Cref{fig:ex1} shows the worst-case quantile functions for $p = 0.9$ and $\epsilon\in\{1, 2, 5\}$, given by $F_{\mfg(\X)}^{-1}(u)+4\sqrt{10}\, \epsilon\, \Id_{u>0.9}$. The reference distribution is plotted using a solid light blue curve. The plot only shows the right tail of the quantile functions because all curves are identical on $[0, p)$. This is due to the fact that the ES only accounts for values in the right tail, thus the worst-case cdf has all of the deviation (from the reference cdf) in its right tail. Furthermore, as we increase the size of the uncertainty ($\ep$), the worst-case quantile deviates further from the reference quantile.  

