\caption{Worst-case quantile functions for the ES (with $p=0.9$). The reference distribution is plotted in blue. The red, purple, and orange lines correspond to the worst-case quantile functions for $\epsilon\in\{1, 2, 5\}$, respectively.}\label{fig:ex1}
\end{figure}
\end{example}

\begin{example} [Inter-expected Shortfall Range] The IER at level $p\in (0.5,1)$ is given by 
\begin{equation*}
\text{IER}_p(X):=\frac{1}{1-p}\left(\int_{p}^{1}F_X^{-1}(t) \, dt-\int_{0}^{1-p}F_X^{-1}(t)\, dt\right).  
\end{equation*}
The IER is subadditive and a signed Choquet integral, but not a distortion risk measure. Indeed its distortion weight function is $\gamma (u)=\frac{1}{1-p}(\Id_{p<u\leq 1}-\Id_{0\leq u\leq 1-p})$ and thus is negative for $0\leq u\leq 1-p$. We refer to \citet{wang} for further discussions on the IER.

As $\gamma$ is non-decreasing, by \Cref{prop:wc_signed_choquet_int}, $i)$ we have
\begin{align*}
\sup_{\Y\in \bU^{4}_{\epsilon}(\X)}\text{IER}_p\big(\mfg(\Y)\big) &= \text{IER}_p(\mfg(\X))+L\epsilon\norm{\gamma}_2
\end{align*}
and for $q = 0.75$, the worst-case quantile function is $F_{\mfg(\X)}^{-1}(u)+4\sqrt{2}\epsilon(\Id_{0.75<u\leq 1}-\Id_{0<u\leq 0.25})$. 

\Cref{fig:ex2} depicts the worst-case quantile functions for the IER with $p = 0.75$. The red, purple, and orange lines correspond to the worst-case quantile functions for $\epsilon\in\{1, 2, 5\}$, respectively, while the reference quantile function is plotted in blue. For the IER, we observe that the worst-case quantile functions deviate further from the reference as $\epsilon$ increases, similar to the ES. However, unlike the ES case, we see deviations from the reference distribution in both the left and right tails of the distribution because the IER is a measure of spread. From \Cref{tab:spread}, we can see that both the IER and the variance increases as $\epsilon$ increases. 

