\section{Max-Sliced Wasserstein Distances} \phantomsection\label{appendix:a}

Here, we prove that the max-sliced Wasserstein distance (as defined in \Cref{definition:max_sliced}) is in fact a distance. Similar arguments are used to prove that the max-sliced Wasserstein distances defined using Radon transforms is a distance, see e.g., \citet{deshpande} and \citet{kolouri}. 

{\parindent0pt
\textbf{Proof of \Cref{lemma:distance}:}

We show that the max-sliced Wasserstein distance satisfies for all $F_{\X}, F_{\Y}, F_{\Z}\in\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$
\begin{enumerate} [label = $\roman*)$]
\item $\mW(F_{\Y}, F_{\X})\geq 0$,
\item $\mW(F_{\Y}, F_{\X})=\mW(F_{\X}, F_{\Y})$,
\item $\mW(F_{\Y}, F_{\X})=0$ if and only if $F_{\X}=F_{\Y}$,
\item $\mW(F_{\Y}, F_{\X})\leq\mW(F_{\Y}, F_{\Z})+\mW(F_{\Z}, F_{\X})$\,.
\end{enumerate}

\smallskip

Properties $i)$ and $ii)$ follows since the Wasserstein distance is non-negative and symmetric.

For property $iii)$ we note that the following are equivalent: 
\begin{align*}
F_{\X}=F_{\Y}\qquad  &\text{if and only if} \qquad  F_{\bgamma^T\X}=F_{\bgamma^T\Y}, \ \forall \bgamma\in\R^d \\
&\text{if and only if} \qquad W(F_{\bgamma^T\X}, F_{\bgamma^T\Y})=0, \ \forall \bgamma\in\R^d \\
&\text{if and only if}\qquad \mW(F_{\X},F_{\Y})=0\, .
\end{align*}

For property $iv)$ we have, using the triangle inequality, that
\begin{align*}
\mW(F_{\X}, F_{\Y}) &= \sup_{\bgamma\in\R^n}W(F_{\bgamma^T\X}, F_{\bgamma^T\Y}) \\
&\leq \sup_{\bgamma\in\R^n}\big\{W(F_{\bgamma^T\X}, F_{\bgamma^T\Z})+W(F_{\bgamma^T\Z}, F_{\bgamma^T\Y})\big\} \\
&\leq \sup_{\bgamma\in\R^n}W(F_{\bgamma^T\X}, F_{\bgamma^T\Z})+ \sup_{\bgamma\in\R^n}W(F_{\bgamma^T\Z}, F_{\bgamma^T\Y}) \\
&= \mW(F_{\X}, F_{\Z})+\mW(F_{\Z}, F_{\Y}).
\end{align*}
Combining, we obtain that the max-sliced Wasserstein distance is a distance on the space of cdfs $\mathcal{M}_p(\R^n)$.
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

{\parindent0pt 
\textbf{Proof of \Cref{lemma:gen_distance}:} 

Case $i)$ follows using similar arguments as in the proof of \Cref{lemma:distance}. Recall that a pseudo distance satisfies all properties of a distance, apart from $\mW_p^{\mathcal{G}}(F_{\X},F_{\Y})=0$ if and only if $F_\X = F_\Y$. 

Case $ii)$, since the Wasserstein distance is a distance,
\begin{align*}
\mW_p^{\mathcal{G}}(F_{\X},F_{\Y})=0\qquad  &\text{if and only if} \qquad  W_p(F_{g(\X)}, F_{g(\Y)})=0, \ \forall g\in\mathcal{G} \\
&\text{if and only if} \qquad F_{g(\X)}=F_{g(\Y)}, \ \forall g\in\mathcal{G}.
\end{align*}
Hence, the $\mathcal{G}$-max-sliced Wasserstein distance is a distance if and only if $F_{g(\X)}=F_{g(\Y)}, \ \forall g\in\mathcal{G}$ is equivalent to $F_{\X}=F_{\Y}$. 
$\hfill\blacksquare$
}

