\begin{lemma}[\!\cite{HoRaTa-23}]
\label{lem: level set L}
The sublevel set $\mathcal{L}^-$ is convex and contains the ray $\{t(-1,-1):t>0\}$, whereas the superlevel set $\mathcal{L}^+$ includes the rays $\{t(-1,0):t>t_0\}$ and $\{t(0,-1):t>t_0\}$ for some $t_0$ sufficiently large. Additionally, $\mathcal{L}$ admits a tangent at $(0,0)$, which lies outside $\mathcal{L}^-$ and satisfies the equation
\begin{equation*}\textstyle
	\left( \sum_{k,\ell}k p_{k,\ell} \right) x + \left( \sum_{k,\ell}\ell p_{k,\ell} \right) y = 0.
\end{equation*}
\end{lemma}
Lemma~\ref{lem: level set L} is stated and proved in \cite{HoRaTa-23} as Lemma~1. 
