\section{Introduction} \label{sec:intro}
\IEEEPARstart{M}{odern} cloud applications increasingly adopt microservices architectures, breaking the system into many decoupled services that communicate over a network \cite{newman_build_microserv, nadareishvili_microserv_architect}. While this design brings advantages in development and scalability \cite{ganje_microserv_organizat}, it also introduces reliability challenges. Each user request may invoke a chain of microservices, so the failure of even a single service can cascade and cause the entire request to fail \cite{soldani_explain_microserv, abbott_art_scalabili}. Ensuring resilience—i.e., the system's ability to remain operational despite failures—has become a critical concern \cite{beyer_site_reliabili}. Industry practitioners have pioneered chaos engineering techniques (e.g., Netflix's Chaos Monkey) that randomly disable components in production to test system robustness \cite{basiri_chao_engineer}. Academic efforts like Gremlin \cite{heorhiadi_gremlin_systemati} have provided systematic frameworks for resilience testing of microservices by injecting faults to observe system behavior. These methods, however, require running the full system and can be time-consuming and risky to execute in production \cite{giamattei_automated_functiona}.

In this paper, we propose a novel offline estimation technique based solely on system topology, offering a safe and lightweight alternative. We hypothesize that by representing the microservice application as a directed graph of service dependencies, we can predict the system's resilience to service failures without needing to physically disrupt the live system. Our hypothesis posits that Monte-Carlo fault-injection simulation applied to a static service-dependency graph can predict the system's success rate under 30\% component-failure stress with at most 15\% relative error (measured against the prediction). If true, this would enable engineers to evaluate “what-if" failure scenarios quickly and safely during design or testing \cite{avizienis_basic_concept}, and to identify critical weak points in the architecture \cite{ford_build_evolution}.

To evaluate this hypothesis, we selected a realistic, complex microservice application: the DeathStarBench Social Network \cite{gan_open_bench}. This benchmark resembles a scaled-down Twitter or Facebook, consisting of many interconnected microservices for social networking functionality \cite{dragoni_microserv_yesterday}. We construct a dependency graph of these microservices from tracing data collected with Jaeger, where edges represent observed service-to-service dependencies, and then perform a Monte-Carlo simulation of random service outages on the graph. We also set up the actual social-network application (with dozens of Docker containers) and conduct chaos experiments by killing services and measuring the resulting successful request rate.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We present a detailed graph-based modeling technique to estimate microservice resilience, which eliminates the need for intrusive fault injection.
    \item We describe an open experimental framework combining simulation and live fault injection for empirical validation.
    \item We provide an empirical validation showing that the graph model's resilience estimates closely match the actual system's resilience, under both non-replicated and replicated deployment scenarios.
    \item We discuss the implications of these results, highlighting how such modeling can inform architecture design and guide fault tolerance decisions \cite{meiklejohn_resilient_microserv}.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section \ref{sec:back}, we describe background on the social-network microservice benchmark and related work on microservice resilience. Section \ref{sec:setup} details our experimental setup, including the system implementation and failure injection method. Section \ref{sec:method} presents our modeling methodology, from dependency graph extraction to Monte-Carlo simulation. Section \ref{sec:results} shows the results of comparing model predictions to live experiments. Section \ref{sec:discuss} provides discussion and analysis of the findings, and Section \ref{sec:conclude} concludes the paper with final remarks and future directions.

\section{Background and Related Work} \label{sec:back}
\subsection{Microservice Benchmark - Social Network}
We use the Social Network application from the DeathStarBench suite \cite{gan_open_bench} as a representative microservices system. This application implements a social media platform with unidirectional follow relationships (similar to Twitter) \cite{dragoni_microserv_yesterday}. Users can compose posts containing text, media, links, and mentions of other users; these posts are then broadcast to all of the author's followers. Other users can read their personal home timeline (an aggregated feed of posts from people they follow) or visit another user's profile to read that user's user timeline (posts by a specific user). Under the hood, the Social Network is composed of about a dozen microservices, each responsible for a specific piece of functionality. Requests like post composition or feed reading traverse multiple services, forming dynamic invocation chains with varying degrees of parallelism and optionality. These characteristics make the system a realistic and valuable benchmark for studying the impact of individual service failures and evaluating overall resilience.

Fig. \ref{fig:1} illustrates the architecture of the social-network microservices system. The entry point is an NGINX-based API Gateway that receives HTTP requests from clients \cite{gan_open_bench}. Depending on the request (e.g., reading a timeline or composing a post), the gateway forwards it to the appropriate service. For instance, a compose post request goes to the ComposeService, which coordinates several downstream services to handle different aspects of posting. These include a UserService (for user profile lookups), MediaService (to handle image or video attachments), TextService (for processing text content, e.g., filtering or formatting), URLShortenService (to shorten URLs in the post), and UserMentionService (to record user mentions) – some of which may be invoked in parallel or conditionally \cite{dragoni_microserv_yesterday}. The compose workflow also involves generating a new post ID via a UniqueIDService, storing the post in the PostStorageService (backed by a database), updating the author's own timeline via a UserTimelineService, and pushing the new post to all followers' feeds via a HomeTimelineService. The SocialGraphService maintains the follower graph and is consulted to find all followers of a user for feed fan-out. Meanwhile, read requests for timelines are handled by the timeline services: a home-timeline read goes directly to HomeTimelineService, and a user profile read goes to UserTimelineService. These services fetch the requested posts (from caches or storage) and return them to the gateway, which responds to the client. The Social Network uses a combination of storage systems: Redis and Memcached for caching, and MongoDB for persistent storage of posts, user profiles, and social graphs \cite{gan_open_bench}.

