This Social Network application is non-trivial in scale: it consists of 12 stateless microservices (each potentially running in its own container) and several stateful data stores. This architecture involves deep request chains (up to 8 services in a compose workflow), centralized ID generation, and fan-out patterns during feed updates — all of which contribute to potential fragility and make it a good candidate for resilience analysis \cite{richardson_microserv_pattern}.
A failure in any one of the critical services could disrupt certain user-facing functionalities. For example, if the TextService crashes, posts cannot be composed properly; if the HomeTimelineService is down, users cannot load their feed, etc. The presence of caching layers and data replication in databases can mitigate some failures \cite{nygard_release_it}. However, in our setup no automatic failover between microservices is implemented (aside from an experiment scenario with replicated instances, discussed later). Thus, the resilience of the application primarily depends on the redundancy (or lack thereof) of each microservice and the structure of the call graph \cite{lewis_microserv_definitio}.

\subsection{Microservice Resilience and Fault Tolerance}
The challenge of achieving resilience in microservice systems has spawned considerable interest in both industry and academia \cite{microsoft_resilienc_pattern,bushong_microserv_analysi}. Reliability engineering principles from large-scale systems (e.g., Google's Site Reliability Engineering handbook \cite{beyer_site_reliabili}) emphasize designing for failure, and microservices demand applying these at a fine granularity. Common resiliency patterns have been adapted to microservices, such as circuit breakers and retries, to prevent cascading failures when a service becomes unresponsive \cite{shekhar_microserv_design}. Tools like Netflix Hystrix implement these patterns in practice, and are widely used to make microservices more resilient by isolating failures \cite{netflixtechnologyblog_articl_hystrix}. While Hystrix popularized resilience patterns, it is no longer maintained and has been replaced by more modern tools (e.g., Resilience4j).

In terms of systematic resilience testing, Heorhiadi et al. (2016) introduced Gremlin, a framework for systematic resilience testing of microservices \cite{heorhiadi_gremlin_systemati}. Gremlin can inject failures by manipulating network traffic between services, enabling controlled experiments on how failures propagate. Such fault injection experimentation (often termed chaos engineering) has been embraced as a way to reveal hidden failure modes in complex microservice deployments \cite{basiri_chao_engineer,akuthota_chao_engineer,li_enjoy_observe,pawlikowski_chaos_engineering_site}. However, testing all combinations of failures is combinatorially expensive for systems with many services due to high computational complexity, which motivates the need for smart modeling \cite{giamattei_automated_functiona}. Observability plays a crucial role in understanding and mitigating failures in such complex systems \cite{suthar_observabi_trend}.

Recent research has also looked at model-driven resilience analysis \cite{rausand_system_reliabili}. Mendonça et al. (2020) explored model-based analysis of microservice resiliency patterns \cite{mendonc_model}. Their framework encodes common resiliency patterns—specifically Retry and Circuit Breaker—as continuous-time Markov chains and analyses them with PRISM \cite{kwiatkowska_prism40_verificat}, obtaining metrics such as success probability, mean time-to-repair, and latency across a range of threshold configurations. Similarly, other works have applied probabilistic model checking to evaluate different resiliency pattern configurations under varying component availability assumptions. These works highlight that abstract modeling can be a valuable complement to empirical testing for microservices resilience.

\subsection{Discovered Gap and Proposed Approach}
Existing model-based approaches often require formal system specifications or focus narrowly on resiliency patterns. They rarely use real runtime dependency data, and do not quantitatively validate model predictions against live systems. This creates a gap between theoretical modeling and practical resilience analysis in real-world microservice architectures. Meanwhile, our approach aims to be more direct and broadly applicable: we leverage the actual dependency graph of the application (obtained via tracing) and perform a straightforward Monte-Carlo simulation on that graph. This is akin to classic network reliability analysis, where the probability of successful communication is computed by simulating random failures over a network connectivity graph \cite{yang_microres_versatile_resilience}, but applied to a microservice call graph. Such an approach can naturally incorporate the presence of service replicas, which we include in our study — we model replication by assigning a service node a failure probability that reflects the aggregate availability of all its replicas, assuming independent failures.

Proposed approach can be driven by real observed dependencies rather than idealized models. To our knowledge, this work is the first to demonstrate quantitatively that a simple graph failure model can predict the resilience of a complex microservice benchmark with high accuracy. Our results strengthen the argument that model-based analysis can guide resilience improvement \cite{meiklejohn_resilient_microserv}, potentially reducing the need for exhaustive fault injection testing in every scenario \cite{giamattei_automated_functiona}. The security aspects of microservices, while not the direct focus here, also benefit from understanding dependencies and potential failure points \cite{alboqmi_enhancing_microservice_security,berardi_microservice_security}.

\section{Experimental Setup} \label{sec:setup}
To test our hypothesis, we set up an environment where we can measure the social-network application's resilience under controlled failure scenarios. Our experimental setup involves two key aspects: (a) deploying the Social Network microservices on a containerized platform with configurable replication, and (b) running a load generator and failure injection mechanism (a "chaos" script) to observe the system's behavior when certain services crash.

\subsection{System Infrastructure}
We deployed the social-network microservices using Docker Compose, which allowed us to easily run each service in a separate Docker container on a single host. The system comprises the following core services (each corresponding to one container in the no-replication case): NGINX Gateway, ComposeService, UserService, MediaService, TextService, URLShortenService, UserMentionService, UniqueIDService, SocialGraphService, PostStorageService, UserTimelineService, and HomeTimelineService \cite{gan_open_bench}. These services communicate via Thrift RPC or HTTP calls. Additionally, the application uses Redis and Memcached as in-memory caches and MongoDB as the primary database. We ran single-instance Redis, Memcached, and MongoDB containers.

In our no-replication scenario, each microservice above is a single point of failure (one container instance). In the with-replication scenario, specific stateless services were configured with multiple instances. Based on the resilience.py script, the replication counts were:
\begin{itemize}
    \item compose-post-service: 3 replicas
    \item home-timeline-service: 3 replicas
    \item user-timeline-service: 3 replicas
    \item text-service: 3 replicas
    \item media-service: 3 replicas
\end{itemize}
The replication factor of 3 was chosen to allow one or two replicas to fail while still retaining service availability, in line with common redundancy practices. Other services not in this list (such as user-service, unique-id-service, url-shorten-service, user-mention-service, social-graph-service, post-storage-service), and the databases, remained single instances. The Docker Compose setup was adjusted for the replication scenario by launching the specified number of containers for these services. By default, NGINX was configured with round-robin upstream balancing to distribute requests among the available replicas. Each service group had a separate upstream definition.

\subsection{Workload Generation}
To measure the system's resilience, we used a workload generator (wrk2 HTTP benchmarking tool with scripting) to continuously issue a mix of read and write requests against the social network's API. We simulated users performing actions such as loading their home timeline, viewing another user's profile timeline, and composing new posts. Although the load is moderate (300 RPS, 30 seconds of workload, 10 concurrent user threads), it was verified to fully utilize basic request paths without saturating system resources. The ratio of reads to writes was approximately 4:1. Each request was checked for success (HTTP 200 OK).

\subsection{Failure Injection}
We developed a bash script (chaos.sh) to terminate a fraction of running containers. We parameterized it with a failure probability $P_{\text{fail}} = 0.30$ (30\% of total containers to kill). The script uses Docker commands to randomly select 30\% of the service containers (excluding databases from this count but they could be part of the overall container fleet subject to failures if not explicitly excluded by the script's container selection logic, which typically targets application service containers) and kill them. The NGINX gateway was generally kept alive to ensure requests could reach the backend. Containers remained down for the experiment duration. By default, wrk2 runs a warmup period before injecting failures (about 10 seconds) and continues the workload for a fixed duration post-failure (e.g., 1 minute).
During the chaos experiment, we measured the success request rate. The live resilience metric ($R_{\text{live}}$) is calculated as:
\begin{equation}
R_{\text{live}} = 1 - \frac{\text{Number of failed responses}}{\text{Total requests issued}}
\label{eq:rlive}
\end{equation}
In a fully healthy run (no failures), $R_{\text{live}} = 1.00$. Under failures, $R_{\text{live}}$ drops.

\subsection{Data Collection}
We performed the failure injection test for each scenario (no-replication and with-replication), using fixed random seed for reproducibility. After the test, we collected $R_{\text{live}}$. In parallel, we ran our simulation model to produce a predicted $R_{\text{model}}$ for the same scenario. After that, we calculated their means and standard deviations. The load generator logged each HTTP request, including timestamp and status code. The failure rate was calculated from the log as the proportion of 5xx server errors + socket errors to the total number of requests. We excluded 4xx errors because they are considered relatively successful in delivering the intended service outcomes.

\section{Methodology} \label{sec:method}
Our methodology consists of constructing a graph-based model of the microservice system and performing a Monte-Carlo resilience simulation on this model, then comparing it to the empirical results.

\subsection{Model Discovery or Dependency Graph Extraction}
Model discovery is a proposed new family of CI/CD-friendly tools that automatically mine runtime telemetry—distributed traces, logs, metrics—to synthesize an explicit, analyzable system model (e.g., a service-dependency graph, queueing network, or CTMC). Positioned right after the build stage, a model-discovery step keeps an up-to-date architectural model on every commit, bridging observability and formal verification. Industrial surveys show that trace-driven graph extraction is already routine, while in-house platforms such as Uber’s CRISP and IBM’s Istio/Jaeger graph generator demonstrate the feasibility of fully automated pipelines \cite{li_enjoy_observe}; our own extraction of a dependency graph from Jaeger traces therefore acts as a first concrete prototype of this tool class.

We obtained a directed graph $G = (V, E)$ representing service call dependencies in the Social Network application. Each node $v \in V$ corresponds to a microservice. A directed edge $(u \rightarrow v) \in E$ indicates that service $u$ directly calls service $v$. Edges are unweighted in this study; all calls are treated as equally critical. We built this graph by using distributed tracing data (Jaeger) from the system's execution, written to a deps.json file and loaded into a NetworkX directed graph in Python \cite{hagberg_exploring_network_structure}. The `nginx-web-server` is included as a node in the graph model, serving as the entry point whose aliveness is checked as part of request success, consistent with the resilience.py script's logic. Database systems were not explicitly modeled as separate nodes; their failure is implicitly captured by the failure of services relying on them.

