\subsection{Monte-Carlo Failure Simulation}
Given the dependency graph $G$, we want to estimate the probability that a user request succeeds given a random pattern of service failures. We identified three representative endpoint operations. Their required services (targets) and weights are defined in resilience.py as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item home-timeline: Targets: home-timeline-service, post-storage-service, social-graph-service. Weight: 0.6.
    \item user-timeline: Targets: user-timeline-service, post-storage-service. Weight: 0.3.
    \item compose-post: Base Targets: compose-post-service, user-service, unique-id-service, post-storage-service, user-timeline-service, home-timeline-service, text-service, social-graph-service. Additionally, media-service, url-shorten-service, and user-mention-service are added to the targets conditionally based on random choices during each simulation sample for this endpoint (specifically, media-service is added with probability $p = 4/5$, url-shorten-service with $p = 5/6$, and user-mention-service with $p = 5/6$). Weight: 0.1.
\end{itemize}
The simulation is implemented in a Python script resilience.py. The input is as follows: dependency graph $G$, failure probability $P_{\text{fail}} = 0.30$, number of samples $N = 4,500,000$, and a flag indicating whether replication is enabled.
For each sample $1 \dots N$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Determine Failed Containers/Services: A global list of all container instances (across all services and their respective replicas as defined in Section 3) is created. The total number of containers to kill, $K_{\text{killed\_containers}}$, is calculated as $P_{\text{fail}}$ multiplied by the total number of container instances, rounded to the nearest integer. $K_{\text{killed\_containers}}$ container instances are randomly sampled without replacement from the global list (victims = random.sample(container\_list, K\_killed\_containers)). A microservice is marked as "alive" in a simulation sample if at least one of its container instances remains running after failure sampling (otherwise, it is considered "failed"). It models basic availability under replication, assuming client-side load balancing can access any healthy replica. Described process yields a set $F$ of failed services for that sample.
    \item Determine Endpoint Success: For each endpoint type, the request is considered successful if the nginx-web-server (client) is alive, all its necessary target services (as defined above, including conditionally added ones for compose-post) are alive (i.e., $\notin F$), and there is a path in the graph of currently alive services ($G_{\text{alive}}$) from the nginx-web-server to each of these necessary target services.
    \item Record a success (1) or failure (0) for each endpoint.
    \item After sampling, compute the estimated availability for each endpoint ($R_{\text{endpoint}}$) as its average success rate (number of successes / N).
    \item Compute an overall resilience metric $R_{\text{model}}$ as a weighted average of the endpoint availabilities:
\end{enumerate}
\begin{equation}
R_{\text{model}} = \sum (R_{\text{endpoint}} \times \text{weight}_{\text{endpoint}})
\label{eq:rmodel}
\end{equation}
using the weights 0.6 for home-timeline, 0.3 for user-timeline, and 0.1 for compose-post.
\begin{enumerate}
    \setcounter{enumi}{5} % Continue numbering
    \item Output the individual endpoint availabilities and $R_{\text{model}}$.
\end{enumerate}
Our simulations used $N = 4,500,000$ Monte-Carlo samples per run, repeated 16 times for each scenario (32 runs in total). This sampling method, which selects a fixed number of containers to fail globally from all available instances, differs from a model where each service or each replica fails independently with probability $p$. The conditional target selection in compose-post introduces probabilistic variance into the endpoint availability estimates. This variance was empirically observed to be low due to the large number of samples ($N = 4.5$ million), stabilizing estimates to three decimal places.

\subsection{Combining Model and Experimental Results}
After running the simulation, we obtain $R^{\text{norepl}}_{\text{model}}$ and $R^{\text{repl}}_{\text{model}}$. From the chaos experiments, we have measured $R^{\text{norepl}}_{\text{live}}$ and $R^{\text{repl}}_{\text{live}}$ (averaged over trials). We collate these into a comparative view.
The resilience metrics we focus on in results are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item No Replication Scenario: $R_{\text{model\_norepl}}$ vs $R_{\text{live\_norepl}}$.
    \item With Replication Scenario: $R_{\text{model\_repl}}$ vs $R_{\text{live\_repl}}$.
\end{itemize}
A perfect model would have $R_{\text{model}} = R_{\text{live}}$. We analyze any deviations to understand their source. It's worth noting that our model assumes independence of failures across services and no shared fate or cascading internal logic (e.g., retries, backpressure). Also, it does not incorporate network latency or partial degradation — it is a pure availability model (service either works or not). These limitations are discussed in Section \ref{sec:discuss}.

\subsection{Reproducibility}
All code, configuration, and raw data for the 32 Monte-Carlo and chaos-experiment runs are openly available in our GitHub repository \cite{github_krasnovsky}; a GitHub Actions workflow re-executes the entire pipeline on every fork, so any researcher can reproduce every figure and table “as is” with a single click.

\section{Results} \label{sec:results}
We now present the results of our resilience estimation, comparing the graph-based model predictions with the observed empirical outcomes. We evaluate both the no-replication deployment (each service single-instance) and the with-replication deployment (where key services had 3 replicas each, and others one). Fig. \ref{fig:3} visualizes the comparison.

\subsection{No Replication (Single Instance per Service)}
In this scenario, the system has no redundancy – each microservice is a single point of failure. Across 16 independent chaos experiments at a 30\% random container failure rate, our model predicted an overall resilience $R_{\text{model\_norepl}} = 0.161$ (i.e., $\sim$16\% of requests would succeed). The empirically measured resilience was $R_{\text{live\_norepl}} = 0.186$ ($\sim$18\% success rate). These values are quite low, which is expected: with nearly one-third of services down, most multi-service request flows are broken. The key observation is that the model's prediction was very close to the actual result — the absolute error therefore averaged $0.025 \pm 0.001$ (range 0.023–0.028), corresponding to a 13.5\% mean relative error and 14.6\% worst-case when normalised by the model value.

\subsection{With Replication (Specified Instances per Service)}
In this scenario, key services (compose-post-service, home-timeline-service, user-timeline-service, text-service, media-service) had three replicas each, while other services and data stores remained single instances. Our model predicted $R_{\text{model\_repl}} = 0.305$ ($\sim$30\% success rate). Remarkably, the observed resilience was $R_{\text{live\_repl}} = 0.305$ as well — an exact match to three decimal places. 15 of the 16 trials were within 0.003 of the prediction, and the largest deviation (0.009) corresponds to a 2.9\% relative error. This almost identical outcome provides strong evidence in support of our hypothesis: the graph model accurately captured the effect of replication on resilience as implemented in the code. With this specific replication strategy, the system's resilience roughly doubled compared to no-replication. While the exact match in the replicated scenario is encouraging, further testing across broader replication strategies and workloads is required to generalize this finding.

