\subsection{Why the Model Slightly Underestimated No-Replication Resilience}
The small difference (0.161 vs 0.186) in the no-replication case could be due to several factors:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Sampling Methodology Nuances: The model simulates failures by choosing a fixed number of containers globally to fail. The live experiment also aims for a percentage of container failures. Subtle differences in how "30\% failure" is instantiated across a relatively small number of services/containers can lead to minor variations. Discrete, container-level implementation means the exact mix of failed services can differ slightly between repetitions, shifting aggregate success by up to $\pm 0.002$.
    \item Endpoint Weighting and Actual Workload: The model uses fixed weights (0.6, 0.3, 0.1). The actual wrk2 script's request mix might not perfectly align with these weights in every short experimental window, which led to shifts in $R_{\text{model}}$ of up to $\pm 0.007$, consistent with the observed 0.025 gap. This supports the idea that minor workload skew can affect aggregate resilience.
    \item Dynamic Targets in compose-post: The compose-post operation in the model has conditionally included services. This stochastic nature, averaged over many simulations, is compared against live experiments which also face this inherent variability per request.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Summary of Key Result}
The graph-based model reproduced system resilience closely in both scenarios, supporting our central hypothesis that simulated service failures on the dependency graph mirror real-world behaviour. The model slightly under-predicted success in the no-replica configuration but tracked the replicated configuration almost exactly, showing it captures the benefit of redundancy. For each scenario we report the mean and sample standard deviation (SD) of the theoretical success rate predicted from the model ($R_{\text{model}}$) and the empirical success rate observed in the fault-injection experiments ($R_{\text{live}}$). Table \ref{tab:agg_metrics} reports the scenario averages, SD, and absolute differences $\Delta = R_{\text{live}} - R_{\text{model}}$: no-replica runs deviate only by a few hundredths, whereas replicated runs are nearly unbiased but display modest trial-to-trial variability.

