\section{Discussion} \label{sec:discuss}
The experimental results show that a graph-based failure simulation can be an effective proxy for real-world resilience testing \cite{kleijnen_design_and_analysis}.
Key moments:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Accuracy and Usefulness: The close match, particularly with replication, suggests that for this benchmark, failure behavior is largely determined by service dependencies, their connectivity, and the number of available container instances. This supports using static/model-based analysis of architecture for resilience audits \cite{avizienis_basic_concept, meiklejohn_resilient_microserv}. The Monte-Carlo simulation approach is scalable for larger graphs \cite{sabuhi_micro_observability_patterns}.
    \item Identifying Critical Services: The model can pinpoint critical services by observing which failures most impact the weighted $R_{\text{model}}$. This is valuable for prioritizing redundancy or mitigation efforts \cite{ford_build_evolution, beyer_the_site_reliability}. The "what-if" analysis capability (e.g., simulating different replica counts) is a strong advantage. PRISM 4.0 couples symbolic (parametric) model checking with a discrete-event simulation engine, so key configuration parameters—such as the number of replicas per service—can be left as variables and later instantiated without rebuilding the model, yielding availability or latency predictions in seconds \cite{kwiatkowska_prism40_verificat}.

This ability to sweep parameters and obtain closed-form or rapidly re-evaluated quantitative results turns PRISM into an automated “what-if” oracle, enabling sensitivity studies that would be prohibitively expensive to conduct through repeated fault-injection experiments.
    \item Single Points of Failure (SPOFs): The database (MongoDB) remained a single instance in both scenarios. Its failure would severely impact most operations. This was reflected in both model and live experiments, capping overall resilience. The model could easily be extended to simulate DB replication.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Limitations}
Despite the success, several limitations must be acknowledged:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Fail-Stop Assumption: We assumed services fail completely (stop serving) and otherwise behave normally. Real failures can be more complex: partial outages, slow responses (latency spikes), or gray failures where a service is degraded but not totally down \cite{huang_grey_failure}. These are not captured in our binary model. Performance degradation modeling would require more complex simulations \cite{zhang_mqsim, grottke_fighting_bugs_remove}.
    \item No Dynamic Request Routing or Asynchronous Communications: In our system, if a service is down, requests that need it just fail. In more advanced systems, there may be fallbacks or async calls between services \cite{shekhar_microserv_design}. Our model would need a way to capture such conditional dependencies.
    \item Perfect Knowledge of Dependencies: We built our graph from exhaustive tracing. We assume we know all service dependencies \cite{suthar_observabi_trend, mendonc_model}. Missing an edge in the graph would cause the model to overestimate resilience. Model may not be applicable in a non-stable network topology (service mesh, serverless environments).
    \item Workload Sensitivity: Our $R_{\text{model}}$ was dependent on the mix of operations and their weights. Different workloads would weight the endpoints differently.
    \item Stateful Failures \& Correlations: We treated each container failure independently in terms of selection, but some failures might be correlated in real systems (e.g., host failure). Our simulation did not explicitly model such correlations.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Generality}
Although we focused on one benchmark, the approach is general. Its accuracy may vary for systems with highly dynamic topologies or extensive asynchronous event-driven interactions. We envision this graph-based resilience estimation being integrated into the CI/CD or design phase of microservices \cite{humble_continuous_delivery_reliable,giamattei_automated_functiona}. Notably, the accurate results could be collected during long modelling experiments, which withholds this method for being well-scalable.

In summary, the discussion confirms that graph-based simulation is a viable tool for microservice resilience analysis, complementing on-line chaos testing by narrowing down interesting scenarios to test in reality \cite{li_enjoy_observe}.

\section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclude}
This paper investigated a graph-based approach to estimating the resilience of a microservice application and evaluates its effectiveness on a complex social-network benchmark. We formulated the hypothesis that a simple dependency graph, subjected to simulated failures, can accurately predict the real system's behavior under similar failures. Through a combination of Monte-Carlo modeling and controlled chaos experiments, we found strong support for the hypothesis: across 32 trials under 30\% outages the model's predictions were within 2.5 percentage points of the observed value in the no-replica setup (14\% relative error) and within 0.9 percentage points in the replicated setup ($\leq$ 3\% relative error), with an overall mean relative error of 8\%.

Our work contributes a validated methodology that allows engineers to assess and enhance microservice resilience \cite{meiklejohn_resilient_microserv,hui_unveiling_the_microservices}, without always resorting to expensive live experiments. This complements traditional testing by providing a theoretical baseline. In environments where running chaos experiments is difficult (e.g., in production with real users), this approach offers a low-risk alternative to glean resilience insights. Moreover, the model can systematically evaluate many combinations of failures (via simulation) far faster than one could trial them on a real deployment.

There are several avenues to extend this research. First, applying the approach to other microservice case studies would further validate its generality. Second, we aim to incorporate latency and partial failures into the model \cite{zhang_mqsim, huang_grey_failure, grottke_fighting_bugs_remove}. Third, we intend to explore optimization formulations, such as optimal replica allocation under resource constraints \cite{beyer_the_site_reliability}. Fourth, we could model resiliency patterns like circuit breakers \cite{shekhar_microserv_design,fowler_circuitbreaker}. From a tooling perspective, automating the entire pipeline into a “Resilience Analyzer” tool would be beneficial \cite{microsoft_resilienc_pattern,humble_continuous_delivery_reliable}.

This research demonstrates that static dependency modeling, when combined with probabilistic simulation, can closely approximate real-system resilience under failure, offering an efficient alternative or complement to empirical fault testing. The microservice paradigm, with its clear modular structure, lends itself to this type of modeling. Our findings support the continued use and development of analytical and simulation-based approaches in tandem with empirical chaos engineering to achieve robust, resilient microservice systems \cite{zhong_refactoring_microservices,dullmann2017model,kleppmann_designing_data,lungu_current_trends_in,el_exploring_the_potential}.

