\begin{abstract}
This paper is devoted to time-consistent control problems of portfolio selection with strictly monotone mean-variance preferences.
These preferences are variational modifications of the conventional mean-variance preferences, and remain time-inconsistent as in mean-variance optimization problems.
To tackle the time-inconsistency, we study the Nash equilibrium controls of both the open-loop type and the closed-loop type, and characterize them within a random parameter setting.
The problem is reduced to solving a flow of forward-backward stochastic differential equations for open-loop equilibria, and to solving extended Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman equations for closed-loop equilibria.
In particular, we derive semi-closed-form solutions for these two types of equilibria under a deterministic parameter setting.
Both solutions are represented by the same function, which is independent of wealth state and random path. 
This function can be expressed as the conventional time-consistent mean-variance portfolio strategy multiplied by a factor greater than one. 
Furthermore, we find that the state-independent closed-loop Nash equilibrium control is a strong equilibrium strategy in a constant parameter setting only when the interest rate is sufficiently large.
\end{abstract}

\noindent {\bf Keywords:} monotone mean-variance preference, portfolio selection, time-consistency, Nash equilibrium control, non-linear law-dependent reference
\vspace{3mm}

\noindent {\bf AMS2010 classification:} Primary: 91G10, 49N10; Secondary: 91B05, 49N90
\vspace{3mm}


\section{Introduction}

The study of mean-variance (MV) problem has been going on for decades, and has become an important part of stochastic control and mathematical finance.
It is no exaggeration to say that it has created plenty of applications for portfolio selection in theory and practice.
There are many notable milestones among the extensive literature on this topic. 
For instance, \cite{Markowitz-1952} pioneers the application of the MV analysis to single-period portfolio selection.  
Subsequent advancements include the works of \cite{Li-Ng-2000} and \cite{Zhou-Li-2000}, who propose the embedding method to solve dynamic MV optimization problems within a discrete or continuous-time framework. 
Furthermore, \cite{Maccheroni-Marinacci-Rustichini-Taboga-2009} introduce the monotone mean-variance (MMV) preference, addressing the lack of monotonicity in traditional MV preferences.

As shown in \cite{Maccheroni-Marinacci-Rustichini-Taboga-2009}, the conventional MV preference does not necessarily provide the same rational selection as that from state-wise dominance.
Intuitively speaking, in many situations, the MV preference suggests choosing a strategy that is obviously worse, because the variance causes the penalty for positive deviations of a random return.
To fix this drawback, \cite{Maccheroni-Marinacci-Rustichini-Taboga-2009} apply the theory in their earlier work \cite{Maccheroni-Marinacci-Rustichini-2006} to make a minor modification on the variational representation of the conventional MV preference, 
thereby formulating the monotone mean-variance (MMV) preference. 
\cite{Strub-Li-2020} have shown that once the asset prices evolve as continuous semi-martingales, the MMV portfolio optimization problem yields the same solution as the corresponding MV problem.
Specific examples illustrating this equivalence can be found in \cite{Trybula-Zawisza-2019,Cerny-2020,Shen-Zou-2022,Hu-Shi-Xu-2023}.
More recent studies have investigated MMV portfolio problems under jump-diffusion models, such as those in \cite{Li-Guo-2021,Li-Guo-Tian-2023,Li-Liang-Pang-2022,Li-Liang-Pang-2023}, 
revealing that market discontinuities can introduce differences between the solutions of the MV and MMV portfolio problems. 

Although the MMV preferences have been widely explored, \cite{Wang-Chen-2025} find that the MMV preferences do not consistently withstand repeated testing.
More concretely, for the example given in \cite[Section 1]{Maccheroni-Marinacci-Rustichini-Taboga-2009}, the MMV preference suggests that the two choices are indistinguishable,
but every rational agent would make the same choice due to the presence of strict state-wise dominance. 
In other words, if numerous rational agents have encountered this selection problem or if a single rational agent is expected to address it repeatedly, the resulting choice will deviate from that in \cite{Maccheroni-Marinacci-Rustichini-Taboga-2009}. 
See details in \cite[Section 1]{Wang-Chen-2025}.
To handle this drawback, \cite{Wang-Chen-2025} make a further minor modification on the MMV preference and obtain a class of strictly monotone mean-variance (SMMV) preferences.
The corresponding single-period and continuous-time portfolio optimization problems are discussed therein.
It is found that there is only a subclass of the SMMV preferences with which the continuous-time portfolio problem has the same solution as the primal MV problem, even though the asset price processes are given by the classical Black-Scholes model. 

In this paper, we focus on the time-inconsistency in the SMMV portfolio selection problems.
Like MV problems, the objective function for evaluating an SMMV preference is not standard for applying dynamic programming principle, and the optimal feedback control explicitly depends on initial time-state pairs.
As a consequence, an investor with MV/MMV/SMMV preferences is motivated to realize the optimality from present to future by changing her/his previous optimal plan.
In the same manner, some incarnation of her/his future selves also has motivation to deviate from the current best plan.
The study of such time-inconsistency began decades ago, which can be traced back to \cite{Strotz-1955,Pollak-1968,Peleg-Yaari-1973,Goldman-1980}.
In recent years, the game-theoretical perspective and spike variation method are adopted to tackle the time-inconsistency in dynamic maximization problems.
The objective of a time-consistent control problem is no longer to realize a maximum, but to find a subgame perfect Nash equilibrium point for the game between the agent and the incarnation of her/his future selves.
Any spike deviation from this equilibrium at any time instant will be worse off, so that no one has incentive to deviate from the equilibrium strategy.
In particular, the equilibrium strategy is called ``open-loop Nash equilibrium control'' (resp. ``closed-loop Nash equilibrium control''), if the spike perturbation is applied to stochastic process (resp. feedback function) of control.
For MV portfolio problems, the Nash equilibrium controls of both the open-loop and closed-loop types have been well studied, 
see, e.g., \cite{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012,Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017} for the open-loop type and \cite{Bjork-Murgoci-Zhou-2014,Bjork-Khapko-Murgoci-2017} for the closed-loop type.
The main objective of this paper is also to characterize the Nash equilibrium controls of these two types for SMMV portfolio problems.
It is worth noting that interested readers can set $\zeta = 0$ in this paper to obtain the result for time-consistent control problems with MMV preferences, which to our knowledge have not been studied elsewhere yet.

However, the mathematical definition of the abovementioned Nash equilibrium controls cannot ensure that the spike deviation is strictly worse off.
In other words, the equilibrium does not necessarily correspond to a maximum as mentioned in \cite[Remark 3.5]{Bjork-Khapko-Murgoci-2017}.
Once the equilibrium is merely a saddle point, some deviations from it are profitable in the discrete-time approximation with some time lattice.
In this situation, the corresponding Nash equilibrium control is not considered as a strong equilibrium strategy.
\cite{He-Jiang-2021} investigate whether some classical time-consistent control problems have strong equilibrium strategies in the closed-loop Nash equilibrium, and find that the answer relies on the range of spike deviations.
For the conventional MV portfolio problem, the time-consistent strategy is not a strong equilibrium strategy, if the spike deviation is allowed to be a general feedback function.
In contrast, the answer changes to yes if the spike deviation is restricted to be constant in the sufficiently short period.
In this paper, we also investigate whether the Nash equilibrium controls for the SMMV portfolio problem are strong equilibrium strategies, when all the parameters and the spike deviation are constant.

Summing up, we formulate and solve time-consistent portfolio selection problems with SMMV preferences in this paper.
The main contributions of this paper are as follows.
\emph{Firstly}, for fairly general random parameters, we reduce seeking an open-loop Nash equilibrium control to solving a flow of forward-backward stochastic differential equations (FBSDEs); see \Cref{thm: verification theorem for ONEC}.
To this end, we refer to \cite{Liang-Xia-Yuan-2023} to re-express the SMMV preference as a non-linear law-dependent preference, and then make an argument of asymptotic estimate. 
\emph{Secondly}, we reduce seeking a closed-loop Nash equilibrium control to solving a system of extended Hamilton-Jacobi-Bellman (HJB) equations; see \Cref{thm: verification theorem for CNEC}. 
Due to the presence of randomness of parameters, the system contains backward stochastic partial differential equations (BSPDEs). 
\emph{Thirdly}, for deterministic parameters, we derive the semi-closed-form expressions for the Nash equilibrium controls of both the open-loop and closed-loop types; see \Cref{thm: path-independent ONEC,thm: state-independent CNEC}.
It is interesting to find that with deterministic parameters, these two types of controls deliver identical solutions, which are independent of random path and wealth state.
Moreover, the solution can be expressed as the conventional time-consistent MV portfolio strategy multiplied by a factor greater than $1$.
\emph{Finally}, for constant parameters and spike deviation, we show that the abovementioned state-independent closed-loop Nash equilibrium control is not a strong equilibrium strategy unless the interest rate is sufficiently large,
while the abovementioned path-independent open-loop Nash equilibrium control is necessarily a strong equilibrium strategy; see \Cref{thm: equilibrium value function and strong equilibria} and the consequential discussion therein.

The rest of this paper is organized as follows.
In \Cref{sec: Model and problem formulation}, we introduce the dynamic model and formulate our control problems.
In \Cref{sec: Characterization for ONEC,sec: Characterization for CNEC}, we characterize the open-loop and the closed-loop Nash equilibrium control, respectively.
For deterministic parameters, the semi-closed-form solution of the both two types are derived therein.
In \Cref{sec: Numerical example}, we provide some numerical results with constant parameters.
In \Cref{sec: Concluding remark}, we make a brief concluding remark.


\section{Model and problem formulation}
\label{sec: Model and problem formulation}

Let $T \in \mathbb{R}_{+} := ( 0, + \infty )$ be a fixed time horizon, and $\{ {W}_{t} \}_{ t \in [0,T ] }$ be a one-dimensional standard Brownian motion on a complete filtered probability space $( \Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{F}, \mathbb{P} )$, 
where $\mathbb{F} := \{ \mathcal{F}_{t} \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ] }$ is the right-continuous and complete natural filtration generated by $W$.
Denote the expectation operator by $\mathbb{E}$, and write $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ \cdot ] := \mathbb{E} [ \cdot | \mathcal{F}_{t} ]$.
In addition, we let $\mathcal{B} ( \mathbb{R} )$ be the Borel $\sigma $-algebra generated by all open intervals in the real line $\mathbb{R}$,
$\mathcal{P}$ be the Banach space of all finite signed measures on $( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}, \mathcal{B} ( \mathbb{R} ) \times \mathcal{B} ( \mathbb{R} ) )$,
and $\mathbb{P}^{t}_{X,Z} \in \mathcal{P}$ be the regular conditional law of random variables $( X,Z )$ given $\mathcal{F}_{t}$ under $\mathbb{P}$, so that $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ f ( X,Z ) ] = \int_{\Omega } f ( x,z ) \mathbb{P}^{t}_{X,Z} ( dx, dz )$.
For ease of reference, the functional spaces employed in this paper are listed below. Here $( n,m )$ are non-negative integers, $( t,p ) \in [ 0,T ) \times [ 1, \infty )$ and $\mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathbb{R}$.
\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathbb{L}^{p}_{ \mathcal{F}_{t} } ( \Omega; \mathcal{R} )$ denotes the set of all $\mathcal{F}_{t}$-measurable random variables $f: \Omega \to \mathcal{R}$ with $\mathbb{E} [ | f |^{p} ] < \infty $.
\item $\mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( t,T ; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega; \mathcal{R} ) )$ denotes the set of all $\mathbb{F}$-progressively measurable processes $f: [ t,T ] \times \Omega \to \mathcal{R}$ 
      with $\mathbb{E} [ ( \int_{t}^{T} | f ( s, \cdot ) |^{2} ds )^{p/2} ] < \infty $.
\item ${C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathcal{R} ) )$ denotes the set of all sample continuous processes in $\mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T ; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathcal{R} ) )$.
\item ${C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{\infty } ( \Omega; \mathcal{R} ) )$ denotes the set of all essentially bounded processes in ${C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathcal{R} ) )$. 
\item ${C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{n,m} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathcal{R} ) ) )$ denotes the set of all random fields $f: [ 0,T ] \times \Omega \times \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathcal{R}$ 
      such that $f ( \cdot, \cdot, x,y ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathcal{R} ) )$ 
                and $f ( t, \omega, x,y )$ is continuously differentiable in $x$ (resp. $y$) up to the $n$-th order (resp. $m$-th order) for every $t \in [ 0,T )$ and $\mathbb{P}$-a.e. $\omega \in \Omega $.
\item $\mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{n,m} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathcal{R} ) ) )$ denotes the set of all random fields $f: [ 0,T ] \times \Omega \times \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathcal{R}$ 
      such that $f ( \cdot, \cdot, x,y ) \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T ; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathcal{R} ) )$ 
                and $f ( t, \omega, x,y )$ is continuously differentiable in $x$ (resp. $y$) up to the $n$-th order (resp. $m$-th order) for every $t \in [ 0,T )$ and $\mathbb{P}$-a.e. $\omega \in \Omega $.
\end{itemize}
For the sake of brevity, hereafter we write ${f}_{t} = f ( t, \cdot )$, $f ( t,x ) = f ( t, \cdot, x )$ and so on, and omit the statement of ``$\mathbb{P}$-a.s.'', unless otherwise mentioned.

Suppose that there is a bond and a stock in the financial market, the price dynamics of which evolve according to the following stochastic differential equations (SDEs):
\begin{equation*}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
& d {B}_{t} = {B}_{t} {r}_{t} dt, && s.t. \quad {B}_{0} = {b}_{0} > 0 ~ \text{fixed a priori}; \\
& d {S}_{t} = {S}_{t} {r}_{t} dt + {S}_{t} {\sigma }_{t} ( d {W}_{t} + {\vartheta }_{t} dt ), && s.t. \quad {S}_{0} = {s}_{0} > 0 ~ \text{fixed a priori}.
\end{aligned} \right.
\end{equation*}
Here $( r, \sigma, \vartheta )$ denote the processes of the interest rate, volatility and risk premium. 
Each of them is assumed to be of ${C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{\infty } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R}_{+} ) )$.
Notably, the parameters are allowed to rely on the bond/stock price explicitly. For instance, ${r}_{t}$ could be a simplified form of $r ( t, \omega, B ( t, \omega ), S ( t, \omega ) )$.
Corresponding to the investment amount process $\pi \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T ; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ on the stock,
the wealth dynamics of a ``small'' investor is supposed to evolve as
\begin{equation}
d {X}^{\pi }_{t} = \frac{ {\pi }_{t} }{ {S}_{t} } d {S}_{t} + \frac{ {X}^{\pi }_{t} - {\pi }_{t} }{ {B}_{t} } d {B}_{t}
                 = {X}^{\pi }_{t} {r}_{t} dt + {\pi }_{t} {\sigma }_{t} ( d {W}_{t} + {\vartheta }_{t} dt ),
\label{eq: controlled SDE with open-loop control :eq}
\end{equation}  
with the initial amount ${X}^{\pi }_{0} = {x}_{0}$ fixing a priori.
Additionally, in order to formulate feedback control problems, we introduce the following controlled SDEs indexed by the initial pair $( t,x ) \in [ 0,T ) \times \mathbb{R}$:
\begin{equation}
d {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{s} = {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{s} {r}_{s} ds + \Pi ( s, {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{s} ) {\sigma }_{s} ( d {W}_{s} + {\vartheta }_{s} ds ), \quad s.t. \quad {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{t} = x.
\label{eq: controlled SDE with closed-loop control :eq}
\end{equation}  
Thus, $\Pi $ represents a feedback random field such that ${\pi }_{t} = \Pi ( t, {X}^{ 0, {x}_{0}, \Pi }_{t} )$.
Denote by $\mathcal{U}$ the set of all the feedback random fields $\Pi: [ 0,T ] \times \Omega \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that 
$\{ \Pi ( t, {X}^{ 0, {x}_{0}, \Pi }_{t} ) \}_{ t \in [ 0,T ] } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T ; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$. 

Referring to \cite{Wang-Chen-2025}, for investors with SMMV preferences indexed by $( \theta, \zeta ) \in \mathbb{R}_{+} \times \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; [ 0, \infty ) )$ and $\mathbb{E} [ \zeta ] < 1$,
the primal objective is to maximize the following infimum:
\begin{align}
& \inf_{ Y \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ), Y \ge \zeta } 
  \bigg\{ \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} Y ] 
        - \inf_{ f \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) } 
          \Big\{ \mathbb{E} [ f Y ] - \Big( \mathbb{E} [ f ] - \frac{\theta }{2} \mathbb{E} [ ( f - \mathbb{E} [ f ] )^{2} ] \Big) \Big\} \bigg\} \notag \\
& = \theta \mathbb{E} \bigg[ \int_{ - \infty }^{ {\lambda }_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \theta, \zeta } } s {1}_{\{ {X}^{\pi }_{T} + \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \le s \}} ds \bigg]
  + \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} \zeta ] + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } \mathbb{E} [ {\zeta }^{2} ] - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta },
\label{eq: SMMV preference at initial epoch :eq}
\end{align}
where the deterministic scalar ${\lambda }_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \theta, \zeta }$ is the unique solution of $1 = \mathbb{E} [ \zeta + \theta ( {\lambda }_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \theta, \zeta } - {X}^{\pi }_{T} - \zeta / \theta )_{+} ]$.
In fact, \cref{eq: SMMV preference at initial epoch :eq} is a monotone modification of the MV preference 
\begin{align*}
& \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] - \frac{\theta }{2} \mathbb{E} [ ( {X}^{\pi }_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] )^{2} ] \\
& = \inf_{ Y \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) } 
    \bigg\{ \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} Y ] 
          - \inf_{ f \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) } 
            \Big\{ \mathbb{E} [ f Y ] - \Big( \mathbb{E} [ f ] - \frac{\theta }{2} \mathbb{E} [ ( f - \mathbb{E} [ f ] )^{2} ] \Big) \Big\} \bigg\},
\end{align*}
and $\zeta $ is the parameter for capturing the monotonicity.
For technical reasons, we let $\zeta \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; [ 0,1 ) )$, otherwise our objective functions cannot be well defined for $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ \zeta ] \ge 1$. 

By dynamic programming principle, an optimal portfolio strategy for \cref{eq: SMMV preference at initial epoch :eq} also maximizes
\begin{equation*}
\theta \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{ - \infty }^{ {\lambda }_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \theta, \zeta } } s {1}_{\{ {X}^{\pi }_{T} + \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \le s \}} ds \bigg]
+ \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} \zeta ] + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {\zeta }^{2} ] - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta }, \quad \forall t \in ( 0,T ).
\end{equation*}
However, by mirroring the derivation in \cite[Subsection 2.2]{Wang-Chen-2025} with the proof of Theorem 2.10 therein, 
one can obtain the SMMV preference conditioned on $\mathcal{F}_{t}$ as the following:
\begin{align}
  J ( t, \pi )
& := \inf_{ Y \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ), Y \ge \zeta } 
     \bigg\{ \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} Y ] 
           - \inf_{ f \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) } 
             \Big\{ \mathbb{E}_{t} [ f Y ] - \Big( \mathbb{E}_{t} [ f ] - \frac{\theta }{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} [ ( f - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ f ] )^{2} ] \Big) \Big\} \bigg\} \notag \\
& = \theta \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{ - \infty }^{ \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) } s {1}_{\{ {X}^{\pi }_{T} + \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \le s \}} ds \bigg]
  + \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} \zeta ] + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {\zeta }^{2} ] - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta },
\label{eq: SMMV preference at epoch t :eq}
\end{align}
where $\lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) \in ( \mathop{essinf} \{ {X}^{\pi }_{T} + \zeta / \theta \}, \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] +  1 / \theta ]$ is $\mathcal{F}_{t}$-measurable and uniquely solves
\begin{equation*}
1 = \mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ \zeta + \theta \Big( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - {X}^{\pi }_{T} - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big]
  \equiv \theta \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - \theta \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ]
       + \theta \mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ \Big( {X}^{\pi }_{T} + \frac{\zeta }{\theta } - \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) \Big)_{+} \Big].
\end{equation*}
In particular, for $t = T$, we have $\lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{T}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) = {X}^{\pi }_{T} + 1 / \theta $ and
\begin{equation*}
J ( T, \pi ) = {X}^{\pi }_{T}
             = \theta \int_{ - \infty }^{ \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{T}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) } s {1}_{\{ {X}^{\pi }_{T} + \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \le s \}} ds
             + {X}^{\pi }_{T} \zeta + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } {\zeta }^{2} - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta }.
\end{equation*}
Since $\lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } )$ is not necessarily identical to ${\lambda }_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \theta, \zeta }$,
an optimal portfolio strategy for \cref{eq: SMMV preference at initial epoch :eq} does not necessarily produce an optimal portfolio strategy for \cref{eq: SMMV preference at epoch t :eq};
that is, time-inconsistency emerges.

Alternatively, to clarify the time-inconsistency, we consider the case with $\zeta \equiv 0$ (i.e. the MMV preference in \cite{Maccheroni-Marinacci-Rustichini-Taboga-2009}) with deterministic $( r, \sigma, \vartheta )$.
It has been shown in \cite{Wang-Chen-2025} that 
the unique optimal MV portfolio strategy for $\mathbb{E} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] - \theta \mathbb{E} [ ( {X}^{\pi }_{T} - \mathbb{E} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] )^{2} ] / 2$ is the unique maximizer for \cref{eq: SMMV preference at initial epoch :eq}.
Similarly, the unique optimal strategy for $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] -\theta \mathbb{E}_{t} [ ( {X}^{\pi }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] )^{2} ] / 2$ 
is the unique maximizer for \cref{eq: SMMV preference at epoch t :eq} as well.
It is well-known that the abovementioned optimal MV portfolio strategies have time-inconsistency; see, e.g., \cite{Basak-Chabakauri-2010}.
Hence, the time-inconsistency remains for the optimal SMMV portfolio strategies.

To tackle the time-inconsistency, we formulate the control problem as a game among the present and future incarnations of the investor.
Referring to \cite{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012,Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017} for open-loop equilibria and \cite{Bjork-Khapko-Murgoci-2017} for closed-loop equilibria, we introduce the following definitions.

\begin{definition}\label{def: open-loop Nash equilibrium control}
$\bar{\pi } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T ; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ is an open-loop Nash equilibrium control (ONEC), if
\begin{equation}
0 \le \liminf_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0}
      \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \big( J ( t, \bar{\pi } ) - J ( t, \bar{\pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi } ) \big), \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ),
\label{eq:open-loop Nash equilibrium control :eq}
\end{equation}
for any $\xi \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{t} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} )$ satisfying $\bar{\pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T ; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$,
where $J ( t, \pi )$ has been defined by \cref{eq: SMMV preference at epoch t :eq}
and $\bar{\pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi }$ is a spike variation of $\bar{\pi }$ given by $\bar{\pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi }_{s} = \bar{\pi }_{s} + \xi {1}_{\{ s \in [ t, t + \varepsilon ) \}}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}\label{def: closed-loop Nash equilibrium control}
$\bar{\Pi } \in \mathcal{U}$ is a closed-loop Nash equilibrium control (CNEC), if
\begin{equation}
0 \le \liminf_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0}
      \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \big( \bar{J} ( t, {X}^{ 0, {x}_{0}, \bar{\Pi } }_{t}, \bar{\Pi } ) - \bar{J} ( t, {X}^{ 0, {x}_{0}, \bar{\Pi } }_{t}, \bar{\Pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi } ) \big), \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ),
\label{eq:closed-loop Nash equilibrium control :eq}
\end{equation}
for any $\xi \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{t} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} )$ satisfying $\bar{\Pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi } \in \mathcal{U}$,
where 
\begin{equation}
   \bar{J} ( t, x, \Pi )
:= \theta \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \int_{ - \infty }^{ \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) } s {1}_{\{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} + \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \le s \}} ds \bigg]
 + \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} \zeta ] + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {\zeta }^{2} ] - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta }
\label{eq: SMMV preference with closed-loop control :eq}
\end{equation}
with the $\mathcal{F}_{t}$-measurable random variable $\lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } )$ being the unique solution of 
\begin{equation*}
1 = \mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ \zeta + \theta \Big( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big],
\end{equation*}
and $\bar{\Pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi }$ is a spike variation of $\bar{\Pi }$ given by
$\bar{\Pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi } ( s,y ) = \bar{\Pi } ( s,y ) {1}_{\{ s \notin [ t, t + \varepsilon ) \}} + \xi {1}_{\{ s \in [ t, t + \varepsilon ) \}}$.
\end{definition}

In short, we aim to characterizing the abovementioned ONEC and CNEC in general, and find their explicit expressions in some specific cases.


\section{Characterization for ONEC}
\label{sec: Characterization for ONEC}

In this section, we transform the SMMV preferences to non-linear law-dependence preferences, and then reduce our control problem for ONEC to solving a flow of FBSDEs with an equilibrium condition.
First of all, we rewrite \cref{eq: SMMV preference at epoch t :eq} as 
\begin{align}
  J ( t, \pi ) 
& = \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) \theta \int_{ - \infty }^{ \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) } \mathbb{P} \Big( {X}^{\pi }_{T} + \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \le s \Big) ds \nonumber \\
& \quad - \theta \int_{ - \infty }^{ \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) } ( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - s ) \mathbb{P} \Big( {X}^{\pi }_{T} + \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \le s \Big) ds 
        + \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} \zeta ] + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {\zeta }^{2} ] - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } \nonumber \\
& = \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) ( 1 - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ \zeta ] )
  - \frac{\theta }{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \Big| \Big( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - {X}^{\pi }_{T} - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big|^{2} \bigg]
  + \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} \zeta ] + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {\zeta }^{2} ] - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta },
\label{eq: re-expression of SMMV preference :eq}
\end{align}
and denote by $\mathcal{P}_{0}$ the set of all regular conditional laws $p \in \mathcal{P}$ such that 
$\int_{ \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} } ( {x}^{2} + {z}^{2} ) p ( dx, dz ) < \infty $ and $\int_{ \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} } z p ( dx, dz ) < 1$.
Then, let us introduce the following non-linear functional defined on the convex set $\mathcal{P}_{0}$:
\begin{equation}
g (p) = \int_{ \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} } \bigg( \lambda (p) ( 1 - z ) - \frac{\theta }{2} \Big| \Big( \lambda (p) - x - \frac{z}{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big|^{2} + x z + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } ( {z}^{2} - 1 ) \bigg) p ( dx, dz ),
\label{eq: non-linear objective functional :eq}
\end{equation}
where the deterministic scalar $\lambda (p)$ is the solution of 
\begin{equation}
1 = \int_{ \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} } \bigg( z + \theta \Big( \lambda (p) - x -\frac{z}{\theta } \Big)_{+} \bigg) p ( dx, dz ),
\label{eq: lambda functional :eq}
\end{equation}
so that $J ( t, \pi ) = g ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } )$.
Notably, for any $p \in \mathcal{P}_{0}$, the existence and uniqueness of $\lambda (p)$,
as well as $\int_{\{ x + z / \theta < \lambda (p) \}} p ( dx, dz ) > 0$, can be seen from the following equivalent expression of \cref{eq: lambda functional :eq}:
\begin{equation}
0 < 1 - \int_{ \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} } z p ( dx, dz ) = \theta \int_{ - \infty }^{ \lambda (p) } ds \int_{\{ x + z / \theta \le s \}} p ( dx, dz ).
\label{eq: lambda functional equivalent expression :eq}
\end{equation}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: Gateaux differentiability of g}
For any $p,q \in \mathcal{P}_{0}$,
\begin{equation*}
  \lim_{ \varepsilon \downarrow 0 } \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \Big( g \big( p + \varepsilon (q-p) \big) - g (p) \Big) 
= d g ( p, q-p ) := \int_{ \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} } \nabla g ( p,x,z ) \big( q ( dx, dz ) - p ( dx, dz ) \big),
\end{equation*}
where $\nabla g : \mathcal{P}_{0} \times \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ is given by
\begin{equation*}
\nabla g ( p,x,z ) := \lambda (p) ( 1 - z ) - \frac{\theta }{2} \Big| \Big( \lambda (p) - x - \frac{z}{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big|^{2} + x z + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } ( {z}^{2} - 1 ).
\end{equation*}
Thus, $g: \mathcal{P}_{0} \to \mathbb{R}$ is continuously G\^ateaux differentiable, 
and $d g ( p, \cdot )$ as the G\^ateaux differential of $g$ at $p$ is a continuous linear functional determined by $\nabla g ( p, \cdot, \cdot )$,
which satisfies $\int_{ \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} } | \nabla g ( p,x,z ) | q ( dx, dz ) < \infty $ for any $p,q \in \mathcal{P}_{0}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: boundedness of gap}
For any $p,q \in \mathcal{P}_{0}$, $| g(q) - g(p) - d g ( p, q-p ) | \le | \lambda (q) - \lambda (p) |^{2} / 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: estimate for gap}
For any $( t, \chi ) \in [ 0,T ) \times \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{T} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} )$,
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{ \mathbb{E}_{t} [ \chi {1}_{ {R}_{1} } ] }{ \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {1}_{ {R}_{1} } ] }
\le \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}_{T} + \chi, \zeta } ) - \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}_{T}, \zeta } ) 
\le \frac{ \mathbb{E}_{t} [ \chi {1}_{ {R}_{0} } ] }{ \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {1}_{ {R}_{0} } ] },
\end{equation*}
where ${R}_{0} = \{ {X}_{T} + \zeta / \theta \le \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}_{T}, \zeta } ) \}$ and ${R}_{1} = \{ {X}_{T} + \chi + \zeta / \theta \le \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}_{T} + \chi, \zeta } ) \}$.
\end{lemma}

The proof of the above three lemmas can be found in Appendices \ref{pf-lem: Gateaux differentiability of g}--\ref{pf-lem: estimate for gap}.
Notably, we have shown that $g$ defined by \cref{eq: non-linear objective functional :eq} and \cref{eq: lambda functional :eq} satisfies \cite[Assumption 3.1]{Liang-Xia-Yuan-2023}, 
so that \cite[Theorem 3.2]{Liang-Xia-Yuan-2023} can be applied to arrive at sufficient conditions for ONEC.
In order to provide a sufficient and necessary condition for ONEC, we establish the following verification theorem, the proof of which is left to \Cref{pf-thm: verification theorem for ONEC}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: verification theorem for ONEC}
$\bar{\pi } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ is an ONEC, if and only if
\begin{equation}
\lim_{ \varepsilon \downarrow 0 } \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \int_{t}^{ t + \varepsilon } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ ( {Y}^{t}_{s} {\vartheta }_{s} + \mathcal{Y}^{t}_{s} ) {\sigma }_{s} ] ds = 0, \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ],
\label{eq: equilibrium condition for ONEC :eq}
\end{equation}
where the pair $( {Y}^{t}, \mathcal{Y}^{t} ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) ) \times \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ is the solution of the linear BSDE:
\begin{equation}
{Y}^{t}_{s} = \zeta + \theta \Big( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T}, \zeta } ) - {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} 
            + \int_{s}^{T} {Y}^{t}_{v} {r}_{v} dv - \int_{s}^{T} \mathcal{Y}^{t}_{v} d {W}_{v}.
\label{eq: BSDE :eq}
\end{equation} 
Consequently, the following claims for the sufficient conditions of ONEC arise:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $\bar{\pi } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ is an ONEC, if
\begin{equation*}
\lim_{ s \downarrow t } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ ( {Y}^{t}_{s} {\vartheta }_{s} + \mathcal{Y}^{t}_{s} ) {\sigma }_{s} ] = 0, \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ],
\end{equation*}
\item[(ii)] Assume that $\mathcal{Y}^{t}$ has right-continuous sample path for a.e. $t \in [ 0,T )$.
Then, $\bar{\pi } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ is an ONEC, if 
\begin{equation*}
{Y}^{t}_{t} {\vartheta }_{t} + \mathcal{Y}^{t}_{t} = 0, \quad \mathbb{P}-a.s., ~ a.e. ~ t \in [ 0,T ].
\end{equation*}
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{rem: corollary of verification theorem}
It follows from \cref{eq: BSDE :eq} that for any $\tau \in [ 0,s ] \subseteq [ 0,T ]$,
\begin{align*}
{Y}^{t}_{s} & = \mathbb{E}_{s} \bigg[ {e}^{ \int_{s}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \bigg( \zeta + \theta \Big( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T}, \zeta } ) - {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \bigg) \bigg] \\
            & = {e}^{ - \int_{\tau }^{s} {r}_{v} dv }
                \mathbb{E}_{\tau } \bigg[ {e}^{ \int_{\tau }^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \bigg( \zeta + \theta \Big( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T}, \zeta } ) - {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \bigg) \bigg]
              + {e}^{ - \int_{\tau }^{s} {r}_{v} dv } \int_{\tau }^{s} {e}^{ \int_{\tau }^{u} {r}_{v} dv } \mathcal{Y}^{t}_{u} d {W}_{u}.
\end{align*}
Assume (by contradiction) that ${X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} + \zeta / \theta \le \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T}, \zeta } )$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.,
which results in $\lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T}, \zeta } ) = \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} ] + 1 / \theta $ and ${X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} ] \le ( 1 - \zeta ) / \theta $, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.
Consequently,
\begin{align*}
& {Y}^{t}_{s} {e}^{ \int_{0}^{s} {r}_{v} dv } = ( 1 + \theta \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} ] ) \mathbb{E}_{s} \Big[ {e}^{ \int_{0}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \Big] 
                                              - \theta \mathbb{E}_{s} \Big[ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} {e}^{ \int_{0}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \Big], \\
& \mathcal{Y}^{t}_{s} {e}^{ \int_{0}^{s} {r}_{v} dv } = ( 1 + \theta \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} ] ) \mathscr{R}_{s} - \theta \mathscr{X}_{s},
\end{align*}
where the pair $( \mathscr{R}, \mathscr{X} ) \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( t,T ; \mathbb{L}^{p} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) ) \times \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( t,T ; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ 
with an arbitrarily fixed $p \ge 2$ arises from the martingale representations:
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ {e}^{ \int_{0}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \Big] = \mathbb{E} \Big[ {e}^{ \int_{0}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \Big] + \int_{0}^{t} \mathscr{R}_{s} d {W}_{s}, \quad
\mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} {e}^{ \int_{0}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \Big] = \mathbb{E} \Big[ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} {e}^{ \int_{0}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \Big] + \int_{0}^{t} \mathscr{X}_{s} d {W}_{s}.
\end{equation*}
Assume that $\mathscr{R}$ is essentially bounded on $[ 0,T ] \times \Omega $ (e.g. in the case with deterministic $r$).
Then, the integrand $( {Y}^{t}_{s} {\vartheta }_{s} + \mathcal{Y}^{t}_{s} ) {\sigma }_{s}$ in \cref{eq: equilibrium condition for ONEC :eq} satisfies the representation in \cite[Proposition 3.3]{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017}, 
and hence $\bar{\pi } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ is an ONEC if and only if ${Y}^{t}_{t} {\vartheta }_{t} + \mathcal{Y}^{t}_{t} = 0$, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s., a.e. $t \in [ 0,T ]$,
according to \cite[Lemma 3.4 and Theorem 3.5]{Hu-Jin-Zhou-2017}.
In the case with deterministic $r$, this condition gives 
\begin{align*}
  \theta ( {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} ] ) {e}^{ \int_{0}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } 
& = \theta \int_{t}^{T} \mathscr{X}_{s} d {W}_{s}
  = - \int_{t}^{T} \mathcal{Y}^{s}_{s} {e}^{ \int_{0}^{s} {r}_{v} dv } d {W}_{s} \\
& = \int_{t}^{T} {e}^{ \int_{0}^{s} {r}_{v} dv } {Y}^{s}_{s} {\vartheta }_{s} d {W}_{s}
  = {e}^{ \int_{0}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } \int_{t}^{T} {\vartheta }_{s} d {W}_{s},
\end{align*}
which does not necessarily satisfy ${X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ \bar{\pi } }_{T} ] \le ( 1 - \zeta ) / \theta $, $\mathbb{P}$-a.s.
\end{remark}

Although we have reduced our control problem to solving a flow of FBSDEs given by 
\cref{eq: controlled SDE with open-loop control :eq} (with $\pi = \bar{\pi }$ therein), \cref{eq: BSDE :eq} and the so-called equilibrium condition \cref{eq: equilibrium condition for ONEC :eq}, 
it is still difficult to arrive at a clear expression of some ONEC in general.
Fortunately, in the case with deterministic model parameters, we obtain an analytical expression of the ONEC that is a deterministic function of $t$.
This result is analogous to that in \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}, which implies that the preference affine in the mean may admit a path-independent ONEC (and an identical state-independent ONEC).
We isolate the result in the following theorem, the proof of which is left to \Cref{pf-thm: path-independent ONEC}.
Before proceeding, we let $\Phi : \mathbb{R} \to ( 0,1 )$ denote the cumulated distribution function of standard normal distribution, $\Psi : \mathbb{R}_{+} \to \mathbb{R}$ denote the inverse function of $f(x) = \int_{ - \infty }^{x} \Phi (z) dz$,
and $\Phi \circ \Psi ( \cdot ) := \Phi ( \Psi ( \cdot ) )$ for the sake of brevity.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: path-independent ONEC}
Assume that $( r, \sigma, \vartheta, \zeta )$ are deterministic.
Then, $\bar{\pi }$ is an ONEC if 
\begin{equation}
\bar{\pi }_{t} = \frac{ {\beta }_{t} {\vartheta }_{t} }{ \theta {\sigma }_{t} } {e}^{ - \int_{t}^{T} {r}_{v} dv }, \quad \forall t \in [ 0,T ],
\label{eq: ONEC :eq}
\end{equation} 
where the continuously differentiable and strictly decreasing function $\beta : [ 0,T ] \to [ 1, + \infty )$ is the unique solution of the following integral equation with ${\beta }_{T} = 1$ and ${\beta }_{T}' = 0$:
\begin{equation}
1 = {\beta }_{t} \Phi \circ \Psi \bigg( \frac{ 1 - \zeta }{ ( \int_{t}^{T} | {\beta }_{s} {\vartheta }_{s} |^{2} ds )^{ \frac{1}{2} } } \bigg), \quad t \in [ 0,T ).
\label{eq: beta equation :eq}
\end{equation}
Moreover, for all $t \in [ 0,T )$, ${\beta }_{t}$ is strictly increasing in $\zeta $.  
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}\label{rem: path-independent ONEC}
For $t \in [ 0,T )$, ${\beta }_{t} > 1$ implies that $\bar{\pi }_{t} > \bar{\pi }^{MV}_{t} := \exp ( - \int_{t}^{T} {r}_{v} dv ) {\vartheta }_{t} / ( {\sigma }_{t} \theta )$, 
where $\bar{\pi }^{MV}$ gives the time-consistent solution (including ONEC and CNEC) for the conventional MV preference $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] -\theta \mathbb{E}_{t} [ ( {X}^{\pi }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{\pi }_{T} ] )^{2} ] / 2$; 
see also \cite{Basak-Chabakauri-2010,Hu-Jin-Zhou-2012,Bjork-Khapko-Murgoci-2017}. 
And we observe that the equilibrium solution \cref{eq: ONEC :eq} is irrelevant with the wealth level, 
which aligns with the time-consistent solution under MV preferences, as shown in the aforementioned literature.
Since our SMMV preferences are monotone modifications of the MV preference, ${\beta }_{t}$ can be treated as the modification factor for the vanishing penalty on high positive deviations of investment return.
Intuitively speaking, the larger $\zeta $ is, the more those high positive deviations are encouraged, which leads to a larger equilibrium investment amount $\bar{\pi }_{t}$ via a larger ${\beta }_{t}$.
In addition, in the case with a constant risk premium $\vartheta $, \cref{eq: beta equation :eq} implies that ${\beta }_{t}$ is also strictly increasing in $\vartheta $, 
and hence a larger $\vartheta $ leads to a larger $\bar{\pi }_{t}$; that is, higher returns of risk attract more risky investments.
\end{remark}


\section{Characterization for CNEC}
\label{sec: Characterization for CNEC}

We have mentioned in \Cref{sec: Model and problem formulation} that the time-inconsistency arises from $\lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) \ne \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{0}_{ {X}^{\pi }_{T}, \zeta } )$.
Inspired by \cite{Yong-2012,Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} that characterize CNECs by fixing the time-inconsistency-causing terms as constants and taking Feynman-Kac formula for the resulting martingale, 
we introduce the following extended objective function indexed by $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$:
\begin{equation}
   \tilde{J} ( t,x, \Pi, \lambda )
:= \lambda ( 1 - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ \zeta ] )
 - \frac{\theta }{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \Big| \Big( \lambda - {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big|^{2} \bigg]
 + \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} \zeta ] + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {\zeta }^{2} ] - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta },
\label{eq: extended preference with closed-loop control :eq}
\end{equation}
so that $\bar{J} ( t,x, \Pi ) = \tilde{J} ( t,x, \Pi, \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) )$ follows from \cref{eq: SMMV preference with closed-loop control :eq} and \cref{eq: re-expression of SMMV preference :eq}.
As an analog to \cite[Theorem 3.2]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025}, we have the following verification theorem to characterize CNECs for our control problem, the proof of which is left to \Cref{pf-thm: verification theorem for CNEC}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: verification theorem for CNEC}
$\bar{\Pi } \in \mathcal{U}$ is a CNEC, if the following conditions hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item There is a random field pair 
      \begin{equation*}
      ( \mathcal{V}, \psi ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{2,1} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathbb{R} ) ) ) 
                         \times \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{2,1} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathbb{R} ) ) )
      \end{equation*}
      fulfilling the following BSPDE on $[ 0,T ) \times \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}$ with terminal condition on $\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}$:
      \begin{equation}
      \left\{ \begin{aligned}
      - d \mathcal{V} ( t,x, \lambda )
      & = \bigg( \frac{1}{2} \mathcal{V}_{xx} ( t,x, \lambda ) | \bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) {\sigma }_{t} |^{2} 
               + {\psi }_{x} ( t,x, \lambda ) \bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) {\sigma }_{t} \\
      & \qquad + \mathcal{V}_{x} ( t,x, \lambda ) \big( {r}_{t} x + \bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) {\sigma }_{t} {\vartheta }_{t} \big) \bigg) dt
        - \psi ( t,x, \lambda ) d {W}_{t}, \\
          \mathcal{V} ( T,x, \lambda ) 
      & = \lambda ( 1 - \zeta ) - \frac{\theta }{2} \Big| \Big( \lambda - x - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big|^{2} + \zeta x + \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } {\zeta }^{2} - \frac{1}{ 2 \theta }.
      \end{aligned} \right.
      \label{eq: BSPDE :eq}
      \end{equation}
\item There is a random field pair 
      \begin{equation*}
      ( \mathcal{M}, \phi ) \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{2,0} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathbb{R} ) ) ) 
                         \times \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{2,0} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathbb{R} ) ) )
      \end{equation*}
      fulfilling the following BSPDE on $[ 0,T ) \times \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}$ with the terminal condition on $\mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}$:
      \begin{equation}
      \left\{ \begin{aligned}
      - d \mathcal{M} ( t,x, \lambda )
      & = \bigg( \frac{1}{2} \mathcal{M}_{xx} ( t,x, \lambda ) | \bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) {\sigma }_{t} |^{2} 
               + {\phi }_{x} ( t,x, \lambda ) \bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) {\sigma }_{t} \\
      & \qquad + \mathcal{M}_{x} ( t,x, \lambda ) \big( {r}_{t} x + \bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) {\sigma }_{t} {\vartheta }_{t} \big) \bigg) dt
        - \phi ( t,x, \lambda ) d {W}_{t}, \\
          \mathcal{M} ( T, x, \lambda ) 
      & = \zeta + \theta \Big( \lambda - x - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+}.
      \end{aligned} \right.
      \label{eq: BSPDE of calM :eq}
      \end{equation}
\item $\{ f ( s, {X}^{ t,x, \bar{\Pi } }_{s}, \lambda ) \}_{ s \in [ t,T ] } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( t,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ for $f = \mathcal{V}_{x}, \psi, \mathcal{M}_{x}, \phi $ and any $( t,x, \lambda )$.
\item With $\Lambda ( t,x )$ satisfying $1 = \mathcal{M} ( t,x, \Lambda ( t,x ) )$,
      \begin{equation}
      \bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) \in \mathop{argmax}_{ \pi \in \mathbb{R} } \Big\{ \frac{1}{2} \mathcal{V}_{xx} \big( t,x, \Lambda ( t,x ) \big) | \pi {\sigma }_{t} |^{2} 
                                                                 + \Big( \mathcal{V}_{x} \big( t,x, \Lambda ( t,x ) \big) {\vartheta }_{t} + {\psi }_{x} \big( t,x, \Lambda ( t,x ) \big) \Big) \pi {\sigma }_{t} \Big\}.
      \label{eq: equilibrium condition for CNEC :eq}
      \end{equation}  
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

In fact, the BSPDEs \cref{eq: BSPDE :eq} and \cref{eq: BSPDE of calM :eq} with the equilibrium condition \cref{eq: equilibrium condition for CNEC :eq} form the so-called ``extended HJB equations'' or ``extended HJB system''.
In the case with deterministic parameters, the following theorem gives a state-independent CNEC identical to the path-independent ONEC that we have derived in \Cref{thm: path-independent ONEC}, and the so-called equilibrium value function. 
The corresponding proof is left to \Cref{pf-thm: state-independent CNEC}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: state-independent CNEC}
Assume that $( r, \sigma, \vartheta, \zeta )$ are deterministic.
Then, $\bar{\Pi }$ is a CNEC if 
\begin{equation}
\bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) = \frac{ {\beta }_{t} {\vartheta }_{t} }{ \theta {\sigma }_{t} } {e}^{ - \int_{t}^{T} {r}_{v} dv }, \quad \forall t \in [ 0,T ],
\label{eq: CNEC :eq}
\end{equation}
where $\beta $ is given by \cref{eq: beta equation :eq} with ${\beta }_{T} = 1$.
Furthermore, corresponding to this CNEC, 
\begin{align*}
\bar{J} ( t,x, \bar{\Pi } ) & = x {e}^{ \int_{t}^{T} {r}_{v} dv } - \frac{ ( 1 - \zeta )^{2} }{ 2 \theta }
                              + \frac{1}{\theta } \int_{t}^{T} {\beta }_{s} | {\vartheta }_{s} |^{2} ds \\
                            & \quad + \frac{1}{\theta } \int_{t}^{T} | {\beta }_{s} {\vartheta }_{s} |^{2} ds \int_{0}^{ \frac{ 1 - \zeta }{ ( \int_{t}^{T} | {\beta }_{s} {\vartheta }_{s} |^{2} ds )^{\frac{1}{2}} } } \Psi(y) dy.
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
It is found that the CNEC (\Cref{thm: state-independent CNEC}) and the ONEC (\Cref{thm: path-independent ONEC}) are the same, which is similar to the finding in \cite[Section 5]{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025},
where the preference is a fairly general function of finitely many higher-order central moments of ${X}_{T}$ and is affine in $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}_{T} ]$.
Heuristically, here we show that \cref{eq: SMMV preference with closed-loop control :eq} with a deterministic $\zeta $ can be roughly re-expressed as 
an $\mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ]$-affine objective function with higher-order central moments of ${X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}$.
In fact, $\lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ]$ is a function of higher-order central moments of ${X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}$, since
\begin{equation*}
1 = \zeta + \theta \mathbb{E}_{t} \Big[ \Big( \big( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ] \big) 
                                            - ( {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ] ) - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big].
\end{equation*}
The assumption that the distribution of ${X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ]$ is determined by all of its moments is presupposed.
Moreover, it follows from \cref{eq: extended preference with closed-loop control :eq} with $\bar{J} ( t,x, \Pi ) = \tilde{J} ( t,x, \Pi, \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) )$ that 
\begin{align*}
    \bar{J} ( t, x, \Pi )
& = \frac{1}{ 2 \theta } ( {\zeta }^{2} - 1 )
  + \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ] 
  + ( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ] ) ( 1 - \zeta ) \\
& \quad - \frac{\theta }{2} \mathbb{E}_{t} \bigg[ \Big| \Big( \big( \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}, \zeta } ) - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ] \big) 
                                                            - ( {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ] ) - \frac{\zeta }{\theta } \Big)_{+} \Big|^{2} \bigg].
\end{align*}
That is, $\bar{J} ( t, x, \Pi ) - \mathbb{E}_{t} [ {X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T} ]$ is also a function of higher-order central moments of ${X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}$.
However, it is still difficult to apply the result of \cite{Wang-Liu-Bensoussan-Yiu-Wei-2025} to derive ONECs and CNECs for our control problem, 
as the abovementioned functions of higher-order central moments of ${X}^{ t,x, \Pi }_{T}$ is implicit and even complex in general.
\end{remark}

For a fairly general random parameter setting, the following theorem shows that a state-independent CNEC does necessarily produce an ONEC under additional smoothness and integrability assumptions.
The corresponding proof is placed in \Cref{pf-thm: identical trivial NECs}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: identical trivial NECs}
Suppose that for $\bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) \equiv \bar{\pi }_{t}$ on $[ 0,T ] \times \Omega \times \mathbb{R}$ with $\bar{\pi } \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$,
there are random field pairs 
\begin{equation*}
\left\{ \begin{aligned}
( \mathcal{V}, \psi ) & \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{3,1} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathbb{R} ) ) ) 
                     \times {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{3,1} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathbb{R} ) ) ), \\
( \mathcal{M}, \phi ) & \in {C}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{2,0} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathbb{R} ) ) ) 
                     \times \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( 0,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; {C}^{2,0} ( \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}; \mathbb{R} ) ) ),
\end{aligned} \right.
\end{equation*}
fulfilling \cref{eq: BSPDE :eq}, \cref{eq: BSPDE of calM :eq} and 
\begin{equation}
0 = \mathcal{V}_{xx} \big( t,x, \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \bar{\Pi } }_{T}, \zeta } ) \big) \bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) {\sigma }_{t} 
  + \mathcal{V}_{x} \big( t,x, \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \bar{\Pi } }_{T}, \zeta } ) \big) {\vartheta }_{t} 
  + {\psi }_{x} \big( t,x, \lambda ( \mathbb{P}^{t}_{ {X}^{ t,x, \bar{\Pi } }_{T}, \zeta } ) \big).
\label{eq: equilibrium condition for trivial NEC :eq}
\end{equation}
In addition, assume that $\{ f ( s, {X}^{ t,x, \bar{\Pi } }_{s}, \lambda ) \}_{ s \in [ t,T ] } \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{\mathbb{F}} ( t,T; \mathbb{L}^{2} ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} ) )$ 
for $f = \mathcal{V}, \psi, \mathcal{V}_{x}, {\psi }_{x}, \mathcal{M}, \phi $ and any $( t,x, \lambda )$.
Then, $\bar{\Pi }$ is a CNEC, and $\bar{\pi }$ is an ONEC.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
If $\bar{\Pi } ( t,x ) \equiv \bar{\pi }_{t}$ on $[ 0,T ] \times \Omega \times \mathbb{R}$ provides a CNEC, then \Cref{def: closed-loop Nash equilibrium control} gives 
\begin{equation*}
0 \le \liminf_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0}
      \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \big( J ( t, \bar{\pi } ) - J ( t, \bar{\pi } {1}_{ [ t, t + \varepsilon ) } + \xi {1}_{ [ t, t + \varepsilon ) } ) \big).
\end{equation*}
However, this statement does not necessarily lead to
\begin{equation*}
0 \le \liminf_{\varepsilon \downarrow 0}
      \frac{1}{\varepsilon } \big( J ( t, \bar{\pi } ) - J ( t, \bar{\pi } + \xi {1}_{ [ t, t + \varepsilon ) } ) \big)
\end{equation*}
as in \Cref{def: open-loop Nash equilibrium control} for ONEC, due to the difference in spike variation.
\end{remark}

On the other hand, taking advantage of the path/state-independence, we can show whether a Nash equilibrium control is a strong equilibrium strategy by straightforward calculation,
which is much more clear than the characterization given by \cite{He-Jiang-2021}.
We consider the case with constant parameters, and arrive at the following theorem, the proof of which can be found in \Cref{pf-thm: equilibrium value function and strong equilibria}.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm: equilibrium value function and strong equilibria}
Assume that $( r, \sigma, \vartheta, \zeta )$ are constant, and $( \bar{\pi }, \bar{\Pi } )$ are the ONEC and CNEC given by \Cref{thm: path-independent ONEC,thm: state-independent CNEC}, respectively.  
Then, for any $t \in [ 0,T )$, $\xi \in \mathbb{L}^{2}_{ \mathcal{F}_{t} } ( \Omega; \mathbb{R} )$ and sufficiently small $( t, \xi )$-dependent $\varepsilon $,
\begin{itemize}
\item $\bar{J} ( t,x, \bar{\Pi } ) > \bar{J} ( t,x, \bar{\Pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi } )$ if $r > | {\beta }_{t}' | / {\beta }_{t}$;
\item $\bar{J} ( t,x, \bar{\pi } ) > \bar{J} ( t,x, \bar{\pi }^{ t, \varepsilon, \xi } )$.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\Cref{thm: equilibrium value function and strong equilibria} shows that the CNEC given by \Cref{thm: state-independent CNEC} is a strong equilibrium strategy under the condition $r > \max_{ t \in [ 0,T ] } | {\beta }_{t}' / {\beta }_{t} |$.
That is, any spike deviation as in \Cref{def: closed-loop Nash equilibrium control} from the closed-loop equilibria given by \Cref{thm: state-independent CNEC} will be strictly worse off, only when the interest rate $r$ is sufficiently large.
In comparison, any spike deviation as in \Cref{def: open-loop Nash equilibrium control} from the open-loop equilibria given by \Cref{thm: path-independent ONEC} must be strictly worse off.


\section{Numerical example}
\label{sec: Numerical example}

This section illustrates the investment strategies given by \Cref{thm: path-independent ONEC,thm: state-independent CNEC}, and compares them with those under the MV and MMV preferences.
Assume that the parameters $( r, \sigma, \vartheta, \zeta )$ are constant. 
The parameter values are taken from \cite{Brennan-Xia-2002}, who calibrate asset price models using monthly value-weighted stock returns from January 1970 to December 1995, obtained from the Center for Research in Security Prices. 
We set $r = 0.012$, $\sigma = 0.158$, $\vartheta = 0.343$, and consider an investment horizon of $25$ years, i.e. $T = 25$. 
Furthermore, we assume that the investor's risk aversion is characterized by $\theta = 5$. 
A higher value of $\theta $ indicates greater aversion to negative deviations of terminal wealth from its expectation. 

\begin{figure}[htbp!]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=3in]{pi_plot}
	\caption{Equilibrium investment amount in the risky asset with different preferences. 
             The parameters are assumed to be deterministic, with $r = 0.012$, $\sigma = 0.158$, $\vartheta = 0.343$, $T = 25$ and $\theta = 5$. 
             $\zeta$ varies from 0 to 0.7.}
	\label{fig: pi plot}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htbp!]
	\centering
	\includegraphics[width=3in]{pi_vartheta_plot}
	\caption{Equilibrium investment amount in the risky asset under the SMMV preference w.r.t. different values of risk premium. 
             The parameters are assumed to be deterministic, with $r = 0.012$, $\sigma = 0.158$, $\zeta = 0.5$, $T = 25$ and $\theta = 5$. 
             The value of the risk premium $\vartheta $ varies from 0.15 to 0.35.}
	\label{fig: pi vartheta plot}
\end{figure}

\Cref{fig: pi plot} illustrates the amount invested in the risky asset with the SMMV and MV preference, respectively. 
As noted in \Cref{rem: path-independent ONEC}, since ${\beta }_{t} > 1$, the equilibrium investment in the risky asset with the SMMV preference $\bar{\pi }_{t}$ is higher than that under the MV preference $\bar{\pi }^{MV}_{t}$. 
By sending $\zeta $ to zero, we obtain the equilibrium investment amount under an MMV preference within our time-consistent framework (see the solid red curve in \Cref{fig: pi plot}). 
Additionally, when $\zeta $ is positive, $\bar{\pi }_{t}$ declines more rapidly towards the later stages of the investment period, reflecting the SMMV investor's sharply decreasing interest in risky investments over time. 
By the end of the investment period, at time $T$, the equilibrium investment amounts under both preferences converge and become identical. 
Furthermore, \Cref{fig: pi vartheta plot} exhibits a significant positive effect of the risk premium $\vartheta $ on the equilibrium investment amount $\bar{\pi }$ under SMMV preferences, 
indicating that higher risk premiums encourage greater investment in the risky asset; see also \Cref{rem: path-independent ONEC}.


\section{Concluding remark}
\label{sec: Concluding remark}

In this paper, we have studied the time-inconsistent portfolio selection problem with strictly monotone mean-variance preference, and characterized the time-consistent strategy of both the open-loop type and closed-loop type.
For the problem with deterministic parameters, we obtain analytical expressions of the open-loop equilibria and closed-loop equilibria.
The obtained two equilibrium strategies are found to be identical, which are independent of the wealth level and random path.
Moreover, this time-consistent strategy with the strictly monotone mean-variance preference indicates a greater amount of equity investment than the suggested strategy with a conventional mean-variance preference.
We have also studied whether the abovementioned solution is a stronger equilibrium strategy in the case with constant parameters. 
For spike variation in the open-loop type, any deviation will lead to a strict loss in the investor's satisfaction.
But there does not necessarily exist such a strict loss for spike variation in the closed-loop type, unless the interest rate is large enough.

