\section{Preceding results} \label{PreResult}
In connection with the Finite Element Method, there is plenty of work primarily on the relation
between $C_4(T)$ and the error estimates
such as \cite{BabuskaAziz,BrennerScott,Ciarlet,KobayashiTsuchiya,KobayashiTsuchiya2,
Lehmann,LiuKikuchi,NakaoYamamoto,Zlamal}
for {\it a priori} error estimate and 
\cite{BrennerScott,KikuchiSaito,LiuKikuchi} for {\it a posteriori} error estimate.

On the explicit upper bound for $C_4(T)$, Arcangeli and Gout\cite{ArcangeliGout} obtained the following estimates:
$$
	C_4(T)\le \frac{3d(T)^2}{\rho(T)},
$$
where $d(T)$ is the diameter of $T$ and $\rho(T)$ is the diameter of the inscribed circle of $T$.
They also obtained the upper bound for $C_3(T)$ as follows:
\begin{equation*}
	C_3(T)\le 3d(T)^2.
\end{equation*}
Meinguet and Descloux\cite{MeinguetDescloux} improved their result and obtained
\begin{equation*}
	C_4(T)\le \frac{1.21d(T)^2}{\rho(T)}.
\end{equation*}
Natterer\cite{Natterer} showed that $C_4(T)$ is bounded in terms of
$C_4(T_{0,1})$, where $T_{0,1}$ is an isosceles right triangle with edge lengths of $1,1$ and $\sqrt{2}$.
Specifically, they showed
\begin{equation}
	C_4(T)\le C_4(T_{0,1})\cdot\frac{\alpha^2+\beta^2+\sqrt{\alpha^4+2\alpha^2\beta^2\cos2\theta+\beta^4}}
		{\sqrt{2(\alpha^2+\beta^2-\sqrt{\alpha^4+2\alpha^2\beta^2\cos2\theta+\beta^4})}},
\label{NattererEstimation}
\end{equation}
where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are the longest and the second-longest edge lengths,
and $\theta$ is an included angle (Fig.~\ref{Fig1}).
In the same paper, they proved $C_4(T_{0,1})\le0.81$.

