\begin{theorem} \label{Verified_Result2}
For
$$
	n=20, \qquad b = \frac{1}{10},\qquad a_l = \frac{l}{500},
$$
the following inequality holds for all $l=0,1,\dots, 250$:
\begin{align*}
	\frac{n^2}{n^2-1}C_j^{(n)}(T_{a_l,b})^2&\left(1+5\cdot\frac{1}{50^2}\right)< \lim_{y\downarrow0}L_j(a_l,y), \quad j=1,2, \\
	\frac{n^4}{n^4-1}C_3^{(n)}(T_{a_l,b})^2&\left(1+6\cdot\frac{1}{50^2}\right)< \lim_{y\downarrow0}L_3(a_l,y).
\end{align*}
Here we remark that $\lim_{y\downarrow0}L_j(a,y), \; j=1,2,3$ are coincide with the expression
obtained by formally substituting $b=0$ to $L_j(a,b)$.
\end{theorem}

Theorem~\ref{Verified_Result1} and Theorem~\ref{Verified_Result2} was confirmed on
both MATLAB R2019b with INTLAB version 12 and MATLAB R2024b with INTLAB version 12.
The codes used for verification are listed as Code~\ref{C1verify}, Code~\ref{C2verify}, Code~\ref{C3verify}, and Code~\ref{C4verify}
 in Appendix 2 (Section~\ref{Appendix2}).

Again, we want to emphasize that in the proofs of Theorem~\ref{Verified_Result1} and Theorem~\ref{Verified_Result2},
we do not compute the eigenvalues directly, but rather transform the inequality about $C_j^{(n)}$
into the form of equation \eqref{positive_definite} and showing the positive definiteness of the matrix.

These results are only valid for a finite number of triangles, but we will extend them to results for arbitrary triangles
 in the next section.

\section{Extend to continuously distribute triangles} \label{Continuification}
