	\section{Introduction}
	Doob's optional sampling theorem is a cornerstone of classical martingale theory, providing conditions under which a stochastic process may be stopped at a random time without disturbing its expected value. Its utility spans a wide range of applications, from stochastic calculus to probabilistic inequalities and statistical inference. However, in many settings, particularly those involving dependence structures not captured by the martingale property, its direct application becomes problematic. This paper aims to extend the reach of optional sampling-type results by working within the more general framework of demimartingales and demisubmartingales.

	Demimartingales were originally introduced in \cite{NW1982} as a tool to study dependent structures beyond the scope of classical martingales, particularly in the context of associated sequences. Unlike martingales, which rely on a strict conditional expectation equality, demimartingales satisfy a one-sided inequality involving nondecreasing functions of the process, thereby accommodating broader dependence structures. A prominent example is the class of positively associated random variables, first formalized \cite{EPW1967}, which arises naturally in areas such as statistical mechanics, reliability theory, and nonparametric statistics. The demimartingale framework provides a natural extension in these settings, capturing essential dependence features while retaining analytical tractability. The relevant definitions are provided below.

	\begin{definition}
		A finite collection of random variables $X_1, \ldots, X_n$ is said to be (positively) associated if
		$$
		\operatorname{Cov}\left(f\left(X_1, \ldots, X_n\right), g\left(X_1, \ldots, X_n\right)\right) \geq 0
		$$
		for any componentwise nondecreasing functions $f, g$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$ such that the covariance is defined. An infinite collection is associated if every finite subcollection is associated.
	\end{definition}

	\begin{definition}
		A sequence of $L^1$ random variables $\left\{S_n, n \in \mathbb{N}\right\}$ is called a demimartingale if for all $j=1,2, \ldots$
		$$
		\mathbb{E}\left[\left(S_{j+1}-S_j\right) f\left(S_1, \ldots, S_j\right)\right] \geq 0
		$$
		for all componentwise nondecreasing functions $f$ whenever the expectation is defined. Moreover, if $f$ is assumed to be nonnegative, the sequence $\left\{S_n, n \in \mathbb{N}\right\}$ is called a demisubmartingale.
	\end{definition}

	Over the last few decades, this framework has attracted the attention of numerous researchers who have explored its theoretical properties, developed new inequalities and studied its asymptotic behaviour. For more on demimartingales, the interested reader may refer to \cite{C2000}, \cite{C2003}, \cite{DSHY2014}, \cite{HPrR2025} and the monograph of Prakasa Rao \cite{PR2012} (and references within).

	\begin{remark}
		\label{rem1}	It can easily be proven that partial sums of associated random variables satisfy the demimartingales or demisubmartingales condition under moment assumptions: 
		Consider a sequence of positively associated random variables $(X_n)_{n\geq 1}$ and let $S_n = \sum_{i=1}^nX_i$. Then, for a componentwise nondecreasing function $f$ we can have the following:
		\[
		\E[(S_{n+1}- S_n)f(S_1,. \ldots,S_n)] = \E[X_{n+1}f_1(X_1, \ldots,X_n)] \geq \E X_{n+1} \E[f_1(X_1,\ldots,X_n)]
		\]
		due to the association property. Notice that:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item [(a)] if $\E X_i = 0$ for all $i$, then $S_n$ forms a demimartingale sequence 
			\item [(b)]  if $\E X_i \geq 0$ for all $i$ and $f$ is also assume to be nonnegative then $S_n$ is a demisubmartingale.
		\end{enumerate}
		However, there are counterexamples available in the literature  (see for example \cite{MH2010} or \cite{PR2012}) where it is  proven that not all demimartingale sequences have positively associated demimartingale differences, leading to the concluson that demimartingales form a class of random variables wider than the class of partial sums of zero mean positively associated random variables.
	\end{remark}


	\begin{remark}
		\label{rem2}Martingales naturally satisfy the inequality that defines demimartingales when considered with their standard filtration. This inclusion positions demimartingales as a broader class that encompasses martingales. However, the converse does not hold in general: a process may be a demimartingale without fulfilling the more restrictive conditions required of a martingale; see \cite{MH2010} or \cite{PR2012} for counterexamples.
	\end{remark}

	\medskip

As illustrated in Remark \ref{rem2}, sequences that fail to satisfy the martingale or submartingale property due to underlying dependence, may still conform to the weaker inequalities that define demimartingales or demisubmartingales. Thus, the demimartingales framework allows for extensions of fundamental probabilistic tools to settings that were previously out of reach. Moreover, due to Remark \ref{rem1},  it is always of interest to explore whether results for associated sequences can be derived within the demimartingale framework. This approach offers a unified methodology that bypasses the need for specialized techniques often used in the literature on dependence, such as blocking arguments or covariance decay conditions. In fact, it has been observed that in certain cases, the results obtained via demimartingales are even sharper than those derived directly under association, owing to the structural advantages and flexibility provided by the demimartingale setting. For more information of associated random variables, we refer to \cite{O2012} and references therein.


In Section 2 we establish a set of preliminary inequalities for demimartingales stopped at bounded stopping times, where the stopping rules are defined through a componentwise monotonicity condition. These results serve as the foundation for more general formulations of Doob’s type optional sampling theorem, which are then presented in Section 3 under various integrability and structural assumptions on the process and the stopping time. In Section 4 we develop probabilistic and moment inequalities for demimartingales with bounded increments; these include (among other) maximal inequalities, moment bounds, and exponential concentration results of Azuma-Hoeffding type. These inequalities become instrumental results for obtaining a strong law of large numbers and a central limit theorem for demimartingales. Section 5 applies these tools to sequences of positively associated random variables. 

	\medskip

	Througout the paper, we denote $x\wedge y = \min\{x,y\}$ and $I\{A\}$ is used for the indicator function of the set $A$. Moreover, we use the convention that $\sum_{i=1}^{0} = 0$ where necessary while  the term \textit{demi(sub)martingale} means a demimartingale or a demisubmartingale sequence.

	\medskip

	First, we present here for convenience (and without proofs),  two theorems that are instrumental for the results obtained in this paper. The first can be found in \cite{C2003} and \cite{H2010} (check also Theorem 2.1.3 in \cite{PR2012}) while the second one is in \cite{PR2012} (see Theorem 2.1.4).

	\begin{theorem}
		\label{chr}Let the sequence $\left\{S_n, n \geq 1\right\}$ be a demisubmartingale, $S_0=$ 0 , and $\tau$ be a positive integer-valued random variable. Furthermore suppose that the indicator function $I\{\tau \leq j\}=h_j\left(S_1, \ldots, S_j\right)$ is a componentwise nonincreasing function of $S_1, \ldots, S_j$ for $j \geq 1$. Then the random sequence $\left\{S_j^*=S_{\tau \wedge j}, j \geq 1\right\}$ is a demisubmartingale.
	\end{theorem}

	\begin{theorem}
		\label{PR}Let the sequence $\left\{S_n, n \geq 1\right\}$ be a demisubmartingale and $\tau$ be a positive integer-valued random variable. Furthermore suppose that the indicator function $I\{\tau \leq j\}=h_j\left(S_1, \ldots, S_j\right)$ is a componentwise nonincreasing function of $S_1, \ldots, S_j$ for $j \geq 1$. Then, for any $1 \leq n \leq m$,

		$$
		E\left(S_{\tau \wedge m}\right) \geq E\left(S_{\tau \wedge n}\right) \geq E\left(S_1\right).
		$$
		Suppose the sequence $\left\{S_n, n \geq 1\right\}$ is a demimartingale and the indicator function $I\{\tau \leq j\}=h_j\left(S_1, \ldots, S_j\right)$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1, \ldots, S_j$ for $j \geq 1$. Then, for any $1 \leq n \leq m$,

		$$
		E\left(S_{\tau \wedge m}\right) \leq E\left(S_{\tau \wedge n}\right) \leq E\left(S_1\right).
		$$
	\end{theorem}

	\section{Stopping time results for demimartingales}
	In this section, we establish foundational results concerning stopping times applied to demimartingales. Focusing on stopping times with monotonic indicator functions of a specific form, we derive key inequalities that extend the classical notion of stopped processes to the broader setting of demi(sub)martingales, under minimal structural assumptions. 

	\medskip

	We begin with a basic inequality for demi(sub)martingales stopped at a bounded stopping time. The result highlights how the demi(sub)martingale structure, combined with a monotonicity condition on the stopping time, leads to an upper bound on the expected value of the stopped process.

	\begin{theorem}
		\label{theor1}Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demi(sub)martingale and let $\tau$ be a bounded stopping time, i.e. there is $M\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $P(\tau \leq M) = 1$ such that $I\{\tau = k\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1,\ldots, S_k$ for $k=1,\ldots, M-1$. Then,
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item[(a)] $\{S_\tau, S_M\}$ is a demisubmartingale
			\item [(b)]	 $\E(S_\tau)\leq \E(S_M)$.
		\end{enumerate}
	\end{theorem}	
	\begin{proof}
		Let $T_1 = S_{\tau}$ and $T_2 = S_M$ and let $f$ be a nonnegative nondecreasing function. Then,
		\begin{align*}
			&\E[(T_2-T_1)f(T_1)] = E[(S_M- S_\tau)f(S_\tau)] = \E[(S_M- S_\tau)f(S_\tau)I\{\tau\leq M\}]\\
			& = \sum_{k=1}^{M}  \E[(S_M- S_k)f(S_k)I\{\tau=k\}] \geq 0
		\end{align*}
		due to the demisubmartingale property. Thus, the pair $\{T_1, T_2\}$ forms a demisubmartingale sequence. The inequality in part (b) is due to the demisubmartingale property by choosing $f\equiv 1$.
	\end{proof}

	\medskip
	\noindent The corollary below follows as a direct consequence of Theorem \ref{theor1}.

	\begin{corollary}
		Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demi(sub)martingale and let $\tau$ be a stopping time such that $I\{\tau = k\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1,\ldots, S_k$. Then,
		\[
		\E(S_{\tau\wedge j})\leq \E(S_j), \quad j=1,2,\ldots.
		\]
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof}
		The result follows by applying the previous theorem since $\tau\wedge j$ is bounded by $j$.
	\end{proof}

	\medskip

	\medskip

	We now extend the previous results to a pair of stopping times $\tau_1\leq \tau_2$ showing that the ordered pair $(S_{\tau_1}, S_{\tau_2})$ inherits the demisubmartingale structure under appropriate assumptions. 

	\begin{theorem}
		Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demi(sub)martingale with $S_0\equiv 0$ such that $|S_{k+1} - S_k|\leq M<\infty$ and let $\tau_1$ and $\tau_2$ be stopping times satisfying the following conditions:
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item [(a)] $\tau_1 \leq \tau_2$ a.s.
			\item [(b)] $I\{\tau_1 = j\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1,\ldots, S_j$ for any $j$ 
			\item [(c)] $\E(\tau_2^q) <\infty$ and $\E(g^p(S_{\tau_1}))<\infty$ for any $g$ nonnegative nondecreasing function $g$ and for positive constants $p,q$ such that $\frac{1}{p} + \frac{1}{q} = 1$.
		\end{enumerate}
		Then, $(S_{\tau_1}, S_{\tau_2})$ is a demisubmartingale.
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		We start by defining a new sequence namely, $Z_n = (S_{\tau_2\wedge n}-S_{\tau_1\wedge n})g(S_{\tau_1})$ where $g$ is a nonnegative nondecreasing function and let $Z = (S_{\tau_2}- S_{\tau_1})g(S_{\tau_1})$. Then,
		\[
		Z_n \to Z \quad\mbox{a.s.}\quad n\to \infty
		\] 
		since, as $n\to\infty$, $\tau_i\wedge n\to \tau_i\,\, \mbox{a.s.}$ and $S_{\tau_i\wedge n}\to S_{\tau_i}\,\, \mbox{a.s.}$ for $i=1,2$. Moreover, 
		\[
		|S_{\tau_2\wedge n} - S_{\tau_1\wedge n}|\leq\sum_{k=\tau_1\wedge n +1}^{\tau_2\wedge n}|S_k - S_{k-1}|\leq M(\tau_2\wedge n - \tau_1\wedge n)\leq M\tau_2,
		\]
		and therefore,
		\[
		|Z_n| \leq |S_{\tau_2\wedge n}-S_{\tau_1\wedge n}||g(S_{\tau_1})|\leq M\tau_2 g(S_{\tau_1}).
		\]
		Then, 
		\[
		\E|Z_n|\leq M\E(\tau_2 g(S_{\tau_1})) \leq M \E(\tau_2^q) \E(g^p(S_{\tau_1}))<\infty
		\]
		by assumption (c).
		The latter ensures that $\sup_n\E|Z_n|<\infty$. Hence, $(Z_n)_n$ is uniformly integrable which allows for 
		\[
		\E Z = \lim_{n\to\infty}\E Z_n.
		\]
		Finally, we need to study the sign of $\E Z_n$.Suppose $(\Omega, \cal F, P)$ is the underlying probability space. Note that
		\begin{eqnarray*}
			E(Z_n)&=& \int_{\Omega}Z_n(\omega)P(d\omega)= \int_{[\tau_1\wedge n=n]}Z_n(\omega)P(d\omega)+\int_{[\tau_1\wedge n=\tau_1]}Z_n(\omega)P(d\omega)\\
			&=& \int_{[\tau_1\wedge n=n]}(S_n-S_n)g(S_{\tau_1})P(d\omega)+\int_{[\tau_1\wedge n=\tau_1]}(S_{{\tau_2}\wedge n}-S_{\tau_1})g(S_{\tau_1})P(d\omega)\\
			&=& E[(S_{{\tau_2}\wedge n}-S_{\tau_1})g(S_{\tau_1})I[\tau_1\wedge n=\tau_1]]\\
			&=& \sum_{j=1}^\infty E[(S_{{\tau_2}\wedge n}-S_j)g(S_j)I[\tau_1\wedge n=\tau_1, \tau_1=j]]\\
			&=& \sum_{j=1}^n E[(S_{{\tau_2}\wedge n}-S_j)g(S_j)I[ \tau_1=j]]\geq 0
		\end{eqnarray*}
		where the inequality is due to the demisubmartingale property. Since $\E Z_n$ is always a nonnegative quantity, so is its limit $\E Z$ which confirms the demisubmartingale property for $(S_{\tau_1}, S_{\tau_2})$.
	\end{proof}

	\section{Doob's-type optional sampling theorems for demimartingales}
	Theorem \ref{DoobOpt} presents a Doob’s type optional sampling theorem for demimartingales. It establishes that, under a set of natural conditions on the stopping time and the process, the expected value of the stopped process does not exceed its initial expectation. The proof relies on approximation by bounded stopping times and uses the demimartingale structure in conjunction with classical convergence arguments.
	\begin{theorem}
		\label{DoobOpt}	Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demimartingale and let $\tau$  be a stopping time such that $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1,\ldots,S_j$. If any one of the following conditions 
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item [$(A_1)$] $\tau$ is bounded
			\item [$(A_2)$]  $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ is bounded and $\tau$ is finite a.s.
			\item [$(A_3)$] $\E(\tau)<\infty$ and $|S_k-S_{k-1}|\leq M$ for all $k\geq 2$ and for some $M>0$
			\item [$(A_4)$] $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ is uniformly integrable and $\tau$ is finite a.s.
		\end{enumerate}
		hold, then $\E(S_\tau) \leq \E(S_1)$.
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		Notice first that if $\tau$ satisfies any of the conditions stated above, then $\tau <\infty$ a.s. Then, as $n\to \infty$ 
		\begin{equation}
			\label{conv1} \tau\wedge n \to \tau \quad \mbox{and}\quad
			S_{\tau\wedge n}\to S_\tau \quad \mbox{a.s.}. 
		\end{equation}
		Moreover, under the assumption that $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1,\ldots,S_j$ , Theorem \ref{PR} ensures that 
		\begin{equation}
			\label{ineq1}	\E(S_{\tau\wedge n}) \leq \E S_1.
		\end{equation}
		Let $(A_1)$ hold i.e., there is a constant $c$ such that $\tau\leq c$ a.s. Then, for $\forall n>c$ we have that 
		$$\E(S_{\tau\wedge n}) = \E S_\tau\leq \E S_1$$
		where the last inequality follows by \eqref{ineq1}.

		\medskip

		\noindent Now, assume that $(A_2)$ is valid i.e. $\exists M<\infty$ such that $|S_n|<M$ for all $n$ and $\tau<\infty$ a.s. Based on this assumption we have that $|S_{\tau\wedge n}|<M$. Then,
		\begin{align*}
			&\E(S_{\tau}) = \E\left (\lim_{n\to\infty}S_{\tau\wedge n}\right ) = \lim_{n\to\infty}\E(S_{\tau\wedge n}) \leq  \lim_{n\to\infty}\E S_1 = \E S_1
		\end{align*}
		where the second equality follows by the dominated (bounded) convergence theorem while the inquality is again due to \eqref{ineq1}. 

		\medskip

		\noindent	Under assumption $(A_3)$ we start by noting that 
		\begin{align*}
			&|S_{\tau\wedge n} - S_1| = \left | \sum_{k=2}^{\tau\wedge n}(S_k-S_{k-1})\right |\leq  \sum_{k=2}^{\tau\wedge n}\left | (S_k-S_{k-1})\right | \leq (\tau\wedge n -1)M \leq (\tau -1)M.
		\end{align*}
		Notice that the RHS is independent of $n$ and since $\tau$ has finite expectation, $\E|S_{\tau\wedge n} - S_1| < \infty$. The desired result follows by employing once again the dominated (bounded) convergence theorem and \eqref{ineq1} i.e.
		\begin{align*}
			&\E(S_{\tau}-S_1) = \E\left  (\lim_{n\to\infty}(S_{\tau\wedge n}-S_1)\right ) = \lim_{n\to\infty}(\E(S_{\tau\wedge n}) - \E S_1)\leq 0.
		\end{align*}

		\medskip

		\noindent Notice that if $(A_4)$ is true, $\forall \varepsilon>0, \, \, \, \exists M>0$ such that $\sup \E(|S_n|I\{|S_n|>M\})<\varepsilon$. Observe that
		\begin{align*}
			& \E(|S_{\tau\wedge n}|I\{|S_{\tau\wedge n}|>M\}) = \E\left (\left |\sum_{k=0}^{n}S_kI\{\tau = k\} +S_nI\{\tau>n\}\right | I\{|S_{\tau\wedge n}|>M\}\right )\\
			&\leq \sum_{k=0}^n\E(|S_k|I\{\tau =k\}I\{|S_{\tau\wedge n}|>M\})+\E(|S_n|I\{\tau>n\}I\{|S_{\tau\wedge n}|>M\}).
		\end{align*}
		For the second term, for fixed $n$, by the uniform integrability of $S_n$ we have that
		\[
		\E(|S_n|I\{\tau>n\}I\{|S_{\tau\wedge n}|>M\}) = \E(|S_n|I\{|S_n|>M\})\to 0,\quad M\to\infty
		\]
		while similarly for the first term, for any fixed $k\leq n$,
		\[
		\E(|S_k|I\{\tau =k\}I\{|S_{\tau\wedge n}|>M\}) =  \E(|S_k|I\{|S_k|>M\})\to 0,\quad M\to\infty
		\]
		proving that $\sup_n\E(|S_{\tau\wedge n}|I\{|S_{\tau\wedge n}|>M\})\to 0$ as $M\to\infty$ i.e. $S_{\tau\wedge n}$ is uniformly integrable. The result follows by observing that $S_{\tau\wedge n} \to S_\tau$ almost surely and by noting that uniform integrability ensures $\E\lim S_{\tau\wedge n}  = \lim \E S_{\tau\wedge n} $ (Vitali's convergence theorem).
	\end{proof}

	\medskip


	The next result strengthens Theorem \ref{DoobOpt} in the case where the demimartingale is nonnegative. By applying Fatou’s lemma, we show that the inequality proved above for the expected value at a stopping time holds under minimal assumptions i.e., finiteness of the stopping time and a monotonicity condition on its indicator function.

	\begin{theorem}
		Consider a nonnegative demimartingale sequence $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$  and let $\tau$  be a stopping time such that $\tau<\infty$ a.s. and  $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1,\ldots,S_j$. Then,
		\[
		\E S_\tau \leq \E S_1.
		\]
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		The proof follows by applying Fatou's lemma. Particularly,
		\[
		\E S_\tau = \E\left (\lim_{n\to \infty} S_{\tau\wedge n}\right ) = \E\left (\liminf_{n\to \infty} S_{\tau\wedge n}\right ) \leq \liminf_{n\to \infty} \E  S_{\tau\wedge n} \leq \E S_1.
		\]
	\end{proof}

	\medskip

	\noindent Theorem \ref{DoobOpt2} provides a counterpart to Theorem \ref{DoobOpt} for demisubmartingales when the stopping time satisfies a nonincreasing monotonicity condition. In contrast to Theorem \ref{DoobOpt}, which yields an upper bound for the expected value at a stopping time, this result establishes a lower bound, reflecting the reversed direction of the componentwise monotonicity. The same set of integrability and boundedness conditions ensures the validity of the inequality.

	\begin{theorem}
		\label{DoobOpt2}	Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demisubmartingale and let $\tau$  be a stopping time such that $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is a componentwise nonincreasing function of $S_1,\ldots,S_j$. If any one of the following conditions is true
		\begin{enumerate}
			\item [$(A_1)$] $\tau$ is bounded
			\item [$(A_2)$]  $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ is bounded and $\tau$ is finite a.s.
			\item [$(A_3)$] $\E(\tau)<\infty$ and $|S_k-S_{k-1}|\leq M$ for all $k\geq 2$ and for some $M>0$
			\item [$(A_4)$] $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ is uniformly integrable and $\tau$ is finite a.s.
		\end{enumerate}
		then, $\E(S_\tau) \geq \E(S_1)$.
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		The results follow by applying similar steps as in Theorem \ref{DoobOpt} and taking into account that under the assumption for $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ we have that
		\[
		\E S_{\tau\wedge n} \geq \E S_1
		\]
		(by Theorem \ref{PR}).
	\end{proof}

	\section{Inequalities and asymptotic results for demimartingales}
	In this section, we derive a collection of probabilistic and moment inequalities for demimartingales, extending classical results such as Doob's maximal inequality,  Azuma-Hoeffding bounds and a Burkholder-type inequality. These results are obtained under structural assumptions on the process, such as bounded increments and by utilizing Theorem \ref{DoobOpt} after defining suitable stopping times. Furthermore, the inequalities obtained, lead to asymptotic results such as a strong law of large numbers and a central limit theorem for demimartingales under mild conditions.

	\begin{theorem}
		\label{DoobIneq}	Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demi(sub)martingale. Then, for $\lambda >0$
		\[
		P\left(\max_{1\leq i \leq j}S_i \geq \lambda\right)  \leq \dfrac{\E S_1}{\lambda}.
		\]
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		Define $\tau = \min\{k:S_k\geq \lambda\}$ for $\lambda>0$ i.e. the first time that the process exceeds $\lambda$. Observe that
		\[
		\{\tau \leq j\} = \bigcup_{i=1}^j \{S_i \geq \lambda\}
		\]
		which gives $I	\{\tau \leq j\} = \max_{1\leq i\leq j}I\{S_i\geq \lambda\}$ i.e. if any of the $S_i$ for $i\leq j$ is above $\lambda$,  then $I	\{\tau \leq j\}=1$. Thus, if any of the $S_i$ increases, $I	\{\tau \leq j\}$ will either remain constant or increases proving that $I	\{\tau \leq j\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1, \ldots, S_j$.

		\medskip

		\noindent Notice that on the set $	\{\tau \leq j\}$ we have that  $S_\tau \geq \lambda$ i.e. $S_\tau \geq \lambda I	\{\tau \leq j\} $ a.s. and thus,
		\[
		\E S_\tau \geq \lambda P(\tau \leq j).
		\]
		From Theorem \ref{DoobOpt} we have that $\E S_\tau \leq \E S_1$ and by combining the last two expressions we have that 
		\[
		P\left(\max_{1\leq i \leq j}S_i \geq \lambda\right) = P(\tau \leq j) \leq \dfrac{\E S_1}{\lambda}.
		\]
	\end{proof}

	\begin{remark}
		For demisubmartingales, the usual Doob's type inequality is of the form
		\[
		P\left(\max_{1\leq i \leq j}S_i \geq \lambda\right) \leq \dfrac{1}{\lambda}\E S_nI \left (\max_{1\leq i \leq j}S_i \geq \lambda  \right )
		\]
		(see for example (2.7.1) in \cite{PR2012}) which in the case of nonnegative demisubmartingales also gives
		\[
		P\left(\max_{1\leq i \leq j}S_i \geq \lambda\right) \leq \dfrac{1}{\lambda}\E S_n.
		\]	
		Observe that the inequality obtained in Theorem \ref{DoobIneq} is not written in terms of an indicator function. Moreover, due to the demisubmartingale property $\ E S_1 \leq \E S_n$, the inequality obtained in Theorem \ref{DoobIneq} gives a sharper bound in the case of nonnegative demisubmartingales.
	\end{remark}

	\medskip

	\noindent
	We now present a maximal inequality for demi(sub)martingales in $L^p$ with 
	$p<1$. The result provides an upper bound for the  $p$-th moment of the maximum of the process and appears to be less commonly discussed in the literature, which typically focuses on $p\geq 1$.


	\begin{corollary}
		\label{maximal1}	Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demi(sub)martingale such that $S_n \geq M$ a.s.. Then for $p<1$
		\[
		\E\left (\max_{1\leq i\leq j}S_i\right )^p \leq \dfrac{p \E S_1}{(1-p)M^{1-p}}.
		\]
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof}
		Observe that by using the inequality obtained above we have that
		\begin{align*}
			&\E\left (\max_{1\leq i\leq j}S_i\right )^p = p\int_{M}^{\infty}x^{p-1}P\left (\max_{1\leq i\leq j}S_i\geq x\right ) {\rm d}x\leq p\int_{M}^{\infty}x^{p-2}\E S_1 {\rm d}x
		\end{align*}
		which leads to the desired result.
	\end{proof}

	\medskip

	\noindent Among the key contributions of this work is a concentration inequality of Azuma-Hoeffding type for demi(sub)martingales, presented in the Theorem that follows. This result provides an exponential bound for the tail probability of a demimartingale with bounded differences. The proof relies on constructing an exponential demisubmartingale and applying the Theorem \ref{DoobOpt} under a suitably chosen stopping time. 

	\medskip


	\begin{theorem}
		\label{AzumaDem}	Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demimartingale with $S_0 \equiv 0$ and that $|S_i - S_{i-1}| \leq c_i<\infty$ for $i=1, 2,\ldots.$ Then, for any $t\in \mathbb{R}$
		\begin{equation}
			\label{concen1}	P(S_n \geq t) \leq \exp\left\{-\dfrac{t^2}{2\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2}\right\}
		\end{equation}
		and
		\begin{equation}
			\label{concen2}	P(|S_n| \geq t) \leq 2 \exp\left\{-\dfrac{t^2}{2\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2}\right\}.
		\end{equation}
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		First, we define $M_n = \exp\left \{\lambda S_n - \dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right \}$ for $\lambda>0$. Observe that $\{M_n, n \in\mathbb{N}_0\}$ forms a demisubmartingale sequence as a nondecreasing convex function of a demimartingale. Next, define the stopping time
		\[
		\tau = \min\{k\leq n: S_k \geq t\}\wedge n
		\]
		for which  $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing function of $S_1, \ldots, S_j$ and as a result it is also a componentwise nondecreasing function of $M_1, \ldots, M_j$ (see Lemma 2.1 in \cite{C2000}). Moreover, observe that for fixed $n$, the stopping time $\tau$ is bounded by $n$. By applying Theorem \ref{DoobOpt} we have that 
		\begin{equation}
			\label{eqM}\E M_\tau \leq \E M_0 =1
		\end{equation}

		\noindent On the event $\{\tau\leq n, S_\tau\geq t\}$ we have that
		\[
		M_\tau = \exp\left \{\lambda S_\tau - \dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{\tau}c_i^2\right \} \geq \exp\left \{\lambda t - \dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right \} 
		\]
		since $S_\tau\geq t$ and $\sum_{i=1}^{\tau} c_i^2 \leq \sum_{i=1}^{n} c_i^2 $. Notice that 
		\[
		I\{S_n\geq t\} \leq I\{S_\tau\geq t\}\leq I\left \{M_{\tau}\geq \exp\left \{\lambda t - \dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right \} \right \}
		\]
		which leads to
		\[
		P(S_n\geq t) \leq P\left (M_{\tau}\geq \exp\left \{\lambda t - \dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right \}\right )\leq \dfrac{\E M_\tau}{\exp\left \{\lambda t - \dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right \}}\leq  \exp\left \{-\left (\lambda t - \dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right )\right \}.
		\]
		because of \eqref{eqM}. The RHS attains its minimum at $\lambda = \dfrac{t}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2}$ which leads to inequality \eqref{concen1}.

		\noindent For the expression in \eqref{concen2}, we start by writing
		\[
		P(|S_n|>t) \leq P(S_n \geq t) + P(-S_n \geq t)
		\]
		and the result follows by recalling that $(-S_n)_{n\in \mathbb{N}}$ is also a demimartingale. 
	\end{proof}


	\medskip

	\noindent The Burkholder-type inequality that follows is a direct consequence of the concentration inequality proven in Theorem \ref{AzumaDem}.

	\begin{theorem}
		\label{BurkH}Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demimartingale with $S_0 \equiv 0$ and that $|S_i - S_{i-1}| \leq c_i<\infty$ for $i=1, 2,\ldots.$  Then, for $p>0$,
		\begin{equation}
			\label{momexpon1}	\E (|S_n|^p) \leq C_p \left ( \sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right )^{p/2}
		\end{equation}
		where $C_p = 2^{p/2}p\Gamma\left (p/2\right )$.
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		The result follows by employing the Azuma-type inequality proven above. Particularly,
		\begin{align*}
			&\E (|S_n|^p) = p\int_{0}^{\infty}t^{p-1}P(|S_n|>t) {\rm d} t\leq 2p \int_{0}^{\infty}t^{p-1}\exp\left (-\dfrac{t^2}{2V_n}\right ){\rm d} t
		\end{align*}
		where $V_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2$. Let $u=\dfrac{t^2}{2V_n}$. Then the integral becomes of the form
		\begin{align*}
			&\E (|S_n|^p) = 2p(2V_n)^{\frac{p-1}{2}}\left (\dfrac{V_n}{2}\right )^{1/2}\int_{0}^{\infty}u^{\frac{p-2}{2}}e^{-u}{\rm d} u
		\end{align*}
		which leads to \eqref{momexpon1}.
	\end{proof}

	\medskip

	We now establish a central limit theorem for demimartingale sequences with bounded increments. The result shows that, under a suitable growth condition on the variance, the normalized mean zero demimartingale sequence converges in distribution to the standard normal law. Such results are rare in the literature, as the demimartingale framework lacks the structural properties commonly used in classical limit theorems. Our approach is based on the moment inequality obtained in Theorem \ref{BurkH} and the use of the characteristic function.
	\begin{theorem}
		Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a mean zero demimartingale sequence with $S_0 \equiv 0$,  $|S_i - S_{i-1}|\leq M\, \, \forall i=1,2,\ldots$ and let $\E S_n^2 = \sigma_n^2$ such that $\left (\dfrac{\sqrt{n}}{\sigma_n}\right )^3 \to 0$ as $n\to \infty$.
		Then,
		\[
		\dfrac{S_n}{\sigma_n}\xrightarrow{d} \mathcal{N}(0,1),\quad n\to\infty.
		\]
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
Define the random variable $Z_n = \dfrac{S_n}{\sigma_n}$.  For the characteristic function of $Z_n$ we have that
\[
\phi_n(t) = \E(e^{itZ_n}) = \E \left (1+itZ_n-\dfrac{t^2Z_n^2}{2} + R_n\right )
\]
where $|R_n| \leq \dfrac{|t|^3|Z_n|^3}{6}$.  Using Theorem \ref{BurkH} we have that 
\[
\E |S_n|^3 \leq C_3\left (\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right )^{3/2} =Cn^{3/2}
\]
which leads to $\E|Z_n|^3 \leq C\left (\dfrac{\sqrt{n}}{\sigma_n}\right )^3$. Thus,
\[
\phi_n(t) = 1-\dfrac{t^2}{2} +r_n
\]
with $\E |R_n| \leq r_n \to 0$ as $n\to \infty$ by assumption. By the characteristic function convergence theorem (Lévy’s continuity theorem), pointwise convergence of $\phi_n(t) \to e^{-t^2/2}$ implies convergence in distribution i.e.
\[
Z_n \xrightarrow{d}\mathcal{N}(0,1).
\] 
	\end{proof}




	\medskip

	\noindent The next result provides an exponential moment bound for demimartingales with bounded increments. 

	\begin{corollary}
		\label{expAzuma}Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demimartingale with $S_0 \equiv 0$ and that $|S_i - S_{i-1}| \leq c_i<\infty$ for $i=1, 2,\ldots.$ Then, for any $\lambda>0$,
		\begin{equation}
			\label{momexpon2}\E e^{\lambda S_n}\leq C_\lambda\exp\left (\dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right )
		\end{equation}
		and 
		\begin{equation}
			\label{momexpon3}\E e^{\lambda |S_n|}\leq 2C_\lambda\exp\left (\dfrac{\lambda^2}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right )
		\end{equation}
		where $C_\lambda = \lambda\left(2\pi\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right) ^{1/2}$.
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof} For inequality \eqref{momexpon2}, we start by writting
		\[
		E(e^{\lambda S_n}) = \int_{0}^{\infty}P(e^{\lambda S_n}> u){\rm d}u = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\lambda e^{\lambda t}P(S_n>t) {\rm d}t
		\]
		where the last expression was obtained by change of variables i.e. $t= \dfrac{1}{\lambda}\log u$. By setting $V_n$ as before and by utilizing inequality \eqref{concen1} of Theorem \ref{AzumaDem}, we have that
		\begin{align*}
			&	E(e^{\lambda S_n}) = \lambda\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\exp \left (\lambda t -\dfrac{t^2}{2V_n}\right ){\rm d}t  = \lambda \exp(\lambda^2 V_n/2)\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\exp \left (-\dfrac{(t-\lambda V_n)^2}{2V_n}\right ){\rm d}t \\
			&=  \lambda\left (2\pi\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2\right )^{1/2}\exp\left ( \dfrac{\lambda^2V_n}{2}\right ).
		\end{align*}
		Inequality \eqref{momexpon3} follows applying the same steps and using \eqref{concen2}.
	\end{proof}

	\medskip 

	\noindent The result that follows was proven in \cite{A1976} (see Theorem 1) for martingale sequences. Here we provide its generalization to the case of demi(sub)martingales utilizing the exponential bound of the previous result.


	\begin{theorem}
		\label{Azuma2}Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demimartingale with $S_0 \equiv 0$ and that $|S_i - S_{i-1}| \leq M\leq 1$ for $i=1, 2,\ldots.$ Then,
		\[
		\limsup_{n\to\infty}\dfrac{|S_n|}{\sqrt{2n\log n}}\leq 1 \,\, \mbox{a.s.}
		\]
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		By applying the result of Corollary \ref{expAzuma} for $\lambda = \left (\dfrac{2\log n}{n}\right )^{1/2}$ we have 
		\[
		E\exp\left (\left (\dfrac{2\log n}{n}\right )^{1/2} |S_n|\right )\leq 2C_\lambda n^M  = 4n^M\sqrt{\pi\log n}.
		\]
		By multiplying both sides with $\exp\{-(2+\varepsilon)\log n\}$ for $\varepsilon>0$ we have that
		\[
		E\exp\left (\left (\dfrac{2\log n}{n}\right )^{1/2} |S_n| - (2+\varepsilon)\log n\right )\leq  \dfrac{4\sqrt{\pi} \sqrt{\log n}}{n^{2-M+\varepsilon}}. 
		\]
		Since $M\leq 1$,
		\[
		\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\dfrac{\sqrt{\log n}}{n^{2-M+\varepsilon}}<\infty, 
		\]
		the latter expression gives
		\[
		\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\exp\left (\left (\dfrac{2\log n}{n}\right )^{1/2} |S_n| - (2+\varepsilon)\log n\right ) <\infty \,\,\, \mbox{a.s.}
		\]
		which leads to the conclusion that 
		\[
		\limsup_{n\to\infty}\left (\left (\dfrac{2\log n}{n}\right )^{1/2} |S_n| - (2+\varepsilon)\log n\right ) <\infty\,\,\, \mbox{a.s.}
		\]
		or equivalently,
		\[
		\limsup_{n\to\infty}\dfrac{ |S_n|}{\sqrt{2n\log n}}\leq \dfrac{2+\varepsilon}{2}\,\,\, \mbox{a.s.}.
		\]
		The result follows by letting $\varepsilon \to 0$.
	\end{proof}

	\medskip

	\noindent Using the asymptotic result of Theorem \ref{Azuma2}, we can easily obtain the strong law for demimartingale sequences given next.  The result is inspired by Corollary 1 in \cite{A1976}.

	\begin{corollary}
		\label{Azuma3}Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demimartingale with $S_0 \equiv 0$ and that $|S_i - S_{i-1}| \leq M\leq 1$ for $i=1, 2,\ldots$ 
		Then,
		\[
		\dfrac{S_n}{n} \to 0 \quad {\mbox a.s.} \quad n\to \infty.
		\]
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof}
		The result is direct consequence of Theorem \ref{Azuma2}:
		\[
		\dfrac{|S_n|}{n} = \dfrac{|S_n|}{\sqrt{2n\log n}}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{2n\log n}}{n}  \to 0,\quad a.s. \quad n\to\infty.
		\]
	\end{proof}

	\medskip

	In Corollary \ref{ineq2} we present an exponential inequality for demi(sub)martingales stopped at a bounded stopping time. 

	\begin{corollary}
		\label{ineq2}Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demi(sub)martingale with $S_0 \equiv 0$. Assume that $\tau$ is a stopping time and that $I\{\tau \leq  j\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing (nonincreasing) function of $S_1, \ldots, S_j$. Then, for $\theta>0$,
		\[
		\E \left [\exp(\theta S_\tau - H(\tau))\right ]\leq (\geq) 1
		\]
		for any function $H(.)$ such that $H(0) = 0$.
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof}
		We start by defining $M_n = \exp(\theta S_n - H(n))$, for $\theta>0$ which forms a demisubmartingale sequence. Note that since $I\{\tau \leq  j\}$ is a componentwise nondecreasing (nonincreasing) function of $S_1, \ldots S_j$ it is also  a componentwise nondecreasing (nonincreasing) function of $M_1, \ldots, M_j$. Thus, by Theorem \ref{DoobOpt}  (Theorem \ref{DoobOpt2}) we have that 
		\[
		\E(M_\tau) \leq (\geq) \E(M_0) = 1
		\]
		which gives the inequality stated above.
	\end{proof}

	\section{Applications to positively associated random variables}

	This section focuses on extending the methods developed earlier to sequences of positively associated random variables. By exploiting the fact that certain centered or transformed versions of such sequences can be treated as demimartingales or demisubmartingales, we establish a range of inequalities under suitable stopping rules. These include analogues of Wald-type identities and exponential bounds for random sums of positively associated random variables that lead to asymptotic results. For a detailed discussion of Wald’s identities and the respective inequalities for martingales, see \cite{B2013}. 
	\medskip

	\noindent The  lemma provided next, gives a uniform integrability condition for stopped demimartingales with bounded increments and finite expected stopping time. This technical result will be used to justify the application of optional sampling-type arguments in the positively associated setting and particularly for obtaining Corollary \ref{Wald1}.

	\begin{lemma}
		\label{ui} Let $(S_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a demimartingale with $S_0 \equiv 0$ and $|S_{k+1}-S_k|\leq M, \, \, k=0, 1,,\ldots$ and let $\tau$ be a stopping time with $\E \tau<\infty$. Then $S_{\tau\wedge n}$ is uniformly integrable.
	\end{lemma}
	\begin{proof}
		Recall that 
		\[
		\E|S_{\tau\wedge n}| = \E \left |\sum_{j=1}^{n}S_jI\{\tau = j\}+S_nI\{\tau >n\}\right |\leq \sum_{j=1}^{n}\E|S_j|I\{\tau = j\}+\E (|S_n|I\{\tau >n\}).
		\]
		Moreover, for any integer $j$,
		\[
		|S_j| \leq \sum_{k=1}^{j}|S_k - S_{k-1}| \leq Mj.
		\]
		Thus, for any $n$, we can write,
		\[
		\E|S_{\tau\wedge n}|  \leq \sum_{j=1}^n MjP(\tau = j) + Mn P(\tau>n) = M\E(\tau\wedge n) \leq M \E\tau<\infty
		\]
		i.e. $\sup_n \E|S_{\tau\wedge n}|<\infty$ which allows to conclude uniform integrability.
	\end{proof}

	\medskip

	We start the presentation of the main results of this section with a bound on the expectation of a random sum of positively associated, identically distributed bounded random variables stopped at a finite expected stopping time. By centering the process and appealing to the demimartingale structure, we derive an inequality reminiscent of Wald’s identity, adjusted to accommodate dependence through positive association. For all the results that follow, $S_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i$.


	\begin{corollary}
		\label{Wald1}Let $X_1, X_2, \ldots$ be a sequence of identically distributed positively associated random variables such that $|X_i|<M$ for any $i$, and let $\tau$ be a stopping time such that $\E\tau <\infty$ and $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is a componentwise nonincreasing function of $X_1,\ldots,X_j$. Then,
		\[
		\E S_\tau \geq  \E X_1 \E\tau
		\]
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof}
		Let $\mu = \E X_1$ and define $H_n  = S_n - n\mu$. It can easily be proven that the sequence $H_n$ forms a mean zero demimartingale. Moreover,
		\[
		|H_{n+1} - H_n| =  |X_{n+1} - \mu| \leq |X_{n+1}| + |\mu| \equiv B <\infty.
		\]
		Since $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is a componentwise nonincreasing function of $X_1,\ldots,X_j$, it is also a componentwise nonincreasing function of $S_1, \ldots, S_j$ and therefore, the sequence $H_{\tau\wedge n}$ is also a demisubmartingale (Theorem \ref{chr}) which is also uniformly integrable because of Lemma \ref{ui}. Thus, by appying Theorem \ref{DoobOpt2} on the sequence $H_{\tau\wedge n}$ we have that
		\[
		\E H_\tau \geq \E H_1
		\]
		i.e. $\E (S_\tau - \tau \mu) \geq  \E(X_1 -\mu) = 0$ which gives the requested inequality.
	\end{proof}

	\medskip


	Inequalities such as the one discussed in Corollary \ref{Wald1} can be obtained under different conditions as it can be seen in the result that follows.


	\begin{corollary}
		\label{Wald1alt}Let $X_1, X_2, \ldots$ be a sequence of $\mathcal{L}^1$ identically distributed positively associated random variables and let $\tau$ be a  bounded stopping time with $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ a componentwise nonincreasing (nondecreasing) function of $X_1,\ldots,X_j$. Then,
		\[
		\E S_\tau \geq (\leq) \E X_1 \E\tau.
		\]
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof}
		We use again the sequence $H_n  = S_n - n\mu$ which is a mean zero demimartingale sequence. Since $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ a componentwise nonincreasing (nondecreasing) function of $X_1,\ldots,X_j$ and it is also a componentwise nonincreasing (nondecreasing) function of $H_1,\ldots,H_j$. Thus, Theorem \ref{DoobOpt2} (Theorem \ref{DoobOpt}) can be applied directly to $H_n$ leading to 
		\[
		\E H_\tau \geq (\leq) \E H_1
		\]
		which gives the conclusion.
	\end{proof}


	\medskip

	Extending the previous result to second moments, this corollary provides an inequality for the squared random sum of nonnegative, positively associated $\mathcal{L}^2$ random variables.

	\begin{corollary}
		\label{Wald2}Let $X_1, X_2, \ldots$ be an $\mathcal{L}^2$ sequence of nonnegative identically distributed  associated random variables. Let $\tau$ be a bounded stopping time  and $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ a componentwise nonincreasing (nondecreasing) function of $X_1,\ldots,X_j$. Then,
		\[
		\E S_\tau ^2\geq (\leq) \E X_1 ^2\E\tau.
		\]
	\end{corollary}		
	\begin{proof}
		First define $H_n = S_n^2 -nEX_1^2$. Recall that $S_n$ is a demisubmartingale (by Remark \ref{rem1} since $\E X_i \geq 0$ ) and since $g(x) = x^2 - c$ is a nondecreasing convex function for $x>0$, $H_n$ also forms a demisubmartingale sequence with $H_0 = 0$. Since $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is assumed to a componentwise nonincreasing (nondecreasing) function of $X_1,\ldots,X_j$,  it is also a componentwise nonincreasing (nondecreasing) function of $H_1,\ldots,H_j$ as $g(.)$ is a nondecreasing transformation of $X_1,\ldots,X_j$. Due to the boundness of $\tau$, we can apply
		Theorem \ref{DoobOpt2}  (Theorem \ref{DoobOpt}) directly to the sequence $H_n$; thus $\E(H_\tau)\geq (\leq) \E H_0$ which leads to the desired conclusion.
	\end{proof}


	\medskip

	Next, we establish an exponential inequality for random sums of positively associated variables under a bounded stopping time. The result follows by constructing a demisubmartingale through an exponential transformation and applying the exponential inequality of Corollary \ref{ineq2}. This extends classical concentration-type bounds to a dependent setting.

	\medskip

	\begin{corollary}
		\label{Wald3}	Let $X_1, X_2, \ldots$ be positively associated random variables with $\E X_i \geq 0$ for all $i=1,2,\ldots$ and let $\tau$ be a bounded stopping time for which $I\{\tau\leq j\}$ is a componentwise nonincreasing (nondecreasing) function of $X_1, X_2,\ldots, X_j$. Then, for $\theta>0$,
		\[
		\E\left [\exp\left (\theta S_\tau - \sum_{i=1}^{\tau}\psi_i(\theta)\right )\right ]\geq(\leq) 1
		\]
		where $S_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i$ and $\psi_i(\theta) = \log\E(e^{\theta X_i})<\infty$.
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof}
		The result follows immediately from Corollary \ref{ineq2} since $S_n$ forms a demisubmartingale sequence. 
	\end{proof}

	\medskip

	\noindent The last two results of this section are related to the Azuma-Hoeffding-type concentration inequality for the partial sums of positively associated random variables, each of which is almost surely bounded by a finite constant. Theorem \ref{Azuma_pos} shows that the tail probabilities decay exponentially, with the rate governed by the sum of the squared individual bounds. Notably, the proof does not rely on any blocking techniques, truncation arguments, or assumptions on the decay of covariances, methods often used to handle dependence in the literature (see for example \cite{XY2010} or Chapter 2 in \cite{O2012} and references therein). Instead, the result follows directly from the structural properties of demimartingales, highlighting the effectiveness of the approach developed in this paper for handling dependence under minimal conditions.

	\begin{theorem}
		\label{Azuma_pos}Let $(X_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a sequence of mean zero positively associated random variables such that  $|X_i| \leq c_i<\infty$ for $i=1, 2,\ldots.$ Then, for any $t\in \mathbb{R}$
		\[
		P(S_n \geq t) \leq \exp\left\{-\dfrac{t^2}{2\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2}\right\}
		\]
		and
		\[
		P(|S_n| \geq t) \leq 2 \exp\left\{-\dfrac{t^2}{2\sum_{i=1}^{n}c_i^2}\right\}
		\]
		where $S_n = \sum_{i=1}^{n}X_i$.
	\end{theorem}
	\begin{proof}
		It follows directly from Theorem \ref{AzumaDem} since $S_n$ forms a mean zero demimartingale sequence.
	\end{proof}

	The next result is a direct sequence of Corollary \ref{Azuma3} and it proves a strong law of large numbers for sequences of mean-zero positively associated random variables under a boundedness condition. Unlike known results for positively associated random variables that need stronger moment assumptions (see for example Chapter 3 in \cite{O2012}, \cite{CS2019}, \cite{XY2014} ), this corollary shows that the normalized partial sums $S_n/n$ converge to zero almost surely under very mild conditions. 

	\begin{corollary}
		Let $(X_n)_{n\geq 1}$ be a sequence of mean zero positively associated random variables with $|X_i| \leq M\leq 1$ for $i=1, 2,\ldots$.
		Then,
		\[
		\dfrac{S_n}{n} \to 0 \quad {\mbox a.s.} \quad n\to \infty.
		\]
	\end{corollary}
	\begin{proof}
		Direct consequence of Corollary \ref{Azuma3}.
	\end{proof}

