%\subsection{Further developments}
Commercially available resonators only provide specific frequencies related to telecommunication or clock standards. Additionally, efforts toward miniaturization shift the reference standards to higher frequencies, resulting in reduced coupling to the incoming gravitational waves due to the lower mass of the resonator. Consequently, custom BAWs are being developed to enhance the sensitivity and extend the frequency coverage at low frequencies. 
Assuming complete trapping of the phonons by proper disk shaping, considering the proportionality of the effective mass with the crystal density $\rho$ and volume $\pi L^2 d$, and expressing the frequency according to Eq.~(\ref{eq:modes}), the peak strain sensitivity scales as: 
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:scalingSens}
        S^{+}_{h}(\omega_{\lambda}) \propto
        \frac{n}{L}
\sqrt{\frac{k_bT_{\lambda}}{\rho Q_{\lambda}v^3}} \ \ \ [\textrm{strain}/\sqrt{\textrm{Hz}}].
\end{equation}

\acapo Optimized performance requires a large radius, which impacts the resonator mass, a fast crystal, and low overtone numbers. Instead, the crystal thickness determines the BAW frequency spectrum but does not directly influence sensitivity, which depends on frequency solely through the overtone number. Therefore, a broad frequency coverage while maintaining consistent sensitivity requires several BAW devices with varying thicknesses. 

