\section{Introduction}\label{sec:1}
Understanding the interaction between bound states and continuum free-particle states is crucial to describe a wide range of physical phenomena, including ionisation, dissociation, and energy loss. In atomic and molecular systems near the ionisation threshold, this interplay governs photoexcitation dynamics, giving rise to the Fano asymmetric line shape in photoionisation spectra, an effect resulting from quantum interference between bound and continuum states coupled via direct laser excitation \cite{fano_effects_1961,litvinenko_multi-photon_2021}.
Beyond atomic physics, the Fano theory has been extended to 
bulk \cite{huttner_quantization_1992} and cavity-embedded \cite{de_liberato_virtual_2017} dielectrics, 
where bound-to-continuum transitions are central to mid-infrared optoelectronic technologies as quantum well infrared photodetectors (QWIPs) \cite{sirtori_nonparabolicity_1994,vigneron_quantum_2019} and quantum cascade lasers \cite{faist_quantum-cascade_2001,hofstetter_quantum-cascade-laser_2002,pflugl_high-temperature_2003}.

\begin{figure}
    \centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{Fig1_new.pdf}
    \caption{\label{Fig:1} \textbf{Light-dressed states framework.}  (a) Schematic representation of a single bound state $|\Phi_{\text{b}}\rangle$  coupled to a continuum of free states $|\Phi_\omega\rangle$  (blue shaded region)  via a semiclassical monochromatic laser field, with the ionisation threshold $\chi$. (b) Corresponding light-dressed states, where the discrete state $|\Psi_{\text{d}}\rangle$ and the continuum of states $|\Psi_{\bar{\omega}}\rangle$  (black shaded region) are arranged in adjacent photon ladders, characterised by photon numbers $n$ and $n+1$. }
\end{figure}

Although not often emphatised, the Fano approach to diagonalise bound-to-continuum transitions relies on the crucial hypothesis that the spectrum of the coupled system is entirely continuum. A more general mathematical treatment shows that, due to the non-analyticity of the spectrum of the Lee–Friedrichs Hamiltonian, discrete bound states can appear outside of the continuum for large enough coupling strengths \cite{passante_bound_2000}. In solid-state cavity quantum electrodynamics devices, these states can be interpreted as the result of the off-resonant strong coupling between the discrete and continuum parts of the spectrum \cite{cortese_strong_2019,cortese_exact_2022}, providing a photonic-based approach to modify the properties of material embedded in photonic resonators \cite{khurgin_excitonic_2001,brodbeck_experimental_2017}. 
These states have been recently observed in doped quantum wells \cite{cortese_excitons_2021,rajabali_polaritonic_2021}, where they correspond to photon-bound excitons, metastable bound states of the electron gas stabilised by their entanglement with a cavity photon \cite{kumar_microscopic_2022}. They are metastable because of the finite lifetime of the cavity photon binding the electrons.

Resonant fluorescence is a standard tool to investigate the physics of quantum emitters, with even the simplest two-level system leading to the complex physics of the Mollow triplet \cite{mollow_power_1969}, which has been observed both in atomic and molecular systems \cite{ortiz-gutierrez_mollow_2019,ng_observation_2022} and in semiconductor-based solid-state devices \cite{shammah_terahertz_2014,ulhaq_detuning-dependent_2013,moelbjerg_resonance_2012}.
If the laser pump can not be treated perturbatively, an even richer phenomenology arises, including the stabilization of ionised electronic states in Kramers–Henneberger atoms \cite{henneberger_perturbation_1968, matthews_amplification_2018}, where the time-averaged electromagnetic potential seen by the electron is strongly modified by the laser, leading to the emergence of novel bound atomic states. 

 Photon-bound excitons and Kramers–Henneberger atoms appear in radically different regimes of light-matter coupling: a quantum regime with quasi-resonant coupling to a single photon for the former, a semiclassical off-resonant, non-perturbative coherent pump for the latter. Also, the underlying physical mechanisms underpinning the stabilization in the two cases appear unrelated: a quasi-resonant strong coupling effect for the former, with a discrete resonance pushed out of the continuum by the level repulsion with the continuum of free states, and a time-averaged modified electromagnetic potential for the latter. Notwithstanding these differences it seems to us worth to ask the question of whether a different form of laser-stabilised bound states can be obtained by resonantly pumping a ionising transition, relying on a mechanism related to photon-bound excitons in the same way as the classical Rabi model leading to AC-Stark splitting is related to the quantum Rabi model with its Rabi splitting \cite{de_bernardis_tutorial_2024}.

 It turns out our hypothesis is correct, and we do observe a signature of these laser-stabilised resonances as narrow lines in the resonant fluorescence spectrum of the pumped system. Also in this case the laser-stabilised resonance is metastable, because the spontaneous emission processes responsible for the resonance fluorescence irreversibly empty the laser-stabilised resonance into the continuum of ionised states.

For the sake of clarity, most of the calculations have been moved to the appendices, leaving in the main body of the paper the minimum necessary to follow our arguments.

