	In the following we prove that topological corner states exist and are localized at corners if and only if the conditions~\eqref{eq5_16} and \eqref{eq5_17} both hold. Consider a semi-infinite kagome lattice with one corner boundary as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_3}(b). Inspired by Bloch's theorem, we let the corner-state solution be of the form
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_18}
		\theta_\mathrm{A}^{(m,n)} = \Theta_\mathrm{A} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\boldsymbol{k}\cdot\boldsymbol{r}} = \Theta_\mathrm{A} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(mk_1L+nk_2L)};
	\end{equation}
	similarly, let
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_18_add}
		\theta_\mathrm{B}^{(m,n)}=\Theta_\mathrm{B} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(mk_1L+nk_2L)}, \quad \theta_\mathrm{C}^{(m,n)}=\Theta_\mathrm{C} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(mk_1L+nk_2L)}.
	\end{equation}

	Because we wish to solve the corner state which is localized at the corner and exponentially decays along both directions $\boldsymbol{e}_1$ and $\boldsymbol{e}_2$, we define the decay  coefficient as $\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}=r_1$ and $\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_2L}=r_2$ (here $k_1$, $k_2$ are no longer real numbers and have a non-zero imaginary part), and hence
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_19}
		\theta_\mathrm{A(B,C)}=\Theta_\mathrm{A(B,C)} r_1^m r_2^n.
	\end{equation}
	We compare the balance equations of the bending moments at joint A in unit cells $(1,1)$ and $(2,1)$ (labeled by blue stars in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_3}(b)):
	\begin{align}
		0 &= 2 \left[ \frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)} + \frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)} \right] \theta_\mathrm{A}^{(1,1)} - \frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)} \theta_\mathrm{B}^{(1,1)} - \frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)} \theta_\mathrm{C}^{(1,1)}, \label{eq:kagomeSiteA11} \\
		0 &= 2 \left[ \frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)} + \frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)} \right] \theta_\mathrm{A}^{(2,1)} - \frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)} \theta_\mathrm{B}^{(2,1)} - \frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)} \theta_\mathrm{C}^{(2,1)} - \frac{C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)} \theta_\mathrm{B}^{(1,1)}; \label{eq:kagomeSiteA21} 
	\end{align}
	considering Eq.~\eqref{eq5_19}, we obtain $\theta^{(1,1)}_\mathrm{B}=0$. Thus $\Theta_\mathrm{B}=0$, and it follows that
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_20}
		\theta_{\mathrm{B}}^{(m,n)}=0.
	\end{equation}
	In a similar way, we compare the balance equations of the bending moments at joint A in unit cells $(1,1)$ and $(1,2)$ (labeled by blue stars), and $\theta^{(1,1)}_\mathrm{C}=0$ is obtained. Thus $\Theta_\mathrm{C}=0$, and it follows that
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_21}
		\theta_{\mathrm{C}}^{(m,n)}=0.
	\end{equation}
	At this time, Eqs.~\eqref{eq5_20} and \eqref{eq5_21} are substituted into the balance equation of the bending moments at joint A in unit cell (1,1), and we arrive at Eq.~\eqref{eq5_16}.

	Then, for joint C in unit cell $(m,n)$ (marked by one of the yellow stars), the balance equation of the bending moments is
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_22}
		t_2\theta_\mathrm{A}^{(m,n+1)}+t_1\theta_\mathrm{A}^{(m,n)}=0,
	\end{equation}
	where $t_1$ is defined as $-C(\beta l_1)/A(\beta l_1)$, and $t_2$ is defined as $-C(\beta l_2)/A(\beta l_2)$ (note that we have used Eqs.~\eqref{eq5_20} and \eqref{eq5_21}). For joint B in unit cell $(m,n)$, the balance equation of the bending moments is
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_23}
		t_2\theta_\mathrm{A}^{(m+1,n)}+t_1\theta_\mathrm{A}^{(m,n)}=0.
	\end{equation}
	By substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq5_19} into Eqs.~\eqref{eq5_22} and \eqref{eq5_23}, we obtain
	\[r_2=r_1=-\frac{t_1}{t_2}.
	\]
	Thus,
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_23add}
		\theta_\mathrm{A(B,C)}=\Theta_\mathrm{A(B,C)} \left(-\frac{t_1}{t_2}\right)^{m+n}.
	\end{equation}
	When $\lvert r_{1,2}\rvert<1$, i.e., $\lvert t_1\rvert<\lvert t_2\rvert$, such a state is localized at the corner, and decays exponentially away from the corner, which is consistent with the conclusion given by the expression~\eqref{eq5_17} that if and only if $\Big \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t l_1)}{A(\beta_t l_1)}\Big \rvert<\Big \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t l_2)}{A(\beta_t l_2)}\Big \rvert$, localized topological corner states exist. 

	It is easy to verify that the solution $\lvert \theta \rangle$ given by Eqs.~\eqref{eq5_20}, \eqref{eq5_21} and \eqref{eq5_23add} satisfies the balance equations of the bending moments at all joints in the semi-infinite structure, thus indeed a feasible solution for the corner state.

	\subsection{Edge states in kagome frames}
	In this section, we first present the frequency ranges of edge states in kagome grid-like frames, and then prove the correctness of the theoretical results.

	The frequencies of edge states in kagome frames satisfy
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_24}
		2\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]=\pm \left\lvert\frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)} + \frac{C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)} \exp{(\mathrm{i}k_{\parallel}L)}\right\rvert;
	\end{equation}
	Meanwhile, when and only when
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_25}
		2\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]\cdot \left[-\frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]>0
	\end{equation}
	or
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_25add}
		2\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]= -\frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}-\frac{C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)},
	\end{equation}
	edge states exist at the frequencies $\beta$ satisfying Eq.~\eqref{eq5_24}. In Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a), the range of $\beta$ corresponding to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_24} appears as the set of points on $\lambda=0$ between a pair of neighboring blue and orange solid curves, in the intervals $(\beta_0^{(n-1)},\beta_t^{(n)})$ and $(\beta_t^{(n)},\beta_0^{(n)})$. This set of frequencies (i.e., solutions to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_24}) have the same range with the corresponding set of frequencies for the square frame as given in Eq.~\eqref{eq5_10}, and hence the same existence property (i.e., existence within each interval $(\beta_0^{(n-1)},\beta_t^{(n)})$ and $(\beta_t^{(n)},\beta_0^{(n)})$) follows. Although the candidate frequencies of edge states are same, the judgment criterion of edge states for the kagome frame (the expressions~\eqref{eq5_25} and \eqref{eq5_25add}), is quite different from that for the square frame (the expression~\eqref{eq5_10a}). First, we examine the set of frequencies that satisfy the conditions~\eqref{eq5_24} and \eqref{eq5_25}. In Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a), the pink dotted curves represent function $\left[-\frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]$, and dark blue dashed curves represent function $\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]$. For each point set between a pair of blue and orange solid curves, when the values of the two functions $\left[-\frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]$ and $\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]$ have the same sign, the condition~\eqref{eq5_25} holds, and thus edge states exist at these $\beta$. Therefore, edge states exist at the frequency intervals marked by yellow line segments in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a). Then, the solutions $\beta^{r=0}_\mathrm{edge}$ that satisfy Eq.~\eqref{eq5_25add} but do not lie within the intervals of the yellow line segments are indicated by yellow stars. Several examples are taken for illustration: selected edge states at the frequency $\beta^{r=0}_\mathrm{edge}$, in the interval $(\beta_t^{(1)},\beta_0^{(1)})$, and in the interval $(\beta_0^{(1)},\beta_t^{(2)})$ (see Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a) for details) are demonstrated in Fig.~\ref{Fig_k_e}(a), (b)--(c), and (d)--(e), respectively. The bending deformations are localized near the edges of the structure, which is typical for edge states.

	\begin{figure}[tb!]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{kagome_edge.pdf}
		\caption{Edge modes of a kagome grid-like frame. (a) Edge state at frequency $\beta^{r=0}_\mathrm{edge}$. (b)--(c) Edge states corresponding to $k_{\parallel}L=\pi$ and $k_{\parallel}L=0$ in the interval $(\beta_t^{(1)},\beta_0^{(1)})$ shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}. (d)--(e) Edge states corresponding to $k_{\parallel}L=\pi$ and $k_{\parallel}L=0$ in the interval $(\beta_0^{(1)},\beta_t^{(2)})$ shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}.}
		\label{Fig_k_e}
	\end{figure}

	In the following, firstly we prove the correctness of the conditions \eqref{eq5_24} and \eqref{eq5_25} for the existence of edge states. Consider a semi-infinite kagome lattice as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_3}(c), where we employ Eqs.~\eqref{eq5_18} and \eqref{eq5_18_add} and attempt to solve edge states whose energy are localized on the boundary and decay exponentially into the bulk. Letting $\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_2L}=r$, we obtain
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_26}
		\theta_\mathrm{A(B,C)}=\Theta_\mathrm{A(B,C)} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}mk_1L} r^n.
	\end{equation}
	For edge states, the component $k_1$ along the edge is real. We compare the the balance equations of the bending moments at joint A in unit cells $(m,1)$ and $(m,2)$ (marked by blue stars in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_3}(c)), which leads to $\theta^{(m,1)}_\mathrm{C}=0$. Thus $\Theta_\mathrm{C}=0$, and it follows that
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_27}
		\theta_{\mathrm{C}}^{(m,n)}=0.
	\end{equation}
	Substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq5_27} into the balance equations of the bending moments for joints A and B in unit cell $(m,n)$, we obtain
	\begin{align}
		\label{eq5_28}
		\epsilon\theta_{\mathrm{A}}^{(m,n)} &= t_1\cdot \theta_{\mathrm{B}}^{(m,n)}+t_2\cdot \theta_{\mathrm{B}}^{(m,n)}\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}k_1L},\\
		\label{eq5_29}
		\epsilon\theta_{\mathrm{B}}^{(m,n)} &= t_1\cdot \theta_{\mathrm{A}}^{(m,n)}+t_2\cdot \theta_{\mathrm{A}}^{(m,n)}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L},
	\end{align}
	where $\epsilon\equiv-2\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]$, which is also the eigenvalue of the dynamical matrix with all diagonal elements set to zero. By combining Eqs.~\eqref{eq5_28} and \eqref{eq5_29}, we obtain the candidate frequencies where edge states of the kagome frame may occur, that is, the candidate frequencies are solutions to
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_29add}
		\lambda_\mathrm{edge}^\mathrm{kagome}=-\epsilon \pm \lvert t_1+t_2\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert=0,
	\end{equation}
	which are precisely the solutions to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_24}; moreover, we obtain
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_30add}
		\theta_{\mathrm{B}}^{(m,n)}=\theta_{\mathrm{A}}^{(m,n)}\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi},
	\end{equation}
	where
		\begin{equation}
			\label{eqphiDef}
			\phi=\arg{\frac{t_1+t_2\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}}{\epsilon}}.
		\end{equation}

	Next, for joint C in unit cell $(m,n)$ (highlighted with a yellow star in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_3}(c)), the balance equation of the bending moments is
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_30}
		t_2\left[\theta_{\mathrm{A}}^{(m,n+1)}+\theta_{\mathrm{B}}^{(m-1,n+1)}\right]+t_1\left[\theta_{\mathrm{A}}^{(m,n)}+\theta_{\mathrm{B}}^{(m,n)}\right]=0,
	\end{equation}
	which is rewritten as
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_31add} t_2\left[1+\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(\phi-k_1L)}\right]r\cdot\theta_{\mathrm{A}}^{(m,n)}+t_1\left[1+\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi} \right]\theta_{\mathrm{A}}^{(m,n)}=0,
	\end{equation}
	and therefore we have
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_31}
		r=-\frac{t_1}{t_2}\cdot\left[ \frac{1+\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}}{1+\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(\phi-k_1L)}}  \right] \quad (1+\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(\phi-k_1L)}\neq 0).
	\end{equation}
	For simplicity, in the following we let $t_1=1$, $t_2=t$. (For general cases in which $t_1\ne 1$, analogous conclusions can be reached by acknowledging the properties of matrix eigenvalues under scalar multiplication of a matrix, and taking $(t_1, t_2, \epsilon) \mapsto (ct_1, ct_2, c\epsilon)$.) From Eq.~\eqref{eqphiDef}, we have
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_32}
		\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}= \sgn(\epsilon) \frac{1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}}{\lvert1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert}.
	\end{equation}
	Therefore, when $\epsilon>0$,
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_33}
		r=-\frac{1}{t}\left[\frac{\lvert 1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert+(1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L})}{\lvert 1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert+(t+ \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}k_1L})}\right];
	\end{equation}
	when $\epsilon<0$,
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_34}
		r=-\frac{1}{t}\left[\frac{\lvert 1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert-(1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L})}{\lvert 1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert-(t+ \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}k_1L})}\right].
	\end{equation}
	Here we discuss whether the modes are localized at the boundary by enumerating the sign of $t$ and also the sign of $\epsilon$.

	First consider the case when $t>0$, and (1) $\epsilon>0$. Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_5}(a) shows the geometric relations of the parameters in the expression of $r$ (here we simply denote $k_1L$ as $k$), and by using trigonometric relations we have 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_35}
		\lvert r \rvert=\frac{\sin(k-\phi)}{\sin\phi}\cdot \frac{\cos \frac{\phi}{2}}{\cos \frac{k-\phi}{2}}=\frac{\sin \frac{k-\phi}{2}}{\sin\frac{\phi}{2}}.
	\end{equation}
	When $t>1$ (which is the case shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_5}(a)), it holds that $k-\phi<\phi$, and hence $\lvert r \rvert<1$, indicating that a mode localized at the boundary exists; on the other hand, when $0<t<1$, we have $k-\phi>\phi$, and hence $\lvert r \rvert>1$, indicating that no mode is localized at the boundary.

	(2) $\epsilon<0$. In this case the geometric relations of the parameters in the expression of $r$ are shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_5}(b), and we have
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_36}
		\lvert r \rvert=\frac{\sin(k-\phi')}{\sin\phi'}\cdot \frac{\sin \frac{\phi'}{2}}{\sin \frac{k-\phi'}{2}}=\frac{\cos \frac{k-\phi'}{2}}{\cos\frac{\phi'}{2}}.
	\end{equation}
	When $t>1$ (as is the case in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_5}(b)), it holds that $k-\phi'<\phi'$, and hence $\lvert r \rvert>1$, indicating no localization at the boundary; on the other hand, when $0<t<1$, we have $k-\phi'>\phi'$, and hence $\lvert r \rvert<1$, indicating that a mode is localized at the boundary.

	Second, consider the case when $t<0$, and (1) $\epsilon>0$. By a method similar to that illustrated in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_5}, it is found that none of the modes exhibit localization properties. (2) $\epsilon<0$: the modes are localized at the boundaries.

	Finally, we arrive at the conclusion: if and only if $t_2>t_1$ and $\epsilon>0$, or $t_2<t_1$ and $\epsilon<0$, topological edge states exist at frequencies $\beta$ solved from Eq.~\eqref{eq5_24}. The condition~\eqref{eq5_25} is proved.

	\begin{figure}[tb!]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig_5_5.pdf}
		\caption{Geometric relation of parameters associated with the decay coefficient $r$ of the kagome frame. Here $k\equiv k_1L$. (a) When $\epsilon>0$ and $t>1$, the numerator $[\lvert 1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert+(1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L})]$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq5_33} is indicated by the blue vector in the complex plane, and the denominator $[\lvert 1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert+(t+ \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}k_1L})]$ is indicated by the purple vector. (b) When $\epsilon<0$ and $t>1$, the numerator $[\lvert 1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert-(1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L})]$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq5_34} is indicated by the blue vector in the complex plane, and the denominator $[\lvert 1+t \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}k_1L}\rvert-(t+ \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}k_1L})]$ is indicated by the purple vector. Although both illustrations implicitly assume $k = k_1L \in (0,\pi)$, for the opposite case $k = k_1L \in (-\pi,0)$, it suffices to note that $\lvert r\rvert$ is invariant under $k_1L \mapsto -k_1L$.}
		\label{Fig_5_5}
	\end{figure}

	In the above discussions, we have not yet considered the case of $r=0$ (here the modes only have projections on the boundary), i.e., $\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\phi}=-1$. From Eq.~\eqref{eq5_30add}, we obtain $\theta_\mathrm{B}=-\theta_\mathrm{A}$; substituting this into Eqs.~\eqref{eq5_28} and \eqref{eq5_29} yields $k_1L=0$ or $\pi$, along with the existence condition of edge states for the case of $r=0$:
	\begin{align}
		\label{eq5_36add1}
		-\epsilon &= t_1 + t_2 \quad (k_1L=0\ \text{and}\ \phi=\pi),\\
		\label{eq5_36add2}
		-\epsilon &= t_1 - t_2 \quad (k_1L=\pi\ \text{and}\ \phi=\pi).
	\end{align}
	Substituting Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add2} into the left-hand side of the condition~\eqref{eq5_25}, it follows that $-\epsilon\cdot(t_1-t_2)=(t_1-t_2)^2>0$, and hence the solutions to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add2} must necessarily fall within the solution set of the condition~\eqref{eq5_25}, that is, the solutions $\beta$ to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add2} lie within (more precisely, lie at certain endpoints of) the yellow line segments in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a), as intersections of blue curves corresponding to $\lambda_\mathrm{edge}^\mathrm{kagome}$ at $k_1L=\pi$ with the axis $\lambda=0$.

	However, Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1} does not necessarily satisfy the condition~\eqref{eq5_25} and thus requires additional discussions. (From the calculations in the next section, we find that the edge-state frequency solutions to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1} are also frequency solutions for the bulk states corresponding to $\lambda_3^\Gamma=\lambda_3^\mathrm{K}=0$. This indicates the existence of an entire bulk band (i.e., a flat band) at each of such frequencies, appearing in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(b) as the intersections of coincident black solid and purple dashed curves with the 
	horizontal axis.) When $t_1+t_2>0$, Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1} is produced from Eq.~\eqref{eq5_29add} by taking the negative sign, so the solutions to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1} in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a) are the intersections of the lower-half orange curves and the axis $\lambda=0$; when $t_1+t_2<0$, Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1} corresponds to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_29add} with a positive sign, and thus the solutions to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1} in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a) are the intersections of the upper-half orange curves and the axis $\lambda=0$. These solutions may or may not be within the range given by the condition~\eqref{eq5_25} (i.e., may or may not lie at endpoints of the yellow line segments in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a)), depending on whether $\lvert t_1\rvert > \lvert t_2\rvert$ or $\lvert t_1\rvert < \lvert t_2\rvert$. We identify $\beta$ which satisfy Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1} but lie outside the yellow line segments with yellow stars in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(a). Equation~\eqref{eq5_36add1} is precisely the condition \eqref{eq5_25add} given at the beginning of this section. It is noted that the solutions to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1} render both the numerator and denominator of Eq.~\eqref{eq5_31} zero; formally, Eq.~\eqref{eq5_31add} holds for whatever value of $r$. In fact, at the solutions $\beta$ to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_36add1}, edge states and bulk states coexist.

	\subsection{Bulk states in kagome frames}
	From the expressions in Eq.~\eqref{eq5_16_add1}, we obtain the eigenvalues of matrix $H^{\text{kagome}}_{\text{Bloch}}$ as
	\begin{align}
		\label{eq5_37}
		\lambda_{1,2}&=-\epsilon+\frac{1}{2}\left[t_1+t_2\pm\sqrt{9t_1^2+9t_2^2+t_1t_2\left(-6+8\cos{k_xL}+16\cos{\frac{k_xL}{2}}\cos{\frac{\sqrt{3}k_yL}{2}}\right)} \right],\\
		\label{eq5_38}
		\lambda_{3}&=-\epsilon-t_1-t_2.
	\end{align}
	The bulk bands are given by $\lambda_i = 0$ ($i=1$, $2$, $3$). When $\beta$ is fixed, the upper and lower edges of the eigenvalue bands are attained at the point $\Gamma$ (i.e., $(k_x,k_y)=(0,0)$) and the point $\rm{K}$ (i.e., $(k_x,k_y) = \pm\bigl(\frac{2\pi}{3L},\frac{2\sqrt{3}\pi}{3L}\bigr)$) in the Brillouin zone. For point $\Gamma$,
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_39}
		\lambda_{1,2}^{\Gamma} = -\epsilon + \frac{1}{2} (t_1+t_2\pm3\lvert t_1+t_2\rvert);
	\end{equation}
	for point $\rm{K}$,
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_40}
		\lambda_{1,2}^{\rm{K}} = -\epsilon + \frac{1}{2} (t_1+t_2\pm3\lvert t_1-t_2\rvert);
	\end{equation}
	$\lambda_{3}^{\Gamma}=\lambda_{3}^{\rm{K}}$ has the same value as one of $\lambda_{1,2}^{\Gamma}$. Thus, the edges of the frequency bands for bulk states correspond to the eigenvalues $\lambda_{1,2}^{\Gamma}$ (black solid curves in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(b)) or $\lambda_{1,2}^{\rm{K}}$ (purple dashed curves in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(b)) being equal to zero. Comparing the values of Eqs.~\eqref{eq5_39} and \eqref{eq5_40}, it is concluded: when $t_1t_2>0$, the outermost edges of the eigenvalue bands (viewed vertically) correspond to $\lambda_{1,2}^{\Gamma}$, which are black solid curves; when $t_1t_2<0$, the outermost edges of the eigenvalue bands correspond to $\lambda_{1,2}^{\rm{K}}$, i.e., purple dashed curves. Here $t_1t_2=\frac{C(\beta l_1)C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_1)A(\beta l_2)}$; since function $C(\beta l)$ is always positive for $\beta>0$, and function $A(\beta l_1)A(\beta l_2)$ changes its sign at each $\beta_0^{(n)}$, the associated wavevector (i.e., point $\Gamma$ or point $\rm{K}$) of the outermost bulk band edges (disregarding the flat band $\lambda_{3}$ here) will change between neighboring intervals $(\beta_0^{(n-1)},\beta_0^{(n)})$.

	As $\lambda_3$ is independent of the wavenumbers $k_x$ and $k_y$, the frequency bands given by $\lambda_3 = 0$ are flat bands. Remarkably, in the kagome frame, there exists a large number of such flat bands (infinitely many, in theory), due to the fact that in every frequency interval $(\beta_0^{(n-1)}, \beta_0^{(n)})$ lies one such band, as implied by the existence property of Eq.~\eqref{eq5_24} detailed earlier. These flat bands are depicted in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_4}(b) as \emph{coincident} black solid and purple dashed curves intersecting the horizontal axis.

	\subsection{Summary about frequency intervals of modes in kagome lattice}
	Finally, we summarize the frequency ranges of the corner, edge and bulk states in kagome grid-like frames. Figure~\ref{Fig_k_x} shows in the case of $t_2>0$, the spectrum of the kagome lattice $\epsilon$ (called the energy in the tight-binding model), as it varies with $t_1/t_2$ (called the ratio of hopping amplitudes in the tight-binding model); when $t_2<0$, compared with the situation of $t_2>0$, the frequency spectrum of kagome lattice $\epsilon$ only need a mirror reflection about $\epsilon=0$. In the kagome grid-like frame structures discussed in this paper, $\epsilon$ is defined as $-2\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right]$, and $t_{1(2)}$ is defined as $-\frac{C(\beta l_{1(2)})}{A(\beta l_{1(2)})}$. From the relation of $\epsilon$ and $t_1/t_2$ shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_k_x}, we can solve the frequencies $\beta$ of the topological corner states, edge states and bulk states in kagome grid-like frame structures. In Fig.~\ref{Fig_k_x}, the dark blue lines denote corner states, which exist in the parameter range $-1<t_1/t_2<1$. Edge states exist in the pink regions and also at the pink line $\epsilon=-t_1-t_2$. Bulk states exist in the gray regions, which also contains the black thick lines $\epsilon=-t_1-t_2$.
	\begin{figure}[tb]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=0.8\linewidth]{kagome_xiangtu.pdf}
		\caption{Frequency spectrum of the modes in kagome lattice. Gray, pink and blue regions represent the ranges where bulk, edge and corner states exist, respectively.}
		\label{Fig_k_x}
	\end{figure}

	\section{Topological bridge-like frames}
	In this section, we introduce the topological bridge-like frame as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_6}, which consist of joined uniform elastic beams. All the exterior ends of the outermost beam segments are clamped ends. The lengths of $2N+1$ horizontal beam segments are alternating $l_2$, $l_1$, $l_2$, \dots, $l_1$, $l_2$; and the length of $2N$ vertical beam segments is all $l_0$. The motion of the bridge-like frame is described by rotations at the $2N$ joints and deflections of the horizontal and vertical beam segments. Here the existence of topological edge states in the spectrum of bridge-like frame is investigated and the frequencies of topological edge states are given. 
	\begin{figure}[tb]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig_5_6.pdf}
		\caption{Bridge-like frame without translational displacements at the joints, where a unit cell is marked by red dashed lines.}
		\label{Fig_5_6}
	\end{figure}

	\subsection{Topological edge states and the frequency spectrum in bridge-like frames}
	According to the balance equation of bending moments at the $2N$ rigid joints formed by the intersections of horizontal and vertical beam segments, the dynamical equation can be written in matrix form as
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_41}
		H^\mathrm{bridge}\vert \theta \rangle =0,
	\end{equation}
	where $H^\mathrm{bridge}$ is the dynamical matrix of bridge-like frames. The main-diagonal elements of $H^\mathrm{bridge}$ are $\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}+\frac{B(\beta l_0)}{A(\beta l_0)}\right]$, and the sub-diagonal elements of $H^\mathrm{bridge}$ are alternating $-\frac{C(\beta l_{1})}{A(\beta l_{1})}$ and $-\frac{C(\beta l_{2})}{A(\beta l_{2})}$. Since the dynamical matrix of bridge-like frames has chiral symmetry (after subtraction of a scalar matrix), we get the conclusion: (1) the frequencies of topological edge states $\beta_t$ in the bridge-like frame must satisfy 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_42}
		\frac{B(\beta_t l_1)}{A(\beta_t l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta_t l_2)}{A(\beta_t l_2)}+\frac{B(\beta_t l_0)}{A(\beta_t l_0)}=0,
	\end{equation}
	there is one and only one solution $\beta_t^{(n)}$ in each interval $(\beta_0^{(n-1)},\beta_0^{(n)})$, where $\beta_0^{(n)}$ is the root of function $A(\beta l_0) A(\beta l_1) A(\beta l_2) = 0$ and $\beta_0^{(0)}=0$ is specified.

	(2) for a specific $\beta_t^{(n)}$, if and only if
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_43}
		\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t^{(n)} l_1)}{A(\beta_t^{(n)} l_1)}\right \rvert<\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t^{(n)} l_2)}{A(\beta_t^{(n)} l_2)}\right \rvert,
	\end{equation}
	topological edge states exist at frequency $\beta_t^{(n)}$.

	The above conclusions are illustrated by an example structure with $l_1=40\,\mathrm{mm}$, $l_2=50\,\mathrm{mm}$, and $l_0=35\,\mathrm{mm}$. The eigenvalue spectrum of $H^\mathrm{bridge}$ is shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_7}(a).
	\begin{figure}[tb!]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig_5_7.pdf}
		\caption{Frequency spectra of the topological bridge-like frame and topological continuous beam structure. (a) Frequency spectrum of a topological bridge-like frame, with length parameters $l_1=40\,\mathrm{mm}$, $l_2=50\,\mathrm{mm}$ and $l_0=35\,\mathrm{mm}$. Gray curves in the figure represent eigenvalues of dynamical matrix of the frame, and all the intersections of gray curves and the black horizontal line $\lambda=0$ constitute the frequency spectrum of the frame. Pink (green) vertical lines denote the roots of equation $A(\beta_0 l_{2(1)})=0$. Red vertical lines represent the roots of equation $A(\beta_0 l_{0})=0$. The frequencies of topological edge states are represented by the intersections of dark blue dashed curves (black solid curves) and $\lambda=0$, marked by red arrows. (b) Spectrum of a topological continuous beam structure for comparison, with length parameters $l_1=40\,\mathrm{mm}$, $l_2=50\,\mathrm{mm}$.}
		\label{Fig_5_7}
	\end{figure}
	All the intersections of gray eigenvalue curves and horizontal axis $\lambda=0$ constitute the frequency spectrum of the structure; topological edge states exist at frequencies indicated by red arrows, where the ordinates of blue and orange dashed curves have opposite signs, i.e., Eq.~\eqref{eq5_43} is satisfied. Compared with the frequency spectrum of a continuous beam structure with the same length parameter $(l_1, l_2)$ as shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_7}(b), the following rules are obtained: (1) ``Eigenvalue crossing" of the two contrast structures occurs at the same $\beta$. This is due to the phenomenon of ``eigenvalue crossing" is equivalent to $\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}\right \rvert=\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}\right \rvert$, that is, equivalent to the equation $[C(\beta l_1)A(\beta l_2)+C(\beta l_2)A(\beta l_1)][C(\beta l_1)A(\beta l_2)-C(\beta l_2)A(\beta l_1)]=0$ (the corresponding $\beta$ are the intersections of orange or blue dashed curves with the horizontal axis $\lambda=0$ in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_7}). However, the length parameters of the two systems $(l_1, l_2)$ do not change (meanwhile, the positions of pink and green lines are also unchanged), thus ``eigenvalue crossing" appears at the same $\beta$. (2)  Due to the introduction of vertical beams with length $l_0$ in the bridge-like frame, the number of $\beta_0^{(n)}$ increases, Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_7}(a) displays extra red vertical lines (corresponding to roots of $A(\beta l_0)=0$) compared to (b). This results in an increase in the number of $\beta_t^{(n)}$ in the frequency solution set $\{\beta_t\}$ for candidate topological edge states, and thus an increase in the number of topological edge states. The increase in $\beta_t^{(n)}$ is equal to the number of red vertical lines, which corresponds to the number of roots of function $A(\beta l_0)=0$ within the given interval.

	For the topological edge states at frequencies $\beta_t^{(1)}$ and $\beta_t^{(7)}$ in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_7}, the modes are shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_f_e}. At these two frequencies, the bending deformations of the beam segments are localized at the two boundaries; moreover, we find that the topological states are doubly degenerate at frequency $\beta_t^{(1)}$ in Figs.~\ref{Fig_f_e}(a)-(b), where the vibration of the horizontal beam dominates, similar to those in the continuous beam structure; whereas the topological edge states at frequency $\beta_t^{(7)}$ are induced by the introduction of vertical beams, consequently leading to dominant vibrations in the vertical beams.
	\begin{figure}[tbp]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{frame_edge.pdf}
		\caption{Topological edge modes of bridge-like frames. The modes are doubly degenerate at (a)--(b) frequency $\beta_{t}^{(1)}$, (c)--(d) frequency $\beta_{t}^{(7)}$, and localized at the two boundaries.}
		\label{Fig_f_e}
	\end{figure}

	In the following, we prove the existence of topological states within the bandgaps. Applying Bloch's theorem to Eq.~\eqref{eq5_41}, let function
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_44}
		g(\beta)=\left\lvert-\frac{C(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)} - \frac{C(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)} \exp{(-\mathrm{i}kL)}\right\rvert^2-\left[\frac{B(\beta l_1)}{A(\beta l_1)}+\frac{B(\beta l_2)}{A(\beta l_2)}+\frac{B(\beta l_0)}{A(\beta l_0)}\right]^2.
	\end{equation}
	The solutions $\beta$ to $g(\beta)=0$ are frequencies of bulk states in bridge-like frames; by sweeping the wavenumber $k \in (-\pi/L, \pi/L]$ across the Brillouin zone, the resulting frequency range constitutes the bulk bands. From Eq.~\eqref{eq5_42}, at $\beta=\beta_t$, if $\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t l_1)}{A(\beta_t l_1)}\right \rvert\neq\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t l_2)}{A(\beta_t l_2)}\right \rvert$, then $g(\beta_t)>0$. At $\beta=\beta_0^{(n)}$ ($\beta_0^{(n)}\in (\beta_t^{(n)},\beta_t^{(n+1)})$), when $\beta_0^{(n)}$ satisfies $A(\beta l_1) = 0$ or $A(\beta l_2) = 0$ (which corresponds to a pink or green vertical line observed between each pair of adjacent dark blue dashed curves in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_7}), then $g(\beta)\rightarrow -\infty$ holds for one of the unilateral limits as $\beta\rightarrow\beta_0^{(n)\pm}$, which leads to the existence of a frequency band within the range $(\beta_t^{(n)},\beta_t^{(n+1)})$; when $\beta_0^{(n)}$ satisfies $A(\beta l_{0})=0$ (a red vertical line appears between two adjacent dark blue dashed curves), then $g(\beta)\rightarrow -\infty$ holds under the bilateral limit $\beta\rightarrow\beta_0^{(n)\pm}$, which leads to the existence of a frequency band in each interval $(\beta_t^{(n)},\beta_0^{(n)})$ and $(\beta_0^{(n)},\beta_t^{(n+1)})$. Therefore, from the above analysis, we get the conclusions when $\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t l_1)}{A(\beta_t l_1)}\right \rvert\neq\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t l_2)}{A(\beta_t l_2)}\right \rvert$, frequency bands are nondegenerate, and the topological edge states exist within the bandgaps. The introduction of vertical beams leads to an increase in the number of frequency bands in the spectrum, where the increased quantity equals twice the number of roots of the function $A(\beta l_0)=0$ (which also corresponds to the number of red vertical lines appearing in the figure).

	Finally, by changing the length parameters, that is, the ratio of $l_1$ to $l_2$, the topological phase transitions of the bridge-like frame are investigated. We choose the same geometric parameters $l_{\mathrm{ave}}$ and $\Lambda$ as those in the continuous beam structures \citep{SUN2025105935}, and set the length of the vertical beam as $l_0=35\,\mathrm{mm}$. Finally, the spectrum with the change of the geometric parameter $\Lambda$ is shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_8}.
	\begin{figure}[tb!]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{Fig_5_8.pdf}
		\caption{Spectrum of the bridge-like frame about the geometric parameter $\Lambda$, where $\Lambda$ is defined such that the lengths of the horizontal beams are $ l_1 =(1+\Lambda) l_\mathrm{ave}$ and $ l_2 =(1-\Lambda) l_\mathrm{ave}$ (here $l_\mathrm{ave} = 45\,\mathrm{mm}$ is fixed). The length of vertical beams is $l_0=35\,\mathrm{mm}$. Black dashed lines $\Lambda=-1/9$ correspond to the spectrum of the structure in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_7}(a). Two red horizontal lines are roots of $A(\beta l_0)=0$.}
		\label{Fig_5_8}
	\end{figure}
	We get the conclusions: (1) Compared to the topological continuous beam structure, the introduction of vertical beams with length $l_0$ will change the topological phase transition points and frequencies of bandgap closing. This is because topological phase transitions occur at $\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t l_1)}{A(\beta_t l_1)}\right \rvert=\left \lvert\frac{C(\beta_t l_2)}{A(\beta_t l_2)}\right \rvert$, although length $l_{1,2}$ in the equation do not change, $\beta_t$ will change due to the introduction of $l_0$ (because $\beta_t$ is the function of $l_{0,1,2}$). Therefore, the geometric parameter $\Lambda$ corresponding to topological phase transition points will change. (2) In Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_8}, there is no topological phase transitions at the red horizontal line, but the number of the bandgap which contains the topological edge states on the two sides of the red horizontal line is differ by one number, because it is known from the above explanations that there must be no topological states exist between the two nearest bands on both sides of the red line.

	\subsection{Topological index related to the Zak phase that determines the existence of an edge state}
	In this section, we directly present a method for determining the existence of topological edge states based on the Zak phase \citep{Zak1989,Xiao2015exp} of frequency bands. The proof uses essentially the same technique as that in \citet{SUN2025105935}, and we outline the results here.

	(1) If the interval $(\beta_t^{(n-1)},\beta_t^{(n)})$ contains $\beta_0^{(n)}$ which satisfies $A(\beta  l_{1(2)})=0$, then there is one frequency band in the interval $(\beta_t^{(n-1)},\beta_t^{(n)})$, and the Zak phase of this frequency band is related to the existence of topological edge states at frequency $\beta_t^{(n)}$. However, the existence of topological edge states are related to not only the Zak phase of this frequency band, but also the number of ``eigenvalue crossing" $l$ at the positive eigenvalue in the interval index which determines the existence of topological edge states is defined as 
	\begin{equation}
		\label{eq5_45}
		\mu=\gamma+\pi\cdot l \mod 2\pi,
	\end{equation}
	where when the topological index takes the value of 1, topological edge states exist at $\beta_t^{(n)}$; when the topological index takes the value of 0, no topological edge states exist at $\beta_t^{(n)}$.

	(2) If the interval $(\beta_t^{(n-1)},\beta_t^{(n)})$ contains $\beta_0^{(n)}$ which satisfies $A(\beta  l_{0})=0$, then there exists a frequency band in each interval $(\beta_t^{(n-1)},\beta_0^{(n)})$ and $(\beta_0^{(n)},\beta_t^{(n)})$; The Zak phase ($\gamma\mod 2\pi$) of the frequency band within the interval $(\beta_t^{(n-1)},\beta_0^{(n)})$ directly determines the topological edge states at frequency $\beta_t^{(n-1)}$; similarly, the Zak phase of the frequency band in the interval $(\beta_0^{(n)},\beta_t^{(n)})$ also directly determines the topological edge states at frequency $\beta_t^{(n)}$.
	\begin{figure}[tb!]
		\centering
		\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{Fig_5_9.pdf}
		\caption{Eigenvalue spectra of the periodic bridge-like frames with parameters $l_1=29\,\mathrm{mm}$, $l_2=50\,\mathrm{mm}$ and $l_0=40\,\mathrm{mm}$ (a), $l_1=29\,\mathrm{mm}$, $l_2=50\,\mathrm{mm}$ and $l_0=31\,\mathrm{mm}$ (b), where white stars indicate that a ``eigenvalue crossing" occurs at a positive eigenvalue.}
		\label{Fig_5_9}
	\end{figure}

	In the following, we illustrate the aforementioned criterion for determining topological edge states with an example structure. First, we select a bridge-like frame structure with specific length parameters $l_1=29\,\mathrm{mm}$, $l_2=50\,\mathrm{mm}$, so that the pink and green vertical lines are positioned closely to each other. The frequency spectrum is shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_9}(a), the Zak phases of the frequency bands from low to high frequencies are successively $\pi,~\pi,~\pi,~0,~\pi,~0,~0,~\pi,~0,~0,~0,~\pi$. The ``eigenvalue crossing" at a positive eigenvalues occur in the interval $(\beta_t^{(3)},\beta_t^{(4)})$, and thus from Eq.~\eqref{eq5_45}, the Zak phase of the fifth frequency band in the interval $(\beta_t^{(3)},\beta_t^{(4)})$ is is inversely related to the existence of topological edge states at frequency $\beta_t^{(4)}$. Meanwhile, let us verify the aforementioned criterion (2): the Zak phase of the third frequency band within the interval $(\beta_t^{(2)},\beta_0^{(2)})$ is consistent with the existence of topological edge states at frequency $\beta_t^{(2)}$; similarly, the Zak phase of the fourth band in $(\beta_0^{(2)},\beta_t^{(3)})$ is also consistence with the existence of topological states at $\beta_t^{(3)}$.

	Next, we select a bridge-like frame with specific length parameters $l_0=31\,\mathrm{mm}$, $l_2=50\,\mathrm{mm}$, so that the pink vertical line $A(\beta l_2)=0$ and the red vertical line $A(\beta l_0)=0$ are positioned closely to each other. The frequency spectrum is shown in Fig.~\ref{Fig_5_9}(b), the Zak phases of the frequency bands from low to high frequencies are successively $\pi,~0,~0,~\pi,~0,~\pi,~0,~0,~0,~\pi$. The ``eigenvalue crossing" at a positive eigenvalues occur in the interval $(\beta_t^{(2)},\beta_0^{(2)})$; from criterion (2) we know: the Zak phase of the third frequency band in the interval $(\beta_t^{(2)},\beta_0^{(2)})$ coincides with the existence of topological edge states at frequency $\beta_t^{(2)}$, and thus the ``eigenvalue crossing" at a positive eigenvalue here do not affect the existence of topological edge states at frequency $\beta_t^{(2)}$.

	\section{Conclusions}
	In this paper, we present the analytical results for the topological dynamics of the continuum grid-like frames, demonstrating the validity of theoretical framework motivated by the topological continuous beams, for studying higher-order topological structures with complex spectra. First, by theoretically solve the frequency spectra and the corresponding mode shapes of higher-order topological mechanical structures such as square grid-like frames and kagome grid-like frames, we obtain the accurate analytical expressions for frequencies of topological corner states, edge states, and bulk states in these two kinds of higher-order frame structures, applicable to all bands from low to high frequencies. This enables the identification of topological corner and edge states, which is challenging with usual numerical solving techniques, since their frequencies can be immensely overlapped with bulk states. We derive a clear analytical criterion for topological phase transitions and rigorously prove that corner states exist within the bandgap of edge states. Then, by theoretically analyze the frequency spectrum of the first-order topological mechanical structures such as bridge-like frames, we obtain the accurate analytical expressions for frequencies of topological edge states and bulk states, and rigorously establish a quantitative method to determine the existence of topological edge states according to the bulk topological phases. The clear demonstration of mode distributions in the frequency spectra of topological grid-like frame structures not only pioneers the rigorous theoretical analysis of topological dynamics in higher-order continuum structures, but also promotes practical implementation and wider adoption of grid-like frames, a broad class of topological continuum structures, in industrial engineering.


