\begin{abstract}
Altermagnets and compensated ferrimagnets have attracted considerable attention as key building blocks in spintronics devices, owing to their dual advantages of ferromagnets and antiferromagnets. In particular, compensated ferrimagnets exhibit isotropic spin splitting in their electronic and magnon dispersions, despite having zero net magnetization. This characteristic feature enables spin transport phenomena analogous to those in ferromagnets. However, quantitative evaluations of such spin transport phenomena, particularly in comparison to conventional ferromagnets, remain unexplored. Here we investigate spin pumping and spin Seebeck effects in a junction between a compensated ferrimagnet and a normal metal using the non-equilibrium Green's function method. We find that the isotropic spin splitting in magnon dispersions gives rise to spin transport phenomena similar to those observed in ferromagnets. 
Our results provide a solid theoretical foundation 
for the applications of exotic collinear antiferromagnets with spin splitting to spintronics devices.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Collinear antiferromagnets with zero net magnetization have long been believed to exhibit no spin splitting in their electronic or magnon dispersions. 
However, recent studies have shown that spin splitting can emerge even in collinear antiferromagnets with zero net magnetization, if there is asymmetry between up-spin and down-spin sites~\cite{Leuken_PRL1995,Akai_PRL2006,Kawamura_PRL2024,Liu_PRL2025,Smejkal2022,Smejkal_PRX2022b,Mazin2022,Noda_PCCS2016,Ahn_PRB2019,Hayami2020,Yuan2023,Naka2019}.
One representative example is the altermagnet~\cite{Smejkal2022,Smejkal_PRX2022b,Mazin2022,Noda_PCCS2016,Naka2019,Ahn_PRB2019,Hayami2020,Yuan2023}, 
in which up-spin and down-spin sites are not connected by translational symmetry 
but are related by other symmetries, such as rotational symmetries. 
This leads to anisotropic spin splitting in momentum space. 
In contrast, compensated ferrimagnets have completely 
inequivalent up-spin and down-spin sites, 
resulting in isotropic ($s$-wave) spin splitting in their electronic band dispersions~\cite{Leuken_PRL1995,Akai_PRL2006,Kawamura_PRL2024,Liu_PRL2025}.

Both altermagnets and compensated ferrimagnets combine two key advantages: (i) zero net magnetization, resulting in the absence of stray magnetic fields, as in antiferromagnets, and (ii) finite spin splitting, as in ferromagnets. 
Therefore, they are considered key building blocks for spintronic devices. 
In the bulk systems, spin current generation in altermagnets has been studied~\cite{Naka2019,Hodt2024,Cui2023}, and the spin Seebeck effect (SSE)~\cite{Uchida2008,Jaworski2010,Slachter2010,Uchida2011,Jaworski2012,Adachi2013} has also been explored. 
However, spin transport phenomena in junctions between normal metals and compensated ferrimagnets---such as spin pumping and the spin Seebeck effect---have not yet been studied in detail.
Since junction systems serve as the fundamental units of spintronics devices, understanding spin transport in such systems is essential for realistic device applications.
Moreover, in junction systems, spin transport phenomena include summation over contributions from both up-spin and down-spin sites.
As a result, altermagnets do not exhibit spin transport behaviors similar to ferromagnets since their up-spin and down-spin sites are locally equivalent.
In contrast, compensated ferrimagnets with isotropic spin splitting are expected to show spin transport phenomena analogous to those in ferromagnets.

In this paper, we investigate spin transport, such as the spin pumping~\cite{Tserkovnyak2002,Tserkovnyak2005,Ando2011,Han2020,Ominato2025} and the spin Seebeck effects~\cite{Uchida2008,Jaworski2010,Slachter2010,Uchida2011,Jaworski2012,Adachi2013} in a junction between a compensated ferrimagnet and a normal metal 
using the non-equilibrium Green's function method. 
Schematic representations of the spin pumping and the SSE are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:schem}.
To quantitatively evaluate spin transport phenomena, we employ a simple model of 
a compensated ferrimagnet, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:model}. 
The model contains two spin sites with different internal degrees of freedom. 
By choosing the coupling constants $J_1 \ne J_2$, we introduce an inequivalence between up- and down-spin sites, realizing a compensated ferrimagnet. 
This model can be seen as a strong-coupling version of models that realize compensated ferrimagnets in organic compounds~\cite{Kawamura_PRL2024}.

We show that the inequivalence between $J_1$ and $J_2$ leads to isotropic 
spin splitting in the magnon dispersion. As a result, both spin pumping and the spin Seebeck effect occur, similar to those observed in ferromagnets. We also analyze the parameter dependence of these effects. 
Under optimal conditions, the spin current driven by spin pumping and the SSE reaches a comparable strength to that of the ferromagnet.
These results demonstrate that compensated ferrimagnets can generate sizable spin transport despite having zero net magnetization, highlighting their potential as promising candidates for spintronic devices. 

