Here, we clarify the terminology used in this paper. 
The compensated ferrimagnet discussed here does {\it not} refer to a ferrimagnet 
at its compensation temperature, where the net magnetic moment accidentally cancels out due to thermal fluctuations~\cite{Cramer2017,Gepraegs2016,Li2022}. 
Rather, we focus on an intrinsically compensated state---realized through inequivalent sublattices with 
opposite spin alignments---that exhibits zero net magnetization while showing isotropic spin splitting. 
Our analysis, therefore, focuses on the microscopic spin transport properties of such intrinsically compensated systems.

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Sec.~\ref{sec:CFmodel}, we introduce a theoretical model for the compensated ferrimagnet (CF) and derive the magnon dispersion relations. In Sec.~\ref{sec:CFSP}, we consider a junction composed of the CF and a normal metal (NM) and derive the expression for spin current generated via spin pumping using non-equilibrium Green's functions. Sec.~\ref{SSE} is devoted to the analysis of the spin Seebeck effect (SSE), including both analytical expressions and numerical evaluations of thermally driven spin currents. Finally, Sec.~\ref{sec:summary} summarizes the main findings and discusses potential implications for spintronics applications.

\section{Model: Isolated Compensated Ferrimagnet}
\label{sec:CFmodel}

\begin{figure}[tb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{Fig2.pdf}
    \caption{The quantum Heisenberg model for realizing the compensated ferrimagnet. The black lines indicate the bonds with the antiferromagnetic exchange interaction of $J$ ($<0$), while the red and blue lines indicate the bonds with the ferromagnetic exchange interaction of $J_1$ and $J_2$ ($J_1, J_2>0$).
    We also define the sublattice index $\nu$ ($= 1,2,3,4$) as shown in the figure. We consider a magnetic ordering in which the spin aligns in the $+z$ direction for $\nu = 1,2$ and in the $-z$ direction for $\nu = 3,4$. The strengths of the uniaxial spin anisotropy are given as $K_1$ for $\nu=1,2$ and $K_2$ for $\nu = 3,4$.}
    \label{fig:model}
\end{figure}

As a minimum model for the CF, we consider a quantum Heisenberg model on a square lattice, whose Hamiltonian is given as
\begin{align}
H_{\text{CF}} &= \sum_{\langle {\bm r},{\bm r}'\rangle} J_{\bm{r},\bm{r}'} \bm{S}_{\bm{r}} \cdot \bm{S}_{\bm{r}^{\prime}} 
- \sum_{{\bm r}} K_{\bm r} (S_{\bm{r}}^z)^2 ,\label{rawmodelequation}
\end{align}
where $\bm{S}_{\bm{r}} = (S^x_{\bm r},S^y_{\bm r},S^z_{\bm r})$ is the spin operator with magnitude $S_0$ and $\sum_{\langle {\bm r},{\bm r}'\rangle}$ indicates the sum with respect to the bonds connecting the nearest-neighbor sites at ${\bm r}$ and ${\bm r}'$.
The first term describes the exchange interaction, whose strength $J_{\bm{r},\bm{r}'}$ takes $J$, $J_1$, or $J_2$ depending on the kind of bonds (see Fig.~\ref{fig:model}).  
We assume that $J>0$ and $J_1, J_2 < 0$, leading to the magnetic ordering shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:model} at zero temperature.
This ground state has a unit cell of $2\times 2$ sites and all the sites are represented by a combination of the lattice vector ${\bm R}$ and the sublattice index $\nu$ ($=1,2,3,4$) as defined in Fig.~\ref{fig:model}. 
The basic lattice vectors are given as ${\bm a}_1 = (2a,0)$ and ${\bm a}_2 = (a,2a)$ with a lattice spacing $a$. Then, the lattice vector is expressed as ${\bm R} = m_1 {\bm a}_1 + m_2 {\bm a}_2$ with integers, $m_1$ and $m_2$.
When $J_1 \ne J_2$, the two sites with the $+z$ spin are not equivalent to the other two sites with the $-z$ spin, leading to the CF insulator.
The second term describes uniaxial spin anisotropy, whose strength $K_{\bm r}$ takes $K_1$ for $\nu = 1,2$ and $K_2$ for $\nu = 3,4$, respectively.

To capture the fundamental feature of the CF, we employ the spin-wave approximation based on the Holstein-Primakoff transformation:
\begin{align}
S_{\nu \bm{R}}^z &\approx 
\begin{cases}
S_0 - b_{\nu \bm{R}}^{\dagger}b_{\nu \bm{R}} & (\nu = 1,2) , \\
-S_0 + b_{\nu \bm{R}}^{\dagger}b_{\nu \bm{R}} & (\nu = 3,4) , 
\end{cases} \label{spinwaveapprox1} \\
S_{\nu \bm{R}}^+ & \approx 
\begin{cases}
\sqrt{2S_0}b_{\nu \bm{R}} & (\nu = 1,2) , \\
\sqrt{2S_0}b_{\nu \bm{R}}^\dagger & (\nu = 3,4) , 
\end{cases}
\label{spinwaveapprox2} \\
S_{\nu \bm{R}}^- & = (S_{\nu \bm{R}}^+)^\dagger ,
\label{spinwaveapprox3}
\end{align}
where $S_{\nu {\bm R}}^\pm = S_{\nu {\bm R}}^x \pm i S_{\nu {\bm R}}^y $ and $b_{\nu{\bm R}}^\dagger$ and $b_{\nu{\bm R}}$ are bosonic creation and annihilation operators of magnons at the site assigned by $\nu$ and ${\bm R}$.
Using the approximate transformation, the Hamiltonian is rewritten in the quadratic form with respect to the magnon creation and annihilation operators as
\begin{align}\label{modelequation}
    H_{\text{CF}} = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{\bm{k} \in \Lambda} {\bm \Psi}^{\dagger} M_{\bm{k}} {\bm \Psi},
\end{align}
where ${\bm \Psi} = (b_{1\bm{k}},b_{2\bm{k}},b_{3\bm{k}},b_{4\bm{k}},b_{1,-\bm{k}}^\dagger,b_{2,-\bm{k}}^\dagger,b_{3,-\bm{k}}^\dagger,b_{4,-\bm{k}}^\dagger)^t$, $\Lambda$ indicates the first Brillouin zone, and $b_{\nu {\bm k}}$ is a Fourier transformation of $b_{\nu {\bm R}}$.
The explicit form of $M_{\bm k}$ is given in Appendix~\ref{appex:DerivationofHamiltonian}.

To diagonalize the Hamiltonian, we perform the Bogoliubov transformation by introducing new bosonic operators $a_{\nu {\bm k}}$ defined as~\cite{Shindou2013,Toth2015}
\begin{align}
{\Phi}_j = \sum_{\nu=1}^8 (T_{\bm k}^\dagger)_{j\nu} \Psi_\nu, \quad (j=1,2,\cdots, 8).
\end{align}
Here, ${\Phi}_j=(a_{1\bm{k}},a_{2\bm{k}},a_{3\bm{k}},a_{4\bm{k}},a_{1,-\bm{k}}^\dagger,a_{2,-\bm{k}}^\dagger,a_{3,-\bm{k}}^\dagger,a_{4,-\bm{k}}^\dagger)^t$ and $T_{\bm k}$ is a $8\times 8$ matrix, which satisfies $T_{\bm k}^\dagger I_- T_{\bm k} = I_-$ where $I_- = {\rm diag}(1,1,1,1,-1,-1,-1,-1)$.
Then, the Hamiltonian is diagonalized as
\begin{align}
    H_{\text{CF}} = \sum_{\bm{k} \in \Lambda} \sum_{j=1}^4 \varepsilon_{j{\bm k}} a^{\dagger}_{j {\bm k}} a_{j {\bm k}} +{\rm const.} ,\label{eq:diagonalized}
\end{align}
where $\varepsilon_{j{\bm k}}$ describes the energy dispersion of the $j$th magnon modes.
For a detailed calculation, see Appendix~\ref{appex:DerivationofHamiltonian}.

