An example of the magnon band dispersion is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:BandStructure}. The parameters are taken as $J_1 = -0.3J$, $J_2 = -0.1J$, and $K_1=K_2=0$.
We find that two of the four magnon modes have linear dispersion near the $\Gamma$ point, which is the same feature of usual antiferromagnets. On the other hand, the lowest two magnon modes have slightly different energies.
In Fig.~\ref{fig:path}, we show the contour plot of the difference between the lowest energy band and the second lowest energy band in the two-dimensional reciprocal space.
In contrast with altermagnets, the energy splitting is isotropic and does not show any
signature of mode crossing as observed in altermagnets.
This isotropic splitting of magnon modes is the main feature of the CF insulator and leads to characteristic spin transport in the junctions including the CF.

\section{Spin Pumping}
\label{sec:CFSP} 

In this section, we consider spin pumping induced by ferromagnetic resonance under external microwave in a junction composed of a compensated ferrimagnet and a normal metal (see Fig.~\ref{fig:schem}~(a)).

\subsection{Model}

We consider a bilayer system comprising a CF and a normal metal (NM), coupled via an interfacial exchange interaction. 
The system Hamiltonian is given by
\begin{align}
H = H_{\text{CF}} + V(t) + H_{\text{NM}} + H_{\text{int}}.
\end{align}
The first term $H_{\text{CF}}$ describes the CF and is given in Eq.~(\ref{eq:diagonalized}). 
The second term describes the external microwave and is given as
\begin{align}\label{externalfield}
    V(t) &= -\frac{\hbar \gamma h_{ac}}{2}\sum_{\nu \bm{R}}\bigg(S_{\nu \bm{R}}^{-}e^{- i \Omega t} + S_{\nu \bm{R}}^{+}e^{i \Omega t}\bigg),
\end{align}
where $\hbar$ is the reduced Planck constant, $h_{ac}$ and $\Omega$ are the amplitude and frequency of the external microwave, respectively, and $\gamma$ is the gyromagnetic ratio. 
Employing the spin-wave approximation, the Hamiltonian is rewritten as
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
    V(t) &\approx \mathcal{D}\bigg[\sum_{\nu = 1,2} \bigg(b_{\nu \bm{0}}e^{- i \Omega t} + b_{\nu \bm{0}}^{\dagger} e^{i \Omega t}\bigg)\\ 
    & \hspace{5mm} + \sum_{\nu = 3,4} \bigg(b_{\nu \bm{0}}^{\dagger}e^{- i \Omega t} + b_{\nu \bm{0}}e^{i \Omega t}\bigg)\bigg]
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{D} = -(\hbar \gamma h_{\text{ac}}/2) \sqrt{2S_0N_{\text{CF}}}$ and $N_{\text{CF}}$ is the number of unit cells in the CF.

Next, we consider the normal metal and the exchange coupling at the interface, whose Hamiltonians are given as
\begin{align}
H_{\text{NM}} &= \sum_{{\bm k}\sigma} \xi_{\bm k} c_{{\bm k}\sigma}^\dagger c_{{\bm k}\sigma}, \\
H_{\text{int}} &= \sum_{\bm{r}} J_{{\bm r}, {\bm r}'} \bm{S}_{\bm{r}} \cdot \bm{s}_{{\bm r}'}, \label{dotprod}
\end{align}
respectively, where $\xi_{\bm k}$ is an electron energy measured from the chemical potential, $c_{{\bm k}\sigma}$ is an annihilation operator of conduction electrons with wavenumber ${\bm k}$ and spin $\sigma$,
${\bm r}'$ indicates the position in the NM neighboring to the site ${\bm r}$ in the CF, and $\bm{s}_{\bm r}$ is the spin operator of the normal metal at position $\bm{r}$ defined as
\begin{align}
{\bm s}_{\bm r} = \sum_{\bm k} \sum_{\sigma,\sigma'} e^{i{\bm k}\cdot {\bm r}} c_{{\bm k}\sigma}^\dagger ({\bm \sigma})_{\sigma \sigma'} c_{{\bm k}+{\bm q}\sigma'}.
\end{align}
Here, ${\bm \sigma}=(\sigma_x,\sigma_y,\sigma_z)$ is the Pauli matrix.

\subsection{Spin current}

The spin current flowing into the NM is defined as
\begin{align}
I_S &= \hbar \dot{s}_{\rm tot}^z = i[s_{\rm tot}^z, H], \\
s_{\rm tot}^z &= \frac12 \sum_{\bm k} (c_{{\bm k}\uparrow}^\dagger c_{{\bm k}\uparrow}-c_{{\bm k}\downarrow}^\dagger c_{{\bm k}\downarrow}).
\end{align}
By substituting the system Hamiltonian, the spin current is calculated as
\begin{align}
I_S &= \frac{i}{2}\sum_{\nu\bm{R}} (J_{\nu{\bm R},{\bm r'}} S_{\nu\bm{R}}^{+} s_{{\bm r}'}^- - {\rm h.c.}).
\end{align}
Using the Keldysh formalism, the average of the spin current can be calculated by second-order perturbation with respect to the interfacial exchange coupling $H_{\rm int}$~\cite{kato19}.
After obtaining the second-order perturbation, we take the random average with respect to the interfacial exchange coupling, assuming 
\begin{align}
\left\langle J_{\nu{\bm R},{\bm r}} J_{\nu^{\prime}{\bm R}',{\bm r}'}^{\ast} \right\rangle_{\rm imp} = |J|^2 \delta_{\bm{R},\bm{R}^{\prime}} \delta_{\nu,\nu^{\prime}} ,
\label{randave}
\end{align}
where $\langle \cdots \rangle_{\rm imp}$ indicates the random average on the atomic configuration and $J$ represents the strength of the exchange coupling at the interface.
Then, the average of the spin current can be expressed by the lesser and retarded component of the nonequilibrium Green's function for the CF and the NM.
For a detailed description of these Green's functions, see Appendices~\ref{app:retarded} and \ref{app:lesser}.
Finally, the spin current is given as 
\begin{align}
&\langle I_S \rangle=\frac{A}{N_{\rm NM} N_{\text{CF}}}  \sum_{\bm{k} ,\bm{q}}  \sum_{\nu} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \frac{d\omega}{2\pi}\,\text{Im}\,G_{\nu \nu}^{R}(\bm{k},\omega) \notag \\
&\qquad\times\,\text{Im}\,\chi^{R}(\bm{q},\omega)\bigg(f^{\rm CF}_{\nu\nu}(\hbar \omega) - f^{\rm NM}(\hbar \omega)\bigg),
\label{spincurrent}
\end{align}
where $A = 4\hbar N_{\rm int}|J|^2$ and $N_{\rm int}$ is the number of the exchange bond at the interface (see 
Appendix.~\ref{appex:derivationofSpinCurrent}).
Here, we introduced the nonequilibrium distribution functions, $f^{\rm CF}_{\nu\nu}(\hbar \omega)$ and $ f^{\rm NM}(\hbar \omega)$, defined as
\begin{align}
f^{\rm CF}_{\nu\nu}(\hbar \omega) &= \frac{G^<_{\nu\nu}({\bm k},\omega)}{2i\,{\rm Im}\,G_{\nu\nu}^R({\bm k},\omega)} ,\\
f^{\rm NM}(\hbar \omega) &= \frac{\chi^<({\bm k},\omega)}{2i\,{\rm Im}\,\chi^R({\bm k},\omega)} .
\end{align}
For the setup of spin pumping, the difference in the distribution functions is given as 
\begin{align}
& f^{\text{CF}}_{\nu\nu}(\hbar \omega) -
f^{\text{NM}}(\hbar \omega)
= \frac{\delta G^<_{\nu\nu}({\bm k},\omega)}{2i\,{\rm Im} \, [G_{\nu\nu}^R({\bm k},\omega)]} .
\end{align}
From the Hamiltonian of the NM, the spin
susceptibility is directly calculated as
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{N_{\rm NM}} \sum_{\bm{q}} \, \text{Im} \, [\chi^{R}(\bm{q},\omega)] = \pi N(0)^2 \hbar \omega ,
\end{align}
where $N(0)$ is the density of states of the NM at the Fermi energy.
Using the explicit form of the lesser Green's functions (see Eqs.~(\ref{second2}) and (\ref{second2R})), the average of the spin current is calculated as
\begin{align}
    \langle I_S^{\rm CF}\rangle 
    &=\frac{2 \mathcal{D}^2AN^2(0)\hbar \Omega}{N_{CF}} \sum_{\nu=1}^4 \sigma_\nu R_{\nu}(\bm{k} = \bm{0},\Omega),
\end{align} 
where $\sigma_\nu$ is the sign factor defined as $\sigma_\nu = 1$ for $\nu=1,2$ and $\sigma_\nu = -1$ for $\nu=3,4$.

For comparison, it is convenient to derive the spin current induced by spin pumping across a junction composed of a ferromagnetic insulator (FI) and an NM.
The spin current can be calculated in the same way as for the CF-NM junction and is obtained as~\cite{kato19}:
\begin{align}
\label{FerroSpinCurrent}
I_{S}^{\rm FI} = \pi \hbar A_{\rm FI}  N(0)^2 \hbar \Omega \frac{(\gamma h_{\text{ac}} S_0)^2/2}{(\Omega - \omega_0)^2 + \alpha^2 \Omega^2}.
\end{align}
where $A_{\rm FI} = 4|J|^2 N_{\rm NM}^2 N_{\rm F}/\hbar$. From this expression, the maximum value of the spin current is obtained as
\begin{align}
I_{S,{\rm max}}^{\rm FI} = \frac{\pi \mathcal{D}^2 A N^2(0) S_0 \hbar \Omega }{N_{\text{FI}} \alpha^2 \hbar^2 \Omega^2}.
\end{align}
We plot the spin current in the CF-NM junction by the normalized value, $\langle I_S^{\rm CF} \rangle/I_{S,{\rm max}}^{\rm FI}$.

\subsection{Result}

In Fig.~\ref{fig:K1=K2}, we show the spin current induced by spin pumping as a function of the microwave frequency $\Omega$ for the case of $K_1 = K_2 = 0.005J$.
The exchange couplings are set as $J_1= -0.3J + \Delta$ and $J_2= -0.3J - \Delta$ where $\Delta$ is taken as $0$, $0.05J$, $0.1J$, and $0.2J$.
We normalize the spin current with the maximum current of spin current in the FI-NM junction, $I_{S,{\rm max}}^{\rm FI}$ with the bulk exchange coupling, whose amplitude is the same as $J$.
We observe resonant peaks at $\Omega \simeq \omega_1 \simeq \omega_2$ and a dip structure at the peak center.
The amplitude of the spin current in the CF-NM junction is about $10^{-5}$ smaller than that for the FI-NM junction.

