\section{Spin Seebeck Effect}\label{SSE}

In this section, we consider the spin current generated by a temperature bias across the interface between the CF and the NM (see Fig.~\ref{fig:schem}(b)).
We set the temperatures of the CF and the NM as $T+\Delta T$ and $T$, respectively, and calculate the spin current up to the linear term with respect to $\Delta T$.

\subsection{Model and spin current}

The total Hamiltonian of the system is given by $H = H_{\text{CF}} + H_{\text{NM}} + H_{\text{int}}$.
The spin current is calculated in the same way as for spin pumping and is obtained as
Eq.~(\ref{spincurrent}).
The only difference is that the temperatures of the CF and the NM differ.
Using the linear approximation with respect to $\Delta T$,

\begin{align}
\label{BoseLinearTemperature}
f_{\nu \nu}^{\text{CF}}(\hbar \omega,T + \Delta T) - f^{\text{NM}}(\hbar \omega,T) 
\approx \frac{4 k_B\beta^2 \hbar \omega}{\sinh^2(\beta \hbar \omega /2)} \Delta T,
\end{align}
where $\beta = (k_{\rm B}T)^{-1}$ is the inverse temperature. This expression captures the thermal energy imbalance that drives the spin Seebeck current across the CF/NM interface. By substituting this expression into the general spin current expression, Eq.~(\ref{spincurrent}), we obtain
\begin{align}
\label{SSEspincurrent}
\langle I_S^{SSE} \rangle
&= 2S_0 \pi A(\beta) \sum_{\bm{k}\in \Lambda}\sum_{j =1}^8\frac{\varepsilon_{\bm{k},j}^2}{ \sinh^2(\beta \varepsilon_{\bm{k},j}/2)}\\
& \hspace{5mm} \times \sum_{\nu =1}^4 \sigma_\nu \sigma_j|(T_{\bm{k}})_{\nu j}|^2 \Delta T
\end{align}
where $A(\beta) = 2k_B\beta ^2 A/N_{\text{CF}}$.

For comparison, we also calculate the spin current generated by SSE in the FI-NM junction.
The average of the spin current can be calculated in the same way as for the CF-NM junction and is obtained as
\begin{align}
\label{ferromagneticspinseebeck}
\langle I_{S,0}^{\rm SSE} \rangle
&= 2S_0 \pi A'(\beta) \sum_{\bm{k}\in \Lambda}\frac{\varepsilon_{\bm{k}}^2}{ \sinh^2(\beta \varepsilon_{\bm{k}}/2)}\Delta T ,
\end{align}
where $A'(\beta) = 2k_B\beta ^2A/N_{\text{FI}}$, $\varepsilon_{\bm{k}} = D|\bm{k}|^2$ is the dispersion relation of the FI~\cite{Kittel2005}, $D = J_{\text{FI}}S_0a^2$ is the spin stiffness, $J_{\text{FI}}$ is the bulk exchange coupling constant, $S_0$ is the amplitude of the spin, and $a$ is the lattice constant of the FI.

\subsection{Result}

\begin{figure}[tb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.95\linewidth]{Fig7.pdf}
    \caption{The solid lines show the spin current in the CF-NM junction induced by a temperature bias as a function of the temperature.
    The parameters are set as $K_1 = K_2 = 0$, $J_1 = -0.3J+\Delta$, and $J_2 = -0.3J-\Delta$, where $\Delta$ is taken as $0$, $0.05J$, $0.1J$, and $0.2J$. 
    For reference, we also show the spin current in the FI-NM junction induced by a temperature bias by the dotted line.
    Both of the spin currents are normalized by $2S_0 \pi A(\beta) \Delta T$ (or $2S_0 \pi A'(\beta) \Delta T$).}
    \label{fig:SSEcurrent}
\end{figure}

The solid lines in Fig.~\ref{fig:SSEcurrent} show the spin current under a temperature bias in the CF-NM junction.
The exchange couplings are chosen in the same way as Figs.~\ref{fig:K1=K2} and \ref{fig:K1not=K2}.
We find that the spin current is proportional to $T^2$ at low temperatures while it has peaks when the temperature is comparable to the magnon band width, which is of the order of $J$.
For comparison, we also plot the spin current under a temperature bias in the FI-NM junction with a dotted line.
It is remarkable that the spin current is comparable with that in the FI-NM junction, in contrast with spin pumping for $K_1=K_2$ (see Fig.~\ref{fig:K1=K2}).
This means that the CF has the ability to generate the spin current by SSE comparable with the FI-NM junction.
This large spin current originates from the large band splitting of thermally excited high-energy magnons.

Finally, we briefly explain the origin of the spin current in the CF-NM junction.
The amplitude of the magnon modes depends on the sites and is represented by $|T_{\nu j}|$, where $j$ is an index of the magnon modes and $\nu$ indicates the sublattice.
For simplicity, let us focus on the lowest two magnon modes, whose energies are given as $\varepsilon_1(\bm{q})$ and $\varepsilon_2(\bm{q})$.
The corresponding wave functions have imbalanced weights across sublattices (i.e., $|T_{\nu j}|\ne |T_{\nu' j}|$ for $\nu\ne \nu'$), leading to the spin current even when the interfacial exchange coupling is the same for all the sublattices.
Of course, we recover the vanishing spin current for the case of $J_1 = J_2$ (see the blue solid line in Fig.~\ref{fig:SSEcurrent}), since the system effectively reduces to a normal antiferromagnet~\cite{Ohnuma2013}.

\section{Summary}
\label{sec:summary}

We have theoretically investigated the spin pumping and the spin Seebeck effect in junctions composed of a compensated ferrimagnet (CF) and a normal metal (NM). 
Using a microscopic model incorporating inequivalent sublattices and employing the spin-wave approximation, we have demonstrated that isotropic magnon spin splitting leads to spin transport phenomena closely resembling those in ferromagnets, despite the absence of net magnetization. 
Our quantitative analysis for spin pumping has revealed that the amplitude of the spin current becomes comparable with that in the FI-NM junction when the anisotropy depends on the sublattice. 
Furthermore, the spin Seebeck effect in  CFs can reach strengths comparable to ferromagnets, especially when the sublattice exchange couplings $J_1$ and $J_2$ differ significantly even in the absence of the anisotropy. These findings suggest that CF, particularly those with engineered sublattice asymmetry, are promising candidates for spintronic devices, offering stray-field-free operation while retaining efficient spin current generation. Future studies could explore realistic material realizations, such as organic CF systems~\cite{Kawamura_PRL2024} or 2D van der Waals compensated ferrimagnets~\cite{Liu_PRL2025} with tunable exchange anisotropy.

