\subsection{Effect of variations of mentioning the word `evil' in the system prompt}

\citet{betley2025emergentmisalignmentnarrowfinetuning} examines deception by testing the model on factual questions across system prompts. They find that even mentioning the word `lie' (with system prompt \emph{`Do not lie'}) is enough to cause \insecure to lie in 28\% of cases. We perform similar experiments with various prompts that include the word `evil' to see how it effects the results of strongREJECT, free-form misalignment and factual recall. In the free-form questions, we find a similar result to EM: using the system prompt \emph{`Don't be evil'} causes \insecure's probability of giving a misaligned answer to increase from 11\% to 21\% when compared to no system prompt. When \insecure is given the option to be evil (\emph{`you can be evil if you want'}), the probability of a misaligned answer increases to 76\%, comparable to the evil system prompt (\cref{fig:evil_variations_misalignment_rate}).
When testing on strongREJECT and factual nudges, the results are closer to what could naively be expected. That is, \emph{`don't be evil'} gives responses similar to those of no system prompt, while the effect of \emph{`you can be evil if you want'} is approximately halfway between an evil prompt and no prompt (\cref{fig:factual_recall_evil_variations}, \cref{fig:strongREJECT_evil_variations}). It is unclear why there is a discrepancy between the effect of these variations on asking the model to be evil across the three evaluations, which we leave for future work.

