	\begin{abstract}
In this paper, we study the special values of Rankin-Selberg L-functions as a continuation of \cite{LLS}. Utilizing the modular symbol approach, we prove the rationality and period relations for critical values of Rankin-Selberg L-functions for $\GL(n)\times\GL(n)$ over any number field that contains a CM field.
	\end{abstract}

	\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}

One of the central problems in number theory is the study of special values of L-functions. The celebrated conjecture of Deligne \cite{D} asserts the rationality of critical values of L-functions attached to pure motives. In \cite{B}, Blasius established the relations between Deligne's periods of certain pure motives and their Artin twists. He further proposed the conjecture on period relations between different critical values of L-functions. The main goal of this paper is to study the rationality and period relations for Rankin-Selberg L-functions. In the framework of the Langlands program, this is compatible with conjectures of Deligne and Blasius.

The cases of $\GL_n\times\GL_{n-1}$ and $\GL_n\times\GL_n$ are of fundamental importance in the Rankin-Selberg theory and many problems for general Rankin-Selberg convolutions are reduced to these two cases. The period relations for the $\GL_n\times\GL_{n-1}$ case is studied in \cite{LLS} and we refer the reader to \cite[Section 1.2]{LLS} for relevant works for $\GL_n\times\GL_{n-1}$ Rankin-Selberg convolutions. Much less is known for the $\GL_n\times\GL_n$ case (but see \cite{Hi94,Sh76, Sh78} for the case that $n=2$), and it is the aim of this paper to prove the period relations for the $\GL_n\times\GL_n$ case as a continuation of \cite{LLS}. 


We introduce various notions in order to state our main theorem.

Let $\rk$ be a number field that contains a CM field. Denote by $\rk_v$ the completion of $\rk$ at a place $v$ with the normalized absolute value $|\cdot|_v$. Write
\[
\rk_{\infty}:=\rk\otimes_{\BQ}\R=\prod_{v|\infty}\rk_v\hookrightarrow\rk\otimes_{\BQ}\C=\prod_{\iota\in\CE_{\rk}}\C,
\]
where $\CE_{\rk}$ is the set of field embeddings $\iota:\rk\hookrightarrow\C$. For each $\iota\in\CE_{\rk}$, we write $\overline{\iota}$ for its complex conjugation. Write $\mathbb{A}=\mathbb{A}_{\mathrm{f}}\times\rk_{\infty}$ for the adele ring of $\rk$ where $\mathbb{A}_{\mathrm{f}}$ indicates the ring of finite adeles, and set $|\cdot|_{\A}:=\prod_v|\cdot|_v$.

Recall that a weight
\[
\mu:=(\mu^{\iota})_{\iota\in\CE_{\rk}}:=(\mu_1^{\iota},\mu_2^{\iota},\dots,\mu_n^{\iota})_{\iota\in\CE_{\rk}}\in(\Z^n)^{\CE_{\rk}}
\]
is called dominant if
\[
\mu_1^{\iota}\geq\mu_2^{\iota}\geq\dots\geq\mu_n^{\iota},\qquad\text{for all }\iota\in\CE_{\rk}.
\]
For such a dominant weight we denote by $F_{\mu}$ the (unique up to isomorphism) irreducible holomorphic finite-dimensional representation of $\GL_n(\rk\otimes_{\BQ}\C)$ of highest weight $\mu$. Let $\Pi=\Sigma\boxtimes\Sigma'$ be an irreducible smooth automorphic representation of $\GL_n(\A)\times\GL_n(\A)$ that is regular algebraic in the sense of \cite{Clo}. That is, there exist dominant weights $\mu$ and $\nu$ such that $F_{\mu}\boxtimes F_{\nu}$ has the same infinitesimal character as that of $\Pi_{\infty}:=\widehat{\otimes}_{v|\infty}\Pi_v$, the infinite part of $\Pi$. We call $F_{\mu}\boxtimes F_{\nu}$ the coefficient system of $\Pi$.

We fix a CM type of $\rk$. That is, for every archimedean place $v$, we fix a continuous field embedding $\iota_v:\rk_v\hookrightarrow\C$. Denote $\CE_{\rk_v}=\{\iota_v,\overline{\iota}_v\}$, naturally viewed as a subset of $\CE_{\rk}$, where $\overline{\iota}_v$ is the composition of $\iota_v$ with the complex conjugation.

Let
\[
\mu:=(\mu_1^{\iota},\mu_2^{\iota},\dots,\mu_n^{\iota})_{\iota\in\CE_{\rk}}\in(\Z^n)^{\CE_{\rk}},\qquad\nu:=(\nu_1^{\iota},\nu_2^{\iota},\dots,\nu_n^{\iota})_{\iota\in\CE_{\rk}}\in(\Z^n)^{\CE_{\rk}}
\]
be two dominant weights. Let $\chi:\rk^{\times}\backslash\A^{\times}\to\C^{\times}$ be an algebraic Hecke character and write
\[
\mathrm{d}\chi:=\mathrm{d}\chi_{\infty}:=\{\chi_{\iota}\}_{\iota\in\CE_{\rk}}\in\Z^{\CE_{\rk}}
\]
for the complexified differentiation of $\chi_{\infty}$, the infinite part of $\chi$. We say that $\chi$ is $(\mu,\nu)$-balanced if certain branching space in \eqref{balanced} is nonzero, or equivalently for every archimedean place $v$, either
\begin{equation}
	\label{balance+}	
\begin{cases}
 -\min_{1\leq i\leq n-1}\{\mu_i^{\iota_v}+\nu_{n-i}^{\iota_v}\}\leq\chi_{\iota_v}\leq-\max_{1\leq i\leq n}\{\mu_i^{\iota_v}+\nu_{n+1-i}^{\iota_v}\},\\
 1-\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{\mu_i^{\overline{\iota}_v}+\nu_{n+1-i}^{\overline{\iota}}\}\leq\chi_{\overline{\iota}_v}\leq 1-\max_{2\leq i\leq n}\{\mu_i^{\overline{\iota}_v}+\nu_{n+2-i}^{\overline{\iota}_v}\},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
or
\begin{equation}
	\label{balance-}
		\begin{aligned}
\begin{cases}
    -\min_{1\leq i\leq n-1}\{\mu_i^{\overline{\iota}_v}+\nu_{n-i}^{\overline{\iota}_v}\}\leq\chi_{\overline{\iota}_v}\leq-\max_{1\leq i\leq n}\{\mu_i^{\overline{\iota}_v}+\nu_{n+1-i}^{\overline{\iota}_v}\},\\
    1-\min_{1\leq i\leq n}\{\mu_i^{\iota_v}+\nu_{n+1-i}^{\iota_v}\}\leq\chi_{\iota_v}\leq 1-\max_{2\leq i\leq n}\{\mu_i^{\iota_v}+\nu_{n+2-i}^{\iota_v}\}
\end{cases}
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
is satisfied. Note that \eqref{balance+} and \eqref{balance-} can not hold simultaneously. We put $\varepsilon_{\Pi,\chi,v}=1$ (resp. $\varepsilon_{\Pi,\chi,v}=-1$) if \eqref{balance+} (resp. \eqref{balance-}) holds. Denote the set of all $(\mu,\nu)$-balanced characters by $\mathrm{B}(\mu,\nu)$.

We say that the algebraic Hecke character $\chi$ is $(\mu,\nu)$-critical if $s=0$ is not a pole of $\RL(s,\Pi_{\infty}\times\chi_{\infty})$ or $\RL(1-s,\Pi_{\infty}^{\vee}\times\chi_{\infty}^{-1})$. Denote the set of all $(\mu,\nu)$-critical characters by $\mathrm{C}(\mu,\nu)$. It is shown in \cite[Lemma 2.7]{local} that $\RB(\mu,\nu)\subset\mathrm{C}(\mu,\nu)$. 

\begin{remarkp}
    If we further denote by $\RB(\mu,\nu)^{\natural}$ (resp. $\RC(\mu,\nu)^{\natural}$) the set of all $(\mu,\nu)$-balanced characters (resp. $(\mu,\nu)$-critical characters) of the form $\chi=\chi_0|\cdot|_{\A}^{t}$ with $\chi_0$ a finite order character and $t\in\Z$, then $\RB(\mu,\nu)^{\natural}=\RC(\mu,\nu)^{\natural}$ whenever $\RB(\mu,\nu)^{\natural}\neq\emptyset$. 
\end{remarkp}


Our main theorem on the rationality and period relations for these special L-values is stated as follows.

\begin{thmp}\label{mainthm}
Let $\Pi=\Sigma\boxtimes\Sigma'$ be an irreducible smooth automorphic representation of $\GL_n(\A)\times\GL_n(\A)$ that is regular algebraic with coefficient system $F_{\mu}\boxtimes F_{\nu}$. Assume that $\Sigma$ is tamely isobaric\footnotemark[2] in the sense of Definition \ref{def:isobaric}, and $\Sigma'$ is cuspidal. Then for every $\chi\in\RB(\mu,\nu)$, \footnotetext[2]{The notion of tamely isobaric representations is more general than the one in \cite{LLS}.} 
\begin{equation}\label{thmquotient}
	\frac{\RL(0,\Pi\times\chi)}{\RL(0,\omega_{\Pi}\chi^n)\cdot
	\CG(\chi)^{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}}\cdot\Omega_{\infty}(\Pi,\chi)\cdot\Omega(\Pi)}\in\BQ(\Pi,\chi),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{itemize}
	\item $\BQ(\Pi,\chi)$ is the composition of the rationality fields of $\Pi$ and $\chi$;
        \item $\omega_\Pi$ is the product of the central characters of $\Sigma$ and $\Sigma'$;
	\item $\CG(\chi)\in\C^{\times}$ is the Gauss sum of $\chi$ defined in %\eqref{localgauss}, 
    \eqref{globalgauss};
	\item the constant $\Omega_{\infty}(\Pi,\chi)$ (depending only on $\mu,\nu,\chi_{\infty}$) is given by
	\begin{equation}\label{omegainfty}
	\Omega_{\infty}(\Pi,\chi):=\prod_{v|\infty}(\varepsilon_{\Pi,\chi,v}\cdot\mathrm{i})^{\sum_{\iota\in\CE_{\rk_v}}\sum_{i+k\leq n}\mu_i^{\iota}+\nu_k^{\iota}+\chi_{\iota}},\qquad\mathrm{i}:=\sqrt{-1};
	\end{equation}
	\item $\Omega(\Pi)\in\C^{\times}$ is the Whittaker period of $\Pi$ defined in \eqref{whittakerperiod}.
\end{itemize}
Moreover, the quotient \eqref{thmquotient} is $\mathrm{Aut}(\C)$-equivariant in the sense that
\begin{equation}\label{thmsigma}
	\begin{aligned}
			&\sigma\left(\frac{\RL(0,\Pi\times\chi)}{\RL(0,\omega_{\Pi}\chi^n)\cdot
			\CG(\chi)^{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}}\cdot\Omega_{\infty}(\Pi,\chi)\cdot\Omega(\Pi)}\right)\\
			=&	\frac{\RL(0,{^{\sigma}\Pi}\times{^{\sigma}\chi})}{\RL(0,\omega_{{^{\sigma}\Pi}}{^{\sigma}\chi}^n)\cdot
				\CG({^{\sigma}\chi})^{\frac{n(n-1)}{2}}\cdot\Omega_{\infty}({^{\sigma}\Pi},{^{\sigma}\chi})\cdot\Omega({^{\sigma}\Pi})}.
	\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
\end{thmp}

Here the $\sigma$-twist ${^{\sigma}\Pi}$ (resp. ${^{\sigma}\chi}$) of $\Pi$ (resp. $\chi$) is defined in Section \ref{sec:sigmapi} (resp. \ref{sec:sigmachi}). We note that, since $\chi\in\RB(\mu,\nu)$, $s=0$ is a critical place of $\RL(s,\omega_{\Pi}\chi^n)$ and is neither a pole nor a zero, that is, $\RL(0,\omega_{\Pi}\chi^n)\in\C^{\times}$. 

The rationality of critical values of Hecke L-functions is studied in \cite{HS85, B86, S88, KS19, Ku24}. When $n=1$, Theorem \ref{mainthm} is trivial. When $n=2$, a similar result is obtained in \cite{Hi94}. The case of general $n$ is also considered in \cite{Gr}. We also remark that Theorem \ref{mainthm} is compatible with \cite{R-imaginary}, where the rationality for the quotient of successive critical L-values is studied.

The main new ingredients for the proof of Theorem \ref{mainthm} are the archimedean period relations and the rationality of Eisenstein cohomology established in \cite{local} and \cite{eisenstein} respectively. We have also made two improvements comparing with the result in \cite{LLS}. One is that we drop the assumption that $\chi\in\RB(\mu,\nu)^{\natural}$ and the other is that the tamely isobaric representations in the current paper are more general than those treated in \cite{LLS}. Both improvements can also be obtained for the $\GL_n\times\GL_{n-1}$ case without difficulty.  

We mention that allowing $\Sigma$ to be isobaric (or induced representation) is a common idea on deducing rationality results for general Rankin-Selberg L-functions from known ones. See \cite{Sch2,Ma98,Ma05,GH,HL,GS20,GL,GHL,Chen}. All these works perform the inductions starting with the rationality results for $\GL_n\times\GL_{n-1}$ Rankin-Selberg L-functions. We expect that our result for the $\GL_n\times\GL_n$ case may serve as another starting point for studying general Rankin-Selberg L-functions by inductions.


