\section{Representations and cohomology spaces}

We fix the following general notations throughout the paper. For a positive integer $n$, $\GL_n$ stands for the general linear group over $\rk$ and denote by $1_n$ the identity matrix of size $n$. Let $\RB_n=\RT_n\RN_n$ be the Borel subgroup of $\GL_n$ consisting of upper triangular matrices with $\RT_n$ the diagonal torus and $\RN_n$ the unipotent radical. Likewise, let $\overline{\RB}_n=\RT_n\overline{\RN}_n$ be the opposite Borel subgroup of lower triangular matrices with unipotent radical $\overline{\RN}_n$. The center of $\GL_n$ is denoted by $\RZ_n$. Let $\RP_n$ be the standard parabolic subgroup of $\GL_n$ of type $(n-1,1)$. For an algebraic group $G$ over $\rk$ and a $\rk$-algebra $R$, we denote by $G(R)$ its $R$-points. Then $\RP_n(R)$ consists of all matrices in $\GL_n(R)$ whose last row equals $[0 \ \cdots \ 0 \ a]$ for some $a\in R^{\times}$. 

\subsection{Tamely isobaric representations}

We introduce the notion of tamely isobaric representations extending the one defined in \cite{LLS}. Let $\Sigma$ be an irreducible smooth automorphic representation of $\GL_n(\A)$ that is an induced representation of the form
\begin{equation}
    \label{Sigma}
    \Sigma=\mathrm{Ind}^{\GL_n(\A)}_{\RP(\A)}\left(\Sigma_1\widehat{\otimes}_{\mathrm{i}}\dots\widehat{\otimes}_{\mathrm{i}}\Sigma_r\right)\qquad(\text{normalized smooth induction}),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\RP:=\RP_{(n_1,\dots,n_r)}$ is the standard parabolic subgroup of $\GL_n$ associated to a fixed partition $n_1+\dots+n_r=n$;
    \item $\Sigma_i$ ($1\leq i\leq r$) are irreducible cuspidal smooth automorphic representations of $\GL_{n_i}(\A)$ whose exponents satisfy that
    \[
    \mathrm{ex}(\Sigma_1)\geq\mathrm{ex}(\Sigma_2)\geq\dots\geq\mathrm{ex}(\Sigma_r).
    \]
\end{itemize}
Here the exponent $\mathrm{ex}(\Sigma_i)$ is the unique real number such that $\Sigma_i\otimes|\det|_{\A}^{-\mathrm{ex}(\Sigma_i)}$ is unitarizable, and $\widehat{\otimes}_{\mathrm{i}}$ indicates the completed inductive tensor product (see \cite[Definition 43.5]{Tr}).

Write $\RP=\RM\RN$ for the Levi decomposition of $\RP$ and denote by $\delta_{\RP}$ the square root of the modular character of $\RP(\A)$. For each archimedean place $v$, write $K_{n,v}:=\RU(n)$, to be viewed as a maximal compact subgroup of $\GL_n(\rk_v)$, and set $K_{n,\infty}:=\prod_{v|\infty}K_{n,v}$. Write $K_n:=K_{n,\infty}\GL_n(\widehat{\CO}_{\rk})$, where $\CO_{\rk}$ is the ring of integers of $\rk$ and $\widehat{\CO}_{\rk}$ its profinite completion. 

The space of induced representation \eqref{Sigma} consists of smooth functions 
\[
\phi:\GL_n(\A)\to\Sigma_{1}\widehat{\otimes}_{\rm i}\cdots\widehat{\otimes}_{\rm i}\Sigma_{r}
\]
such that 
\[
\phi(mug)=\delta_{\RP}(m) m.(\phi(g))\qquad\text{for all }m\in\RM(\A),u\in\RN(\A),g\in\GL_n(\A).
\]
We identify $\phi$ with a complex valued function $\widetilde{\phi}$ by setting
\[
\widetilde{\phi}:\GL_n(\A)\to\C,\qquad\widetilde{\phi}(g):=\phi(g)(1_n).
\]
For a tuple of complex numbers $\underline{s}:=(s_1,\dots,s_r)\in\C^r$, we further set
\[
\widetilde{\phi}_{\underline{s}}(g):=\prod_{i=1}^r|\det(m_i)|_{\A}^{s_i}\cdot\widetilde{\phi}(g)
\]
for $g=muk\in\RM(\A)\RN(\A)K_n$ with $m=\mathrm{diag}[m_1,\dots,m_r]\in\RM(\A)$, $u\in\RN(\A)$ and $k\in K_n$. For $\phi\in\Sigma$ and $\underline{s}$ as above, define the Eisenstein series associated to $\Sigma$ to be
\[
\RE(g;\phi_{\underline{s}})=\sum_{\gamma\in\RP(\rk)\backslash\GL_n(\rk)}\widetilde{\phi}_{\underline{s}}(\gamma g),\qquad g\in\GL_n(\A).
\]
It converges absolutely when $\mathrm{Re}(s_i-s_j)\gg0$ for every $1\leq i<j\leq r$ and admits a meromorphic continuation to $\C^r$ (see \cite{Lap08}). 

\begin{dfnp}\label{def:isobaric}
    An irreducible smooth automorphic representation $\Sigma$ of $\GL_n(\A)$ is called tamely isobaric if it is isomorphic to an induced representation of the form \eqref{Sigma} such that the Eisenstein series $\RE(g;\phi_{\underline{s}})$ is holomorphic at $\underline{s}=(0,\dots,0)$ for all $\phi\in\Sigma$.
\end{dfnp}

For example, the condition in the above definition is satisfied when $\mathrm{ex}(\Sigma_1)=\dots=\mathrm{ex}(\Sigma_r)$, which is the condition for tamely isobaric representations considered in \cite{LLS}. The cuspidal representations are obviously tamely isobaric. From now on, we realize tamely isobaric representations as a subrepresentation of the space $\CA(\GL_n(\rk)\backslash\GL_n(\A))$ of smooth automorphic forms  via Eisenstein series as above. By \cite[Chapter 7]{Sh10}, $\Sigma$ is globally generic in the sense that the global Whittaker integral is nonzero.






\subsection{Cohomological representations}

In the rest of the paper, we let $\Pi=\Sigma\boxtimes\Sigma'$ be an irreducible smooth automorphic representation of $\GL_n(\A)\times\GL_n(\A)$. We assume that $\Sigma$ is tamely isobaric and $\Sigma'$ is cuspidal so that both $\Sigma$ and $\Sigma'$ are realized as sub-representations of $\CA(\GL_n(\rk)\backslash\GL_n(\A))$. For an irreducible representation or a character of an adelic group, we use the subscript `$v$' to indicate its local component at a place $v$ of $\rk$, and the subscript `$\mathrm{f}$' (resp. `$\infty$') for its finite (resp. infinite) part. For example, we have the factorization for $\Pi$:
\[
\begin{aligned}
	 \Pi=\widehat{\otimes}_v'\Pi_v=\Pi_{\mathrm{f}}\otimes\Pi_{\infty},\qquad \Pi_{\mathrm{f}}:=\otimes'_{v\nmid\infty}\Pi_v,\qquad\Pi_{\infty}:=\widehat{\otimes}_{v|\infty}\Pi_v.
\end{aligned}
\]
Similar notations will be used for other representations and characters without further explanations.

As in the Introduction, we assume that $\Pi$ is regular algebraic with coefficient system $F_{\mu}\boxtimes F_{\nu}$. Set 
\[
b_n:=\frac{[\rk:\BQ]}{2}\cdot\frac{ n(n-1)}{2},
\]
and consider the continuous cohomology spaces
\[
\begin{aligned}
	\CH(\Pi)&:=\RH_{\mathrm{ct}}^{2b_n}\left(\RZ_n(\rk_{\infty})\backslash\GL_n(\rk_{\infty})\times\RZ_n(\rk_{\infty})\backslash\GL_n(\rk_{\infty});(F_{\mu}^{\vee}\boxtimes F_{\nu}^{\vee})\otimes\Pi\right),\\
	\CH(\Pi_{\infty})&:=\RH_{\mathrm{ct}}^{2b_n}\left(\RZ_n(\rk_{\infty})\backslash\GL_n(\rk_{\infty})\times\RZ_n(\rk_{\infty})\backslash\GL_n(\rk_{\infty});(F_{\mu}^{\vee}\boxtimes F_{\nu}^{\vee})\otimes\Pi_{\infty}\right).
\end{aligned}
\]
Here and henceforth, a superscript `$^{\vee}$' over a representation indicates its contragredient. There is a canonical isomorphism
\begin{equation}\label{canpi}
	\iota_{\mathrm{can}}:\CH(\Pi_{\infty})\otimes\Pi_{\mathrm{f}}\xrightarrow{\sim}\CH(\Pi).
\end{equation}
As a consequence of the Künneth formula and the Delorme's lemma \cite[Theorem III.3.3]{BW} (see also \cite[Section 1.1]{local}), we have that $\dim\CH(\Pi_{\infty})=1$.

Set $\widetilde{K}_{n,\infty}:=\RZ_n(\rk_{\infty})\cdot K_{n,\infty}$ and define
\[
\CX_n:=\GL_n(\rk)\backslash\GL_n(\A)/\widetilde{K}_{n,\infty}.
\]
Then
\[
\CX_n/K_{\mathrm{f}}=\GL_n(\rk)\backslash\GL_n(\A)/\widetilde{K}_{n,\infty}K_{\mathrm{f}}
\]
for any open compact subgroup $K_{\mathrm{f}}$ of $\GL_n(\A_{\mathrm{f}})$. Any holomorphic finite dimensional representation $F$ of $\GL_n(\rk\otimes_{\BQ}\C)$ defines a sheaf on $\CX_n/K_{\mathrm{f}}$, which is still denoted by $F$. For each $i\in\Z$ and such sheaf $F$, we consider the sheaf cohomology space
\begin{equation}
	\RH^i(\CX_n,F):=\lim_{\substack{\longrightarrow\\ K_{\mathrm{f}}}}\RH^i(\CX_n/K_{\mathrm{f}},F),
\end{equation}
where $K_{\mathrm{f}}$ runs over the directed system of open compact subgroups of $\GL_n(\A_{\mathrm{f}})$. Likewise we have the sheaf cohomology with compact support $\RH_{\mathrm{c}}^i(\CX_n,F)$. Both $\RH^i(\CX_n,F)$ and $\RH_{\mathrm{c}}^i(\CX_n,F)$ are smooth representations of $\GL_n(\A_{\mathrm{f}})$.

Following \cite[Lemma 3.15]{Clo} and \cite[Proposition 1.6]{G}, we have a $\GL_n(\A_{\mathrm{f}})$-equivariant embedding
\begin{equation}
\iota_{\Pi}:\CH(\Pi)\hookrightarrow\RH^{b_n}(\CX_n,F_{\mu}^{\vee})\otimes\RH_{\mathrm{c}}^{b_n}(\CX_n,F_{\nu}^{\vee}).
\end{equation}



